window.HEXHOLD_INDEX = [{"slug":"first-steps","title":"Your first steps","summary":"Starting a new game, making your character, and your first days on Saltmere Isle, including how to skip the lessons.","section":"Getting started","status":"shipped","tags":["getting-started","tutorial","saltmere","character-creator","new-game"],"headings":[{"id":"what-hexseal-is","text":"What HexSeal is"},{"id":"the-title-screen","text":"The title screen"},{"id":"making-your-character","text":"Making your character"},{"id":"washed-ashore","text":"Washed Ashore"},{"id":"skipping","text":"Skipping"},{"id":"after-that","text":"After that"}],"sections":[{"id":"what-hexseal-is","text":"HexSeal is an offline adventure for iPhone in the spirit of Old School RuneScape. The world is a board of hexes. You tap where you want to go, and everything happens on a steady game tick of 0.6 seconds. You train skills, fight, trade, and slowly piece together what's happening in the Wardlands. There are no classes. What you become is down to what you do."},{"id":"the-title-screen","text":"- Continue picks up your last save and shows its name. - New Game starts a fresh character in an empty save slot. - Saves lists up to five saves. See Save slots. - Hard mode is a checkbox you tick before New Game. In Hard mode, falling in battle loses what you carry. See death."},{"id":"making-your-character","text":"On a new game you're asked: \"Who washed ashore?\" Choose hair, beard, moustache, eyes, eyepatch, brows, nose, skin, colours, top, legs and clothes dye. Surprise me picks everything at random. You can change your look any time from Settings › Change look…"},{"id":"washed-ashore","text":"You wake on a grey beach on Saltmere Isle , soaked and cold. Hattie , a beachcomber, gives you an empty tool belt and tells you the island's folk will fill it. 1. First, Wenna Gale the harbourmaster gives you a practice sword and some bread and shows you how to fight. 2. Then visit the island's mentors, in any order : Mentor Where Teaches Gives you Brannoc, the smith The forge Mining and Smithing Pickaxe and mallet Pell, the shipwright Pell's yard Woodcutting Axe Gaffer Moss His fishing spot on the west coast Fishing Rod Old Tamsin The Salted Eel Cooking, then Magic A cooking lesson, then a magic lesson and starter tiles 3. Show Hattie your full belt to finish. You get 25 coins , and each lesson you complete gives 25 XP in its skill. While you're learning, falling in a fight on Saltmere costs you nothing. Mentors remember where you got to, so you can wander off mid-lesson and pick it up later. Brannoc stays at his forge at any hour until you've finished your lessons, and Hattie will tell you which jobs are still left. The first drowned oarsman you beat always drops a Waters tile , so you have one to practise with after Tamsin's magic lesson."},{"id":"skipping","text":"- One lesson at a time. Every mentor offers \"Teach me\" (a small job and the lesson XP) or \"I know the trade\" (the tool straight away, no job, no XP). - The whole thing. On the very first card, tap \"I've been to Saltmere before\" , then hold Hold to skip for two seconds. You get every tool, the sword, the starter tiles and the coins, and wake in the village."},{"id":"after-that","text":"Hattie has a few ideas about where to go next, and the islanders have more. Where you go first is up to you. See saltmere and quests."}]},{"slug":"controls","title":"Controls and the screen","summary":"Tap to walk, long-press to inspect, drag and pinch the camera, and what everything on the screen means.","section":"Getting started","status":"shipped","tags":["controls","hud","dock","compass","camera","getting-started"],"headings":[{"id":"touch-controls","text":"Touch controls"},{"id":"the-top-of-the-screen","text":"The top of the screen"},{"id":"the-dock","text":"The dock"},{"id":"playing-on-a-foldable","text":"Playing on a foldable"}],"sections":[{"id":"touch-controls","text":"Gesture What it does Tap a hex Walk there, one hex per tick Talk to someone Their words open on a story card with their portrait, name and role. Long speeches come a few lines at a time: tap Next . Any choices are on the last page. Signs and stones use a plain sheet. Tap a tree, rock, person or monster Walk next to it and use it: chop, mine, fish, talk, attack, bank. Tapping anywhere on their hex counts, except for a calm Bone Guard: tap the Guard itself to fight it, so you never start one by accident. Tap a pile of items, or your own hex Pick up what's there Long-press a pile with more than one kind of thing An On the ground card: Take everything , take just one kind, or Leave it for now . Tap a signpost A card with where each arm points Tap a building you can enter Go into… steps inside, unless something you're fighting, or chasing you close behind, is on you. Stations by the door still work from outside. Long-press anything A \"What is this?\" card with its name, what it needs, and buttons for what you can do Drag with one finger Turn the camera around you Pinch Zoom in and out The zoom slider above the menu Zoom with one thumb. Drag the bar, or tap + or − . The top is closest. It sits on the right, or the left in the left-handed layout, and Settings can hide it. Pinch in past the closest zoom Admire your hero up close. Pinch out or walk to go back. Tap the place name or compass Open the map Long-press the compass Turn the camera to face north Panels like Magic and the Journal, and the long-press card, close with the × in their corner, or by tapping their tab again. Tapping the world while a menu is open also closes it, and that tap does nothing else, so you won't walk off by accident. Tapping open floor where a villager stands walks you to the nearest free hex beside them. You can still walk onto their hex to pick up something dropped there. Long-press is how you look closely at things. When a quest needs you to look at something, a normal tap on it works too."},{"id":"the-top-of-the-screen","text":"- Place name : the area you're in. It changes as you walk. Tap it for the map. - Tick dot : flashes on every game tick. - Health : a heart with your hitpoints, top right. It cracks when you drop below a third. - Compass : a brass compass whose rose turns with the camera, with a red N. Six gems round the rim stand for the six provinces; each lights up once you have its map. After a fall, it also points to your grave. - Time dial : the sun and moon cross its sky as the day goes by. See day-and-night. - Pinned skills : up to 3 skill rings down the left edge. See Pinned skills. - Boost badge : under the place name while a draught is working. Tap it for the time left. See brewing. - Hands chip : under the place name. Lit when leaving the app would let your character keep working. See muscle-memory. Up to 4 HUD pins sit on the right, above the dock: bottles, food or other things from your bag you want one tap away. Hold an item in your bag and tap Pin to HUD . Messages, XP drops and item notes appear briefly in the middle. You can turn XP and item popups off in Settings; important messages always show."},{"id":"the-dock","text":"The bar at the bottom has five tabs: Tab What's in it Pack Your worn gear, tool belt and 28-slot bag Skills Levels, XP and what each skill unlocks Magic The tile satchel, rosette and spellbook Journal Your quests, rumours and maps. See quests. ··· Map and Settings Tap an open tab again to close it. Left-handed layout in Settings mirrors the dock. A quest you've pinned in the Journal shows its next step in a strip above the dock, bottom right. The game plays in portrait, either way up: turn your phone over and the screen turns with it."},{"id":"playing-on-a-foldable","text":"On an iPhone Duo , the game follows the hinge: - Closed, or fully open and upright: one screen, like any iPhone. - Half open and upright: the menus stay open on one half and the game plays on the other. The game sits above the menus, or below them with game below the menu in Settings. - Open sideways: one wide landscape screen. Menus open in a phone-width column at the right (the left, with the left-handed layout), and you can keep tapping the world beside them. Turn on side by side in Settings to give the game and the menus a half each instead. The zoom slider and your pinned items sit at the far edge of the screen, just above the menu, and your pinned skills run down the far left edge. In a split, tapping the game's half walks as normal and leaves the menu open."}]},{"slug":"day-and-night","title":"Day and night","summary":"A full day lasts 20 minutes. Ferries stop, shops close and different things come out after dark.","section":"Getting started","status":"shipped","tags":["day-night","time","ticks","night","beds"],"headings":[{"id":"the-tick","text":"The tick"},{"id":"a-day","text":"A day"},{"id":"what-changes-at-night","text":"What changes at night"},{"id":"beds","text":"Beds"}],"sections":[{"id":"the-tick","text":"Everything in HexSeal happens on a game tick of 0.6 seconds, like Old School RuneScape. You walk one hex per tick, and gathering, fighting and respawning all happen on ticks."},{"id":"a-day","text":"A full day is 2,000 ticks, or 20 minutes of play. The clock only runs while you're playing. It stops when the app is closed and while the bank is open. New games start in the morning. Time About Day 13 minutes Dusk 1 minute Night 5 minutes Dawn 1 minute The time dial at the top shows a sun by day, a moon at night and orange at dusk and dawn, and its ring fills through the day."},{"id":"what-changes-at-night","text":"- The ferry doesn't sail. - Some shops close , including Ida's stall on Saltmere and the general store and curio shop in Kingsferry. - People keep their own hours. Some only work by day, others only come out at night. Anyone a quest is waiting on stays out after dark so you can still find them. - Some monsters only come out at night , others only by day. See monsters. - Windows glow, lamp posts light the streets, and lighthouses light up. Lamps and lit windows throw warm light on the ground near you. - Some riddle steps only work at dawn, dusk or night."},{"id":"beds","text":"Every inn lets rooms from dusk to dawn . After dark, tapping the inn from outside offers Sleep through the night under the way in: one tap pays, sleeps and leaves you outside in the morning. Inside, just tap the stairs or bed to pay and sleep. By day a tap tells you rooms open at dusk. You can also long-press it, or ask the innkeeper: \"A room for the night?\" Pay, sleep through to morning, and wake at full health. The last bed you slept in , an inn's or your own, is where you wake if you fall in that province, with no fee. Fall anywhere else and you wake at that region's mender as usual. See death. Bed Where Cost A room above the Salted Eel Saltmere 5 coins A room at the Kingsferry tavern Fairmeadow 5 coins A hammock at the Leaky Eel Mistmarsh 5 coins A cot at the Windy Ewe Highmoor 5 coins A cot at the Lost Lantern Greenwood 5 coins A bunk at the Quench Cinderreach 5 coins A bunk at the Kettle Frostpeak 8 coins Your own bed The Driftwood Cottage, once its roof is patched Free A cot in the Lantern hall The Crown Valley, once it's built (see Late works) Free You can't sleep in broad daylight, or while you're in a fight: whatever you're fighting, or something chasing you that's close behind. Get clear and it gives up. The innkeepers are Tamsin (the Salted Eel), Wilf Cask (Kingsferry), Nessa Sedge (the Leaky Eel), Dougal Hogg (the Windy Ewe), Kip (the Lost Lantern), Gorran Ashby (the Quench) and Sorrel (the Kettle)."}]},{"slug":"settings-and-saving","title":"Settings and saving","summary":"Sound, haptics, camera and layout settings, and how the game saves itself.","section":"Getting started","status":"shipped","tags":["settings","saving","sound","haptics","left-handed"],"headings":[{"id":"settings","text":"Settings"},{"id":"saving","text":"Saving"},{"id":"save-slots","text":"Save slots"},{"id":"moving-or-backing-up-a-save","text":"Moving or backing up a save"}],"sections":[{"id":"settings","text":"Open ··· › Settings from the dock. Setting Starts at Music On, 80% Sound effects On, 80% Haptics On Camera distance Between close and far Text size, Icon size 100%. Step from 80% to 150%. Reset sizes to default puts both back. XP and item popups On Left-handed layout Off Zoom slider above the menu On. The one-handed zoom bar; see controls. Calmer effects (no gust lines, still plants) Off. Turn it on to hide the wind lines in the sky and keep plants still; windmills keep turning. Battery saver Off. Holds the game at a steadier, slower frame rate to save battery. Show frame rate Off. Shows the frame rate in a corner of the screen. Muscle Memory On. See muscle-memory. Journal: show all quest starts Off. See quests. The game already slows its frame rate when nothing on screen is moving, when your phone is hot, or in Low Power Mode; the game itself never runs slower. To change a setting, tap its switch ; tapping the rest of the row does nothing, so you won't flip one by accident. The sliders move with a sideways drag or a tap; an up or down swipe that starts on one scrolls the list instead. On a foldable iPhone Duo , two more settings appear: game below the menu (when it's half open and upright, the game sits under the menu half instead of above it) and side by side (when it's open sideways, the game and the menu take a half each instead of one wide screen). Also here: Change look… to restyle your character, and Reset save… , which wipes the save you're playing after asking first."},{"id":"saving","text":"HexSeal saves on its own; there's no save button. - It saves every few seconds whenever something has changed. - It saves when you leave the app, travel, sleep, or touch a waystone. - Saves are written safely, so a crash can't corrupt your game. Saves are kept on your phone. See the privacy policy for what that means for your data."},{"id":"save-slots","text":"You can keep up to five saves . Tap Saves on the title screen to see them all, each with its name, character level, region, play time and when you last played. - Play loads that save. - Rename gives it a name of your choice. - Delete removes it, after asking first. New Game always uses an empty slot. It only asks to replace a save when all five are full. If you played before save slots arrived, your save moved into slot 1 on its own, and a backup was kept."},{"id":"moving-or-backing-up-a-save","text":"You can copy any save to a file and open it on another iPhone or iPad. To export: open Saves , then tap Export on a slot. The share sheet opens, so you can AirDrop the file, send it in Messages or Mail, or save it to Files. The file is called something like Ash (slot 1).hexhold , and a copy also stays in Files › On My iPhone › HexSeal › Exports . To import: put the .hexhold file in the HexSeal folder in the Files app (On My iPhone › HexSeal), then open the game. The title screen asks whether to load it into an empty slot or replace one. Replacing asks first, keeps a copy of the old save, and never offers the save you're playing. If you tap Not now , ×, or Back, the file moves to Files › On My iPhone › HexSeal › Declined . Nothing is deleted and the game won't ask about it again. To import it later, put it back in the HexSeal folder. If the question closes without an answer, it comes back after a few minutes. - A damaged file is refused, so it can't break your game. - Exported saves are sealed. A file that's been edited outside HexSeal is refused with \"This save was changed outside HexSeal.\" - Files exported before build 200 need exporting again from the latest version. - A save from a newer build asks you to update HexSeal first. - Nothing is sent to a server. The file only goes where you send it. Don't delete anything in the saves folder inside the HexSeal folder. That's where the game keeps your slots. - Tapping a .hexhold file to open it straight in HexSeal. - Moving saves to and from Android."}]},{"slug":"item-uses","title":"What is this for?","summary":"What the things in your bag are used for: bones, hides, ores, logs, herbs, clay, tiles and monster drops, by skill.","section":"Getting started","status":"shipped","tags":["items","uses","materials","bones","drops","crafting","smithing","cooking","brewing","what-is-this-for"],"headings":[{"id":"quick-answers","text":"Quick answers"},{"id":"bones","text":"Bones"},{"id":"ores-bars-and-smithing","text":"Ores, bars and smithing"},{"id":"wood","text":"Wood"},{"id":"hides-fibres-and-leather","text":"Hides, fibres and leather"},{"id":"food-and-cooking","text":"Food and cooking"},{"id":"clay-and-pottery","text":"Clay and pottery"},{"id":"magic","text":"Magic"},{"id":"herbs-and-creature-parts","text":"Herbs and creature parts"},{"id":"trophies-and-keepsakes","text":"Trophies and keepsakes"}],"sections":[{"id":"quick-answers","text":"Hold any item in your bag to open its card, which says what it is and what you can do with it right now. This page fills in the rest: where an item gets used across all the skills. Anything you have no use for can be sold at a general store or the market. Brewing ingredients are listed by name only. Which herb goes with which ingredient is for you to discover; see brewing."},{"id":"bones","text":"Item Used for Bones Burn them at a forge for bone ash (Smithing). Cook fish broth from them with a small raw fish (Cooking). Lay the Bone Guard's to rest (bones). Big bones The same as bones, but one does the work of two in broth. Also a Brewing ingredient. Guard bones Bone ash at a forge, and a Brewing ingredient. Some people ask for them too. Dragon bones Bone ash at a forge. Bone ash Smelt it with hexslate into blank wardglass (etching). Make glaze slip for pottery (Crafting 30). A Brewing ingredient, and the base of Warden's ink . The forges' ore bin holds bones and bone ash too, and the furnace burns bones from it. See bones for everything bones do."},{"id":"ores-bars-and-smithing","text":"Item Used for Copper, tin, iron, gold and other ores Smelt into bars at a furnace (Smithing). Copper ore is also a Brewing ingredient. Coal Fuel for smelting the harder bars. Bars (bronze up to starsteel) Smith weapons, armour, tools, arrowheads, studs, nails and rivets at an anvil (smithing). Some go into Crafting too: a steel bar for the reinforced satchel, bronze for some kit. Iron studs Studded leather armour (crafting). Iron nails Signboards and other wooden fittings. Sealsteel rivets Drake-scale armour (Drake gear). Arrowheads Tip headless arrows (archery). Emberite, rimesilver, starstone, duskite Smelt into the bars above steel: embersteel, frostsilver, starsteel and deepsteel (smithing). Where to find them: Where to mine. Hexslate Smelt into blank wardglass for etching. Also a Brewing ingredient."},{"id":"wood","text":"Item Used for Logs of every kind Staffs, wands, fishing rods, shields and bows at a workbench (Crafting). Oak logs also make planks (Crafting 5), and logs go into chests and other kit. Planks Signboards, chests and building work. Bark twine Bundles, sacks and lots of kit; one of the most-used things in Crafting. Also a Brewing ingredient. Arrow shafts, feathers Headless arrows (archery)."},{"id":"hides-fibres-and-leather","text":"Item Used for Cowhide Tan it into leather at a workbench (Crafting 1). Leather Archer's armour, quivers, satchels and sacks (crafting). Tallow Hardened leather armour and the reinforced satchel. Flax Flax bowstrings and canvas quivers. Sinew Dry it into a bowstring (Crafting 30), or make a hide quiver. Drakehide, drake scales, drake sinew Drake gear and the Warden's satchel (Drake gear). Frostsilk Frostsilk bowstrings. Fleece Not used in making anything yet. Sell it, or keep some for anyone who asks."},{"id":"food-and-cooking","text":"Item Used for Raw fish Cook on a fire or range (cooking). Small fish also make broth with bones. Some are Brewing ingredients. Raw meat Cook on any fire. Wheat Grind into flour at a mill. Flour, jug of water, eggs, jug of milk, apples Baking: bread, pies and cakes (Baking). Flour is also a Brewing ingredient. Bread dough, pie shell Bake them in an oven. Jug Fill it at a well, a spring or fresh water. A full jug gives four pours."},{"id":"clay-and-pottery","text":"Item Used for Brin, marsh, fire and snow clay Wet it at a potter's wheel into soft clay (Pottery). Soft clay Shape it at a wheel into an unfired piece. Unfired pieces Fire them in a kiln. Glaze slip Glazes a pot before firing. Pie dish, brew crock Carry a pie dish to burn fewer pies; carry a brew crock to get 2 more sips from a brew."},{"id":"magic","text":"Item Used for Element tiles Cast spells (magic). A few also go into special Crafting pieces and Brewing. Blank wardglass Etch it into tiles at a Warden statue (etching). It also makes wands. Element tomes Hold in the shield hand for a Magic boost, or rebind into a stronger edition (Tome editions). Warden's ink Rebinding tomes."},{"id":"herbs-and-creature-parts","text":"Herbs (bog myrtle, heather, meadowsweet, saltwort, elderflower, firethorn, rimecap, greyleaf and more) and creature parts (eye of newt, ember beetle, golem pebble, gull feather, fish scales, moonmoth wing, troll tooth, wolfman claw) are Brewing ingredients. See brewing to start working out what they make."},{"id":"trophies-and-keepsakes","text":"Some big monsters drop a trophy, like a huge fang or a scale that never cools. These aren't used in making anything at the moment; they're worth a good price, or keep them as proof. A worn riddle map starts a treasure hunt; see riddle-maps."}]},{"slug":"skills-overview","title":"Skills and levels","summary":"All 15 skills, how XP and levels work, your character level, and how to read the Skills tab.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["skills","xp","levels","skills-tab","character-level"],"headings":[{"id":"the-skills","text":"The skills"},{"id":"how-xp-works","text":"How XP works"},{"id":"total-level-and-character-level","text":"Total level and character level"},{"id":"the-skills-tab","text":"The Skills tab"},{"id":"pinned-skills","text":"Pinned skills"},{"id":"gathering-versus-making","text":"Gathering versus making"}],"sections":[{"id":"the-skills","text":"HexSeal has 15 skills . Each goes from level 1 to 99. Group Skills Combat Attack, Strength, Defence, Hitpoints, Magic, Archery Gathering Woodcutting, Fishing, Mining Making Cooking, Crafting, Smithing, Brewing Other Etching (makes magic tiles), Trade (your market stall) Every skill starts at level 1, except Hitpoints, which starts at 10. Skills feed each other: you fish, cook what you catch, eat it in a fight, and smith better tools from what you mine."},{"id":"how-xp-works","text":"Every action that trains a skill gives XP. Levels follow the same curve as Old School RuneScape, so each level takes a little more than the one before, and the top levels take a lot. Level XP needed Level XP needed 2 83 40 37,224 5 388 50 101,333 10 1,154 60 273,742 15 2,411 70 737,627 20 4,470 80 1,986,068 25 7,842 90 5,346,332 30 13,363 92 6,517,253 35 22,406 99 13,034,431 Level 92 is about halfway to 99 in XP."},{"id":"total-level-and-character-level","text":"- Total level is all 14 levels added up. A new character has 23; the most is 1,386. - Character level (1 to 99) is the average of your XP across all 15 skills, read off the same curve. The free XP Hitpoints starts with doesn't count, so a new character is level 1, and only 99 in every skill makes 99."},{"id":"the-skills-tab","text":"Open Skills from the dock. - The top strip shows your character level in a gold hex, a progress bar and your total level. - Each skill is a ring , four to a row. The ring fills as you head toward the next level, and the little pill shows your level. - Tap a ring for that skill's page: - your level, XP, and XP left to the next level - Next unlock : what your next level brings - Best tool : what's on your tool belt for that skill - Per action now : the best XP per action you can earn right now - The ladder : everything the skill unlocks, by level. Locked rows are dimmed and the next one is gold. Rows marked \"(coming later)\" aren't in the game yet. Pinned skills Pin up to 3 skills to the left edge of the screen: use the pin on a skill's page, or press and hold its ring. Each pinned skill shows a small ring that fills as you train, with a \"+XP\" as you earn it and a flash when you level. Tap one to open its page, or hold it to unpin. When you level up, a banner slides down for three seconds with what you've unlocked, such as \"You can now chop oak trees\". It never blocks your taps, and several level-ups queue up. Your character level can rise from any XP, even when no single skill levels. When it does, it gets its own banner and jingle, right after any skill banner. The ladders already list a few tiers above what the world gives today (Frostpine trees, Emberite ore, Heartfin and up). Their items exist, but nowhere to gather them yet. See each skill's page for what you can actually gather today."},{"id":"gathering-versus-making","text":"- Gathering (Woodcutting, Fishing, Mining): tap a tree, fishing spot or rock. You keep working until your bag is full, the resource runs out, or you tap somewhere else. You need the right tool on your belt. - Making (Cooking, Crafting, Smithing): tap a station (a fire, a workbench or a forge) and pick what to make. Around 1 in 150 gathering or smithing and cooking actions also turns up a magic tile. See Getting tiles."}]},{"slug":"tools","title":"Tools and the tool belt","summary":"Axes, pickaxes, rods, hammers and chisels hang on your belt, out of your bag. Better tools work faster.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["tools","tool-belt","axe","pickaxe","rod","hammer","chisel"],"headings":[{"id":"the-tool-belt","text":"The tool belt"},{"id":"faster-with-better-tools","text":"Faster with better tools"},{"id":"hammers","text":"Hammers"},{"id":"chisels","text":"Chisels"}],"sections":[{"id":"the-tool-belt","text":"Hattie gives you a tool belt early on Saltmere, along with a wooden axe, a wooden pickaxe and a driftwood rod. Until you have it, gathering tells you: \"You'll need tools for that. Maybe someone on the strand can help.\" - The belt has five loops: Woodcutting (axe), Mining (pickaxe), Fishing (rod), Smithing (hammer) and Etching (chisel). - Tools on the belt don't take bag slots. - Use a better tool from your bag and it goes on the belt; your old one drops back into your bag so you can sell it. - Each tool needs a level in its own skill. The first time you use a forge without a hammer, the smith lends you a wooden mallet."},{"id":"faster-with-better-tools","text":"A better axe, pickaxe or rod raises your chance of success on every try. The bonus multiplies your chance: +100% means twice as likely, up to a sure thing. Tier Axe Pickaxe Rod Bonus Starter Wooden (1) Wooden (1) Driftwood (1) – 2 Bronze (1) Bronze (1) Oak (5) +25% 3 Iron (5) Iron (5) Willow (10) +50% 4 Steel (10) Steel (10) Ash (20) +75% 5 Embersteel (20) Embersteel (20) – +100% 6 Frostsilver (30) Frostsilver (30) – +125% 7 Starsteel (40) Starsteel (40) – +150% 8 Deepsteel (50) Deepsteel (50) – +175% 9 Sealsteel (60) Sealsteel (60) – +200% 10 Hexsteel (70) Hexsteel (70) – +225% The number in brackets is the level needed in that tool's skill. A crafted sturdy axe also gives +25%. Wyrmfang tools (Woodcutting or Mining 70 to use, smithed at Smithing 78 from 1 wyrmfang and 2 hexsteel bars) give +250% , and each has a trick: - The wyrmfang axe sometimes chars the cut, and a lump of coal drops out with the log (about 1 in 20). Sells for 5,400. - The wyrmfang pickaxe sometimes smelts the ore as it comes out, so you get the bar instead, with half the smelting XP (about 1 in 20). Metal axes and pickaxes are smithed; see smithing. Rods past ash, and heartsteel tools, exist but can't be made yet from what the world gives you."},{"id":"hammers","text":"A better hammer gives a chance that a smithing job uses no bars . Hammer Smithing Bar-save chance Wooden mallet 1 – Bronze 1 10% Iron 10 20% Steel 20 30%"},{"id":"chisels","text":"Chisels are for Etching: they speed it up and can make bonus tiles. Chisel Etching Smithing to make Ticks per blank Bonus tile chance Bronze 1 4 2 2% Iron 5 13 2 4% Steel 10 23 1 5%"}]},{"slug":"woodcutting","title":"Woodcutting","summary":"Chop trees for logs. Logs feed Crafting (staffs, rods, sacks, chests) and twine.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["woodcutting","gathering","logs","trees","axe"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-chop","text":"How to chop"},{"id":"trees","text":"Trees"},{"id":"chance-per-swing-wooden-axe","text":"Chance per swing (wooden axe)"},{"id":"extras","text":"Extras"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-to-chop","text":"Tap a tree. You walk next to it and swing every 4 ticks (2.4 seconds). Each swing has a chance to give logs, and that chance rises with your level and your axe. After giving logs, a tree may fall; it grows back after a short while. You keep chopping until your bag is full, the tree falls, or you tap elsewhere. A full bag spills into a log sling on your belt if you've made one (see crafting)."},{"id":"trees","text":"Tree Level XP per log Falls after a log Grows back Where Tree 1 25 35% 15 s Saltmere, Fairmeadow, Highmoor Oak 10 37.5 12.5% 8 s Every region Willow 20 67.5 12.5% 8 s Saltmere, Fairmeadow, Mistmarsh (most) Ash 30 100 11% 18 s Fairmeadow, Greenwood Emberleaf 40 125 12% 30 s Wyrmholm Elder 60 175 6% 36 s Greenwood only Duskwood 70 210 6% 48 s The Crown Valley, Wyrmholm Hexwood 80 250 5% 60 s The Crown Valley Chance per swing (wooden axe) Tree At its level At 60 At 99 Tree 25% 57% 79% Oak 15% 29% 40% Willow 18% 29% 40% Ash 12% 18% 25% Emberleaf 28% 37% 55% Elder 11% 11% 16% Duskwood 10% – 14% Hexwood 10% – 12% A better axe multiplies these. A bronze axe makes them 25% higher, an iron axe 50% and a steel axe 75%."},{"id":"extras","text":"- About 1 log in 150 comes with a Roots tile for magic. - Some magic can help here, once you've found it. Frostpine and Heartwood trees are on the Skills ladder but don't grow anywhere yet."}]},{"slug":"fishing","title":"Fishing","summary":"Catch fish with a rod. Cook them to eat in fights, or sell them.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["fishing","gathering","fish","rod"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-fish","text":"How to fish"},{"id":"waters","text":"Waters"},{"id":"chance-per-cast-driftwood-rod","text":"Chance per cast (driftwood rod)"},{"id":"extras","text":"Extras"},{"id":"scaling-boards","text":"Scaling boards"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-to-fish","text":"Tap a fishing spot: a ripple on the sea, a pool or a river, which you fish from the bank or shore beside it. You cast every 5 ticks (3 seconds). Fishing spots never run out. Each catch is one of the fish that water holds, at your level and at that hour. The best fish you can catch is twice as likely as the one below it, so a higher level pays off on the same spot. If nothing will bite, the spot says why: your level is too low for that water, or it's the wrong time of day."},{"id":"waters","text":"Fish live by the kind of water, and some only bite by day or only at night. Water Lowest level Sea shallows 1 River mouths 1 Deep sea 10 Rivers and becks 15 Ponds and lakes 15 Marsh pools 15 Ice holes 15 Every region has a local fishing expert who knows its waters: when the fish bite and where the good ones are. Ask them. Region Expert Saltmere Gaffer Moss Fairmeadow Fen Barrow Mistmarsh Marta Eelwife Highmoor Tib Beck Greenwood Old Wade, at the weir Cinderreach Cass Frostpeak Ulla Auger, by the tarn Chance per cast (driftwood rod) Water At its level At 60 At 99 Sea shallows 19% 68% 100% Deep sea 19% 51% 75% River mouths 13% 51% 75% Rivers, ponds and lakes 17% 42% 63% Marsh pools 21% 47% 69% Ice holes 12% 31% 47% The moat (hexcarp) 23% – 32% A better rod multiplies these: oak +25%, willow +50%, ash +75%. See tools."},{"id":"extras","text":"- About 1 catch in 150 brings up a Waters tile for magic. - Some magic can help here, once you've found it. - A full bag spills into a fish creel on your belt, if you've made one (crafting)."},{"id":"scaling-boards","text":"Each fishing region has a scaling board by the water: Moss's by the Saltmere docks, and others in Kingsferry, Mistmarsh, Windle, Thornwick, Emberfall and Frostpeak. Pick a raw fish and you keep the fish , get 1 fish scale for each one, and a little Cooking XP. Each fish can only be scaled once. Scales stack, and brewers have a use for them (see brewing). Deepgill and heartfin are on the Skills ladder but can't be caught yet."}]},{"slug":"mining","title":"Mining","summary":"Mine ore for Smithing, and hexslate for Etching. Which rocks there are, and where to find them.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["mining","gathering","ore","pickaxe","hexslate","emberite","rimesilver","where-to-mine"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-mine","text":"How to mine"},{"id":"rocks","text":"Rocks"},{"id":"where-to-mine","text":"Where to mine"},{"id":"chance-per-swing-wooden-pickaxe","text":"Chance per swing (wooden pickaxe)"},{"id":"extras","text":"Extras"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-to-mine","text":"Tap a rock. You swing every 4 ticks (2.4 seconds). When a rock runs out it goes dull and comes back after a short wait."},{"id":"rocks","text":"Rock Level XP Runs out after an ore Back in Where Copper 1 17.5 50% 5 s Saltmere, Fairmeadow Tin 1 17.5 50% 5 s Saltmere, Fairmeadow Iron 10 35 Every time 5 s Saltmere, Fairmeadow, Highmoor, Cinderreach, Frostpeak Coal 20 50 50% 24 s Fairmeadow, Highmoor Coal seam 20 50 Every time 18 s Cinderreach, Frostpeak Emberite 30 40 75% 18 s Cinderreach Rimesilver 40 55 Every time 27 s Frostpeak Gold 45 65 Every time 36 s Cinderreach, Frostpeak Starstone 50 80 Every time 48 s The Delvings Duskite 60 100 Every time 60 s The Delvings Sealstone 70 125 Every time 72 s The Crown Valley Greyed slate 75 135 Every time 90 s The Crown Valley Wyrmglass 75 120 Every time 66 s Wyrmholm Hexstone 80 150 Every time 108 s The Crown Valley Hexslate 1 5 Never – Every region Hexslate is the stone etching starts from. It never runs out. Greyed slate etches into Hollow tiles, and sealstone and hexstone smelt into the two highest bars so far. See smithing."},{"id":"where-to-mine","text":"Ore Where to look Copper, tin Saltmere's quarry, and Fairmeadow Iron Saltmere, Fairmeadow, Mistmarsh, Highmoor, Greenwood's Highwood ridge, the Scorch in Cinderreach, and all over Frostpeak Coal Fairmeadow, the scree on Highmoor's Fells, Greenwood's Charwood, the Scorch, and Frostpeak Emberite Cinderreach: along the Firebreak crack, in the old workings, and on the Scorch's east edge Rimesilver Frostpeak: by the ice spires on the Fells, round the frozen tarn, and on the Shoulder's ledges Gold The Kiln's slopes and the Scorch in Cinderreach, and Frostpeak's heights Fireclay, snowclay Cinderreach's foothills, and up by Frostpeak's tarn (see Pottery) Hexslate Every region Stones that are only scenery, like carved walls, standing stones, rails and rubble, can't be mined. Chance per swing (wooden pickaxe) Rock At its level At 60 At 99 Copper, tin 40% 98% 100% Iron 47% 98% 100% Coal, coal seam 13% 26% 40% Emberite 21% 32% 47% Rimesilver 19% 25% 38% Gold 15% 19% 29% Starstone 13% 16% 24% Duskite 13% 13% 20% Sealstone 13% – 18% Greyed slate 13% – 16% Wyrmglass 14% – 18% Hexstone 12% – 14% Hexslate 63% 100% 100% A better pickaxe multiplies these: bronze +25%, iron +50%, steel +75%. See tools."},{"id":"extras","text":"- About 1 ore in 150 comes with a Stone tile for magic. - Some magic can help here, once you've found it. - A full bag spills into an ore sack on your belt, if you've made one (crafting). Emberite and rimesilver smelt into the bars between steel and the top tiers; see smithing."}]},{"slug":"smithing","title":"Smithing","summary":"Smelt ore into bars at a forge, then hammer bars into weapons, armour and tools.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["smithing","making","forge","bars","armour","weapons","village-forge","fine","ore-bin"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-smith","text":"How to smith"},{"id":"smelting-bars","text":"Smelting bars"},{"id":"smithing-items","text":"Smithing items"},{"id":"arrowheads","text":"Arrowheads"},{"id":"the-village-forges","text":"The village forges"},{"id":"bone-ash","text":"Bone ash"},{"id":"extras","text":"Extras"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-to-smith","text":"Tap a forge . There's one in Saltmere, Fairmeadow and Highmoor, Emberfall's great furnace in Cinderreach, and Kettlepass in Frostpeak (Mistmarsh and Greenwood have none). You need a hammer on your tool belt; if you don't have one yet, the smith lends you a wooden mallet the first time. Pick what to make; the list has a tab for each metal. Most jobs take 4 ticks (2.4 seconds) and never fail."},{"id":"smelting-bars","text":"Bar Level Needs XP Bronze 1 1 copper ore, 1 tin ore 6.2 Iron 10 1 iron ore 12.5 Steel 20 1 iron ore, 1 coal 18.8 Embersteel 30 1 emberite, 1 coal 25 Frostsilver 40 1 rimesilver, 2 coal 31.2 Gold 45 1 gold ore 22.5 Starsteel 50 1 starstone, 2 coal 37.5 Deepsteel 60 1 duskite, 3 coal 43.8 Sealsteel 70 1 sealstone, 3 coal 50 Hexsteel 80 1 hexstone, 4 coal 56.2 Smelting always works."},{"id":"smithing-items","text":"Each metal unlocks items in the same pattern. The levels below are for bronze ; add 9 for iron and 19 for steel (iron starts at 10, steel at 20). Item Bronze level Bars Dagger 1 1 Axe 2 1 Mace 3 1 Helm 4 1 Chisel 4 1 Sword 5 1 Hammer 5 1 Pickaxe 6 2 Viking helm 6 2 Battleaxe 7 2 Shield 8 2 Warhammer 8 3 Greatsword 9 3 Platelegs 9 3 Platebody 10 5 XP is per bar used: 12.5 for bronze, 25 for iron and 37.5 for steel. A steel platebody gives 187.5 XP. Embersteel, frostsilver, starsteel and deepsteel follow the same pattern from Smithing 30, 40, 50 and 60 (add 29 , 39 , 49 or 59 to the bronze level), for 50 , 62.5 , 75 and 87.5 XP a bar. Each makes its own chisel for etching, and starsteel and deepsteel make arrowheads too. Where to find their ores: Where to mine. Sealsteel and hexsteel follow the same pattern from Smithing 70 and 80 (add 69 or 79 to the bronze level), for 100 and 112.5 XP a bar. Each also makes a chisel for etching. From Smithing 72, one sealsteel bar makes 10 sealsteel rivets (90 XP), used for drake-scale armour. Wyrmfangs make a spear (Smithing 75, with sealsteel), and an axe and a pickaxe (78, with hexsteel). See Drake gear and tools."},{"id":"arrowheads","text":"Arrowheads for archery take 1 bar each batch. Fletch them onto arrows at a workbench (see Crafting). Arrowheads Smithing Heads per bar XP Bronze 1 15 12.5 Iron 10 15 25 Steel 20 30 37.5 Starsteel 50 60 75 Deepsteel 60 80 87.5 Sealsteel 70 100 100 Hexsteel 80 100 112.5"},{"id":"the-village-forges","text":"Every forge is listed on Where to find the stations. Three forges are proper smithies, indoors, each with its own smith: Brannoc's on Saltmere, Aggie Brand's in Kingsferry and Morwen Hollis's in Windle. They work better than an anvil in the open: What it does Ore bin Holds ore, coal, bones and bone ash by the furnace, and the furnace smelts straight from it. It's one bin shared by all three forges , so ore you leave at one is waiting at the others. Fill it by hand, or have a runner or rook bring your ore. Smith's strongbox Opens your bank, for ore, coal, bars and smithing tools only. Carter's crate Deposit only, like a post box: drop your smithing in it and it goes to the bank without the walk. Bellows Every smelting and smithing job takes one tick less , for the same XP. Quench trough Now and then a weapon or armour piece comes out Fine : +1 to its main stat, and it sells for a quarter more (and gives a quarter more Trade XP). The chance is 3% at Smithing 1, rising to about 6% at 99. Tools never come out Fine. Brannoc has smithing tips if you ask."},{"id":"bone-ash","text":"At a forge you can also burn bones into bone ash (Smithing 1): bones make 1 ash, big bones 3, Guard bones 2, dragon bones 6. Ash is used in etching to make extra wardglass. See bones."},{"id":"extras","text":"- Iron nails (Smithing 10): 1 iron bar makes 10 nails, 12.5 XP. They go into signboards and post houses. See The King's Post. - A better hammer can save your bars. See Hammers. - About 1 job in 150 turns up a Flame tile."}]},{"slug":"cooking","title":"Cooking","summary":"Cook fish on a fire, and bake bread, pies and cakes in an oven, so you can eat them to heal. Higher levels burn less.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["cooking","making","fish","food","burn","fire","baking","bread","pie","cake","wheat","flour","mill","oven","hearthhouse","range","pantry"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-cook","text":"How to cook"},{"id":"fish","text":"Fish"},{"id":"meat","text":"Meat"},{"id":"fish-broth","text":"Fish broth"},{"id":"baking","text":"Baking"},{"id":"hearthhouses","text":"Hearthhouses"},{"id":"extras","text":"Extras"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-to-cook","text":"Tap a fire with raw fish in your bag. Saltmere has the hearth inside the Salted Eel and the Quay campfire, and the other regions have campfires. The Hearthspark spell (magic, level 7) lights a short-lived campfire wherever you are. For the best kitchen, use a town Hearthhouse. Each fish takes 4 ticks (2.4 seconds). Cooked fish show in your bag as \"Cooked trout\" and so on. Some burn; burnt fish are no good to eat. Your chance of burning falls as your level rises, until you stop burning that fish altogether."},{"id":"fish","text":"Fish Level XP Burn chance at that level 10 levels later Never burns at Shrimp 1 30 50% 34% 34 Sardine 5 42.5 47% 31% 38 Herring 10 55 43% 27% 43 Perch 15 67.5 38% 22% 48 Trout 20 70 38% 24% 50 Salmon 25 90 40% 27% 58 Marsh eel 35 117.5 15% 0% 68 Pike 30 105 22% 4% 63 Frostfin 40 130 9% 0% 73 Cinder eel 50 155 0% 0% 83 Hexcarp 70 205 0% 0% 99 Sealray 80 230 0% 0% 99 Kilnfin 82 215 0% 0% 97"},{"id":"meat","text":"Raw meat comes from Fairmeadow's sheep and cows, fell rams on Highmoor, wolfmen and Kingsway bandits, among others. Cook it on any fire from Cooking 1 for 30 XP. It burns like shrimp (50% at level 1, never from 34), and cooked meat heals 4."},{"id":"fish-broth","text":"From Cooking 10 (or the fish's own level, if higher), cook 1 raw small fish (shrimp, sardine, herring, perch or trout) with 2 bones or 1 big bones into fish broth. Better fish are too good for the pot. It gives the fish's cooking XP plus 5, with the same burn chance. See bones."},{"id":"baking","text":"The Cooking skill guide (Skills tab) lists every bake, with All / Fish / Baking tabs. Tap a bake there to open its recipe card, with how many you've baked. Baking is part of Cooking, and it gives Cooking XP. New to it? Mabry Loaf at the Kingsferry Hearthhouse walks you through your first loaf. 1. Pick wheat in the Fairmeadow fields. It grows back about 30 seconds after you pick it. 2. Grind it at a mill. The mill grinds your whole stack of wheat into flour at once. At the Old Mill , carrying wheat gives you a choice of Grind wheat or Trade . 3. Fill a jug with water. Any well, spring or water butt works, and so does fresh water: rivers, becks, ponds and tarns, but not the sea. There are wells beside the bread ovens and mills. A jug of water holds 4 pours : each dough or pie shell takes one, and you keep the jug. 4. Mix and bake at a bread oven. You'll find ovens in the farmsteads and at tavern hearths. Once the jug is empty you keep it to fill again. Farmstead and farmhouse ovens only bake, while tavern hearths work as fires too. An oven lists every bake; the ones you can't make yet are greyed out with their level and ingredients. Once you've added the hearth and range to your cottage, it's an oven too, and it burns a little less than the others. The Old Mill shop sells flour, empty jugs, eggs, jugs of milk, apples and raw meat. Bake Level What goes in XP Burn chance at that level 10 levels later Never burns at Heals Bread 1 Bread dough (1 flour, 1 pour of water) 40 50% 34% 35 6 Fish pie 20 Pie shell (2 flour, 1 pour of water), 1 raw trout 110 33% 16% 55 8 a bite, 2 bites Meat pie 30 Pie shell, 1 raw meat 140 22% 4% 64 10 a bite, 2 bites Apple pie 40 Pie shell, 2 apples 170 9% 0% 72 12 a bite, 2 bites Cake 50 2 flour, 2 eggs, 1 jug of milk 220 0% 0% 82 12 a slice, 3 slices There are more bakes than these to find. Mabry's bake book has a page for each of them. Mixing bread dough gives 5 XP, and rolling a pie shell (Cooking 20) gives 10. Carry a clay pie dish (see Pottery) and your pies, tarts and pasties burn less. Pies and cakes are eaten a bite at a time : each bite heals, and what's left goes back in your bag."},{"id":"hearthhouses","text":"Every town has a Hearthhouse , a two-hex cook house with a kitchen room inside: Saltmere, Kingsferry, Reedwick, Windle, Thornwick, Emberfall and Kettlepass. Each has its own resident cook to chat with. Ask any of them about baking and they'll send you to Mabry Loaf in Kingsferry, who teaches new bakers in the First Loaf errand. - Cooking range : burns food as if your Cooking were 3 levels higher , and each cook takes one tick less than over a fire. - Bread oven : each bake takes one tick less than in an outdoor oven. - Work table : mix dough and roll pie shells here. It has a water butt too, so you can fill jugs without leaving the room. - Pantry chest : holds raw fish and baking things. The range and oven take what they need from the pantry first, then from your bag. There's one pantry shared by every Hearthhouse , so you can fill it in one town and cook in another. Bank your raw catch in the pantry on the way past, then cook it all at whichever Hearthhouse is nearest."},{"id":"extras","text":"About 1 fish in 150 gives a Flame tile for magic. Deepgill and heartfin have cooking levels set (up to Heartfin 90) but can't be caught yet. The better the fish, the more it heals and sells for."}]},{"slug":"crafting","title":"Crafting","summary":"Turn logs into staffs, rods, shields, bows and arrows, make archer's armour and pottery, plus twine, planks, gathering sacks, field chests and bundles.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["crafting","making","workbench","staff","bows","arrows","fletching","flax","bowstring","quiver","leather","sacks","chests","hauling","pottery","clay","kiln","potter's wheel"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-craft","text":"How to craft"},{"id":"where-to-find-the-stations","text":"Where to find the stations"},{"id":"basics","text":"Basics"},{"id":"wooden-gear","text":"Wooden gear"},{"id":"bows-and-arrows","text":"Bows and arrows"},{"id":"bowstrings","text":"Bowstrings"},{"id":"bows","text":"Bows"},{"id":"arrows","text":"Arrows"},{"id":"quivers","text":"Quivers"},{"id":"archer-s-armour","text":"Archer's armour"},{"id":"pottery","text":"Pottery"},{"id":"tile-satchels","text":"Tile satchels"},{"id":"drake-gear","text":"Drake gear"},{"id":"sacks-chests-and-bundles","text":"Sacks, chests and bundles"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-to-craft","text":"Tap a workbench , a long carpenter's bench that takes up two hexes. There's one in every region's main town; see Where to find the stations. Pick what to make. Most jobs take 4 ticks (2.4 seconds)."},{"id":"where-to-find-the-stations","text":"Region Workbench Forge Potter's wheel and kiln Also Saltmere The village, outside the Workshop near the Salted Eel Brannoc's forge, in the village by the Hearthhouse – A spinning wheel in the Saltmere mill Fairmeadow Kingsferry , just west of the forge, by the well and the Kingsway stables Aggie Brand's forge, Kingsferry Kingsferry's east side, by the drying rack near the Brin clay bank and the glider field Spinning wheels in the farmsteads; a cauldron at the Wayside apothecary Mistmarsh Reedwick , east across the road from the waystone, by the marsh clay and the eel pool None The wheel is by Reedwick's waystone; no kiln Mother Gloam's cauldron, in her hut Highmoor Windle , just below the kiln and the forge, by the beck Morwen Hollis's forge, Windle, by the Windy Ewe The Windle kiln, with the wheel close by, near the flax Spinning wheels in the windmills Greenwood Thornwick , just east of the Hearthhouse None – – Cinderreach Emberfall , by the Hearthhouse and the Quench, near the bank The great furnace, Emberfall Both by the glassworks, near the cart station – Frostpeak Kettlepass , by the hot spring The forge, Kettlepass, by the bank and the Kettle The wheel by the Delvers' hall; no kiln – Every Hearthhouse has a cooking range, a bread oven and a work table for cooking. Campfires for cooking are dotted all over."},{"id":"basics","text":"Item Level Needs XP Twine (×3) 1 1 log 4 Planks 5 1 oak log 12 Sturdy axe 8 4 logs 45 Signboard 8 2 planks, 5 iron nails 40"},{"id":"wooden-gear","text":"Each kind of log makes staffs, rods and shields. Staffs are for magic, rods for fishing. One-handed wands are made from a log and a blank wardglass; see Wands. Bows are under Bows and arrows. Logs Staff Rod Shield Logs used Logs 1 2 3 2 / 2 / 3 Oak 5 6 7 2 / 2 / 3 Willow 10 11 12 2 / 2 / 3 Ash 20 21 22 2 / 2 / 3 Duskwood 60 61 62 2 / 2 / 3 Hexwood 70 71 72 2 / 2 / 3 Staffs and rods give 20 XP per log tier step (20 for plain logs, 40 oak, 60 willow, 80 ash). Shields give 30, 60, 90 and 120."},{"id":"bows-and-arrows","text":"Bow-making is part of Crafting. See archery for shooting them. Bowstrings String Crafting How Flax bowstring 1 Pick flax in the Fairmeadow and Windle fields, then spin it at a spinning wheel (in farmsteads and mills) Sinew bowstring 30 Dry sinew from wolfmen at a fire Frostsilk bowstring 45 Spin 2 frostsilk. Frostsilk can't be found yet. Drake sinew bowstring 60 Dry drake sinew from rust and ember drakes at a fire Bows Carve an unstrung bow at a workbench (a shortbow takes 3 logs, a longbow 4), then string it from your bag , anywhere. Levels and strings for each wood are on the Archery page. Arrows 1. Shafts: cut logs at a workbench. Plain logs give 15 shafts, oak 20, willow 30 and ash 50. 2. Feathers: gather them from hen runs at the farmsteads and gull nests on the coast. Feather 15 shafts at a time to make headless arrows . 3. Arrowheads: smith them at a forge. See smithing. 4. Fletch 15 headless arrows with 15 arrowheads at a workbench. Arrows Crafting XP per 15 Arrowheads (Smithing) Heads per bar Bronze 1 20 1 15 Iron 5 35 10 15 Steel 10 50 20 30 Starsteel 40 110 50 60 Deepsteel 50 135 60 80 Sealsteel 60 160 70 100 Hexsteel 70 190 80 100 Quivers Quiver Crafting Made from Canvas quiver 1 6 flax, 1 leather Hide quiver 30 4 leather, 1 sinew Drakehide quiver 65 2 drakehide, 1 drake sinew Archer's armour Made at a workbench from leather (the Old Mill sells it, with tallow). You can also tan cowhide into leather at a workbench from Crafting 1; cows in Fairmeadow's pens drop it. Piece Leather Hardened (adds tallow) Studded (adds iron studs) Coif 1 (1 leather) 10 20 Vambraces 3 (1 leather) 12 22 Chaps 5 (2 leather) 14 24 Body 7 (3 leather) 16 26"},{"id":"pottery","text":"Pottery is a branch of Crafting, and it gives Crafting XP. Prue Crock keeps a potter's yard across the Brin from the watermill in Fairmeadow, and her errand First Firing teaches the whole craft, start to finish. 1. Dig clay at a clay bank. 2. Wet and shape it at a potter's wheel . There are wheels in Fairmeadow, Mistmarsh, Highmoor, Cinderreach and Frostpeak. 3. Fire it in a kiln . Firing can crack a piece; the better your Crafting, the less often it happens. Clay Crafting Where to dig Fires in Brin clay 1 The Brin's banks, Fairmeadow Any kiln (Fairmeadow, Windle, Emberfall) Marsh clay 20 The mud flats south of Reedwick Any kiln; there's none in the marsh Fireclay 40 The Kiln's lower slopes, Cinderreach Only Tamsy Glass's hot kiln at the Emberfall glassworks Snowclay 60 Above Kettlepass, Frostpeak Only the hot kiln in Emberfall From Crafting 30 you can glaze your pots too. Some pots are handy as well as pretty: - A pie dish in your bag makes your pies burn less when you bake. You keep the dish. - A brew crock in your bag when you brew holds the draught with 2 more sips than a bottle, and comes back empty when it's drunk. Prue also gives you her book, and its pages show what each clay makes as you go."},{"id":"tile-satchels","text":"Bigger tile satchels at Crafting 20, 45 and 75."},{"id":"drake-gear","text":"From Crafting 65 you can work drakehide , and from 72 drake scales riveted with sealsteel rivets, into armour. From 75, wyrmglass makes a staff. See Drake gear."},{"id":"sacks-chests-and-bundles","text":"Crafting also makes the gear for carrying more. It has its own page: hauling. - Gathering sacks hang on your belt and catch logs, fish or ore when your bag is full. - Field chests are set down where you work and hold a lot. - Bundles pack several logs, fish or ores into one bag slot. - The King's Post carries your load to the bank once you have the post horn. Frostpine and Heartwood gear is in the recipe list, but those logs can't be chopped yet."}]},{"slug":"magic","title":"Magic","summary":"Cast spells by laying hex tiles in the rosette. No runes, no fixed order, and a failed try never costs you a tile.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["magic","spells","rosette","tiles","satchel","spellbook","teleport","combat","mage","quests"],"headings":[{"id":"the-short-version","text":"The short version"},{"id":"the-magic-tab","text":"The Magic tab"},{"id":"petals-open-as-you-level","text":"Petals open as you level"},{"id":"discovering-spells","text":"Discovering spells"},{"id":"casting-in-combat","text":"Casting in combat"},{"id":"hitting-and-damage","text":"Hitting and damage"},{"id":"combat-spells","text":"Combat spells"},{"id":"other-spells","text":"Other spells"},{"id":"getting-tiles","text":"Getting tiles"},{"id":"bigger-satchels","text":"Bigger satchels"},{"id":"mage-gear","text":"Mage gear"},{"id":"wands","text":"Wands"},{"id":"where-to-get-mage-gear","text":"Where to get mage gear"},{"id":"tome-editions","text":"Tome editions"},{"id":"training-magic","text":"Training Magic"},{"id":"something-for-the-centre","text":"Something for the centre"},{"id":"high-magic","text":"High Magic"}],"sections":[{"id":"the-short-version","text":"Magic in HexSeal has no runes. Instead you carry hex tiles , each holding one element, and you cast by laying tiles into the rosette , a flower of seven hexes on the Magic tab. The mix of tiles decides the spell. The order you lay them in never matters. - Six everyday tiles: Waters, Flame, Winds, Stone, Roots and Roads . - Tiles live in your tile satchel , not your 28-slot bag. Everyone carries one from the start, holding 250 of each kind , and tiles you pick up go straight into it. Tap the satchel on your tool belt to open your tiles on the Magic page. - If a mix isn't a spell, nothing happens and you keep your tiles . Experimenting is free. - Spells you discover go into your spellbook , so you only need to work a mix out once."},{"id":"the-magic-tab","text":"Open Magic from the dock. The panel is tall, and from top to bottom you'll find: - Your levels. The header shows both Magic and Etching, the skill that makes tiles. - The ? button. Opens a short How magic works card, handy if you skipped Tamsin's lesson. - The tile satchel. Your six tiles with counts. A count like 0+33 means none in the satchel and 33 in your bag. Tap a tile to lay it. - The rosette. It folds down to a single line while you browse, and opens when you lay a tile or tap a spell's name, which lays that spell's tiles for you. Tap a filled petal to lift a tile back out, and Hide folds it away again. - The preview and Cast. As you lay tiles, the preview names the spell they make if you know it, or shows Unknown mix if you don't. One big Cast button tries it. A combat spell gets Ready instead, to fire at a monster. - The spellbook. The spells you've found, plus a Heard of group for spells someone has hinted at, and a count of how many are left to find. Element filters narrow the list, and you can combine them, so picking two elements shows their mixed spells too. Pin your favourite spells to keep them at the top. Petals open as you level You start with three petals, so early spells use at most three tiles. Magic level Petals 1 3 25 4 50 5 70 6 80 7 (the centre) Locked petals show the level they open at."},{"id":"discovering-spells","text":"Lay some tiles and tap Cast . One of three things happens: - \"A new spell: …! It's in your spellbook.\" You found one and you're high enough to use it. - \"You feel a spell here (Magic N).\" The mix is a real spell, but you need a higher Magic level. Remember it. - \"The tiles don't hold.\" Not a spell. You lose nothing. When a Magic level-up opens a spell you haven't found yet, the level-up banner says so: \"A new spell to find: try your tiles in the Magic tab.\" On the Skills tab, Magic's ladder marks each spell you've reached as (can now be discovered) or (learned) . The spellbook only lists the spells you've found. Some townsfolk know a thing or two about magic and will share a hint; once they have, that spell shows under Heard of , and you can tap it to reread the hint. The book also counts how many spells are still out there to find. The preview only names a mix when you already know that spell. If it says Unknown mix , you're trying something new, and a failed try never costs tiles."},{"id":"casting-in-combat","text":"1. Open the spellbook and tap a combat spell. This readies it and closes the tab. 2. Tap a monster. You walk until you're within 5 hexes and can see it. You can't cast across water or over cliffs. 3. You keep casting every 5 ticks (3 seconds) while you have tiles. This is autocast. 4. Tap the spell again to go back to your weapon. Gust and Snare are different: they fire once at the monster you tap, then you're back on your weapon. Hitting and damage - Accuracy comes from your Magic level plus your gear's magic bonus, against the monster's Defence. Spells ignore the monster's armour. - Max hit goes up with your gear's magic damage bonus (up to +30% in total) and element bonuses. - Elements matter. Each strikes 25% harder against the creatures it's strong against: Element Strong against Water Fire creatures (heat golems, cinders, lamplighters) Earth Bone (skeletons and the like) Fire Wood and bog (mire, bog, rootbound and moss creatures) - Fire spells also burn for 1 damage a tick for 3 ticks. Lightning jumps to a second monster within 2 hexes for half damage. Ice freezes the target for 3 ticks. These only trigger when the hit does damage."},{"id":"combat-spells","text":"There are five ranks: Dart, Lance, Blast, Wave and Storm. The three Darts are in your spellbook from the start. Spell Magic Max hit XP per cast Extra Water Dart 1 4 5 Strong vs fire Earth Dart 3 6 5 Strong vs bone Fire Dart 5 5 5 Burns; strong vs wood The Lances start at Magic 10, the Blasts at 30, the Waves at 50 and the Storms at 82. Your spellbook shows the level and tile count for each one; working out the mix is up to you. Waves and Storms draw on the Hollow , and every cast with a Hollow tile costs you 1 health . A Storm fills the whole rosette."},{"id":"other-spells","text":"Spells that aren't for fighting cast the moment you tap them in the spellbook. These are in your spellbook from the start: Spell Magic Tiles What it does Home Tide 1 Free Takes you home to Saltmere. Once every 30 minutes. Light 2 1 Flame Lights the area round you for 2 minutes. Gust 4 1 Winds Pushes a monster back one hex. Cast near a windmill, it swings the sails round. Snare 6 1 Roots Holds a monster in place for 5 ticks. Hearthspark 7 Flame, Roots A campfire beside you for a minute, ready for cooking. Mend 8 Roots, Waters Heals 1 a tick for 8 ticks. Uses your attack turn. Kingsferry Teleport 12 3 Roads To the Old Mill waystone in Fairmeadow. Reedwick Teleport 20 2 Roads, Waters To Reedwick in Mistmarsh. Windle Teleport 22 2 Roads, Winds To Windle in Highmoor. Thornwick Teleport 32 2 Roads, Roots To Thornwick in Greenwood. Emberfall Teleport 38 2 Roads, Flame To Emberfall in Cinderreach. Kettlepass Teleport 45 2 Roads, Stone To Kettlepass in Frostpeak. Waymark 55 1 Roads Sets an anchor where you stand. Return to Waymark 55 3 Roads, Winds Takes you back to your anchor. Crownwall Gate 68 3 Roads, Hollow To inside any Crownwall gate you've walked through. Hexhold Teleport 90 3 Roads, Heart To the Hexhold's door in the Crown Valley, once you've touched the waystone near it. Kindle 72 2 Flame, 1 Heart Learned in a late Cinderreach quest. Lights a fire anywhere that lasts twice as long as Hearthspark's, or, cast at a forge, your next 20 bars each take one coal fewer. Teleports need you to have touched that town's waystone first. Walk up to it once and it remembers you. See travel. More spells sleep in the book, waiting to be found: gathering boosts, stronger healing, and a few surprises."},{"id":"getting-tiles","text":"- A starter pouch. On Saltmere, ask Hattie about the glowing tiles: 20 Waters, 20 Flame and 10 Roots, once. - Shops. Ida Rook's stall on Saltmere sells Flame and Waters. Silas Quill's curio shop in Kingsferry sells all six. See shops-and-markets. - Monsters. Many drop tiles. Drowned creatures drop Waters, wolfmen and reavers drop Winds, wreckers and lamplighters drop Flame, mire golems drop Roots and Stone. - Lucky finds. About 1 in 150 gathering actions turns up a tile: Waters from fishing, Roots from woodcutting, Stone from mining, Flame from smithing and cooking. - Etching. Make them yourself. See etching. Tiles in your bag count for casting too, but the satchel is used first. Tap a tile's item card and choose Put in satchel to move it out of your bag. Shops and your own stall can sell tiles straight from the satchel. Bigger satchels Made at a workbench with Crafting: Satchel Crafting Holds of each tile Made from Leather satchel 20 500 3 leather, 4 twine Reinforced satchel 45 1,000 4 leather, 2 tallow, 1 steel bar Warden's satchel 75 2,500 2 drakehide, 1 drake sinew, 10 blank wardglass Tile Shop price (base) Waters 8 coins Flame 10 coins Winds 10 coins Roots 12 coins Stone 14 coins Roads 24 coins Shops charge more the more you buy before they restock. See shops-and-markets."},{"id":"mage-gear","text":"Staffs take both hands and are the strongest weapons for casting. They're crafted at a workbench from 2 logs, and the Crafting level matches the level to wield one. Better staffs add accuracy, damage and a chance to save a tile on a cast. Staff Level Magic Damage Tile save Wooden 1 +3 +1% – Oak 5 +5 +2% 2% Willow 10 +7 +3% 4% Ash 20 +10 +4% 6% Emberleaf 30 +13 +5% 9% Frostpine 40 +16 +6% 12% Elderwood 50 +19 +7% 15% Duskwood 60 +22 +8% 18% Hexwood 70 +26 +9% 22% Heartwood 80 +30 +10% 25% Hexwood and Heartwood staffs also cast a tick faster. Wielding a staff moves any tome or shield you're wearing to your bag. Wands Wands are weaker than staffs but take only one hand , so a tome or a shield fits in the other. Each is crafted at a workbench from a log and a blank wardglass, at the same Crafting level as its wield level. Wand Level Magic Damage Wooden 1 +2 +1% Oak 5 +3 +1% Willow 10 +4 +2% Ash 20 +6 +2% Emberleaf 30 +8 +3% Frostpine 40 +10 +4% Elderwood 50 +11 +4% Duskwood 60 +13 +5% Hexwood 70 +16 +5% Heartwood 80 +18 +6% Duskwood, Hexwood and Heartwood wands also cast a tick faster. The wyrmglass staff (wield at Magic 70, crafted at Crafting 75 from 3 wyrmglass and 2 duskwood logs) gives +27 Magic, +9% damage and a 22% tile save, casts a tick faster, and every fire spell costs one Flame tile fewer (never less than one). It sells for 3,100 coins. Robes come in tiers: woollen (Magic 1) and dyed wool (Magic 20) from Silas Quill, and reedsilk (Magic 40) further afield. There are hats, tops, bottoms, and gowns that fill both top and legs. Capes and tomes (held in the shield hand) add more, and each tome boosts certain elements. Each element's tome comes from a magic expert, for a small favour. See Where to get mage gear. Where to get mage gear Gear Where it comes from Staffs Craft them at a workbench (2 logs of that wood, at the matching Crafting level). A few monsters drop them now and then: Bone Guard lamplighters (willow staff), Cinder lamplighters (ash staff), and Rime lamplighters and cold-callers (frostpine staff). Wands Craft them at a workbench from one log and one blank wardglass, at the matching Crafting level. See etching for blank wardglass. Wyrmglass staff Crafting 75, from wyrmglass and duskwood logs. See wyrmholm. Robes Silas Quill's curio shop in Kingsferry (woollen and dyed wool) and Mother Gloam's chest in Reedwick (reedsilk). Element tomes The magic experts below, each for a small favour. Tome editions Rebind a tome yourself. See Tome editions. Any monster also has a small chance, about 1 in 100, to drop a weapon of its own. Tome Ask Where They want Roots Old Tamsin The Salted Eel, Saltmere 3 cooked trout Roads Old Hob Fairmeadow 3 bronze bars Waters Mother Gloam Reedwick, Mistmarsh 5 raw trout Winds Old Corra By the Sixstones, Highmoor 5 oak logs Flame Old Bellows Cinderreach 5 coal Stone Gunna Delve Frostpeak 5 cooked perch Tome editions An element tome can be rebound at a workbench into stronger editions: Bound at Magic 30, Cornered at Magic 55 and Etched at Magic 80. Each step takes the tome before it, a stack of that house's tiles and Warden's ink , which you brew in brewing from Brewing 20. Tile saving tops out at 40% and magic damage at +30%, however much you stack. Metal armour throws off your aim. A metal body costs 10 magic accuracy, legs 6, a helm or shield 4 each."},{"id":"training-magic","text":"- Combat spells give their XP per cast, plus 2 XP for every point of damage . They also train Hitpoints, like melee. - Every other spell gives a flat amount of XP each cast. - Magic counts toward your combat level."},{"id":"something-for-the-centre","text":"Five long quest lines end with something that changes your Magic: one from Old Hob in Kingsferry, one from Tobin Reed in Reedwick, one from Old Corra at the Sixstones, one from Hesper Vane in Thornwick, and one from Tamsy Glass in Emberfall. See quests. Each is a puzzle that takes you across its province."},{"id":"high-magic","text":"Magic What you get 70 Mastery: one cast in ten takes no tiles. 75 Spell focus: a staff saves 5% more tiles. 85 Readying a combat spell keeps the last one ready too, so you can swap between two. The quick bar. Your first three pinned spells sit at the bottom left, just above the menu. Tap one to ready or cast it, the same as in the spellbook. From Magic 85, a swap button there switches between your two ready spells. Pin spells in the Magic tab."}]},{"slug":"archery","title":"Archery","summary":"Shoot bows from a few hexes off, with arrows that live in a quiver on your tool belt and mostly come back.","section":"Skills","status":"partial","tags":["archery","ranged","bows","shortbow","longbow","arrows","quiver","combat","fletching","leather","windle","butts"],"headings":[{"id":"the-short-version","text":"The short version"},{"id":"getting-started","text":"Getting started"},{"id":"shooting","text":"Shooting"},{"id":"styles","text":"Styles"},{"id":"armoured-and-light-foes","text":"Armoured and light foes"},{"id":"your-quiver","text":"Your quiver"},{"id":"bows","text":"Bows"},{"id":"arrows","text":"Arrows"},{"id":"archer-s-armour","text":"Archer's armour"},{"id":"where-to-buy","text":"Where to buy"}],"sections":[{"id":"the-short-version","text":"- Archery is a combat skill. Wield a bow, put arrows in your quiver, and tap a monster. You walk until it's in range and in sight, then shoot on the tick. - Bows take both hands , so no shield. - Arrows go in a quiver on your tool belt , never in your bag. - Arrows that don't break land near the target . Walk over them and they go straight back in your quiver. - Archery counts toward your combat level , alongside melee and Magic. Whichever route is highest counts. See Combat level."},{"id":"getting-started","text":"Captain Hale Yarrow runs the archery butts in Windle (highmoor). He only teaches people who make their own arrows, and his quest, First Feather , shows you how. Old Tamsin at the Salted Eel will point you his way. Before that, the straw targets at the butts give 8 Archery XP a hit, up to Archery 10 . The butts shop sells feathers, bronze, iron and steel arrows, flax bowstrings, a canvas quiver and a wooden shortbow, so you can start shooting before you can make everything yourself."},{"id":"shooting","text":"Shortbow Longbow Range 5 hexes 7 hexes Speed Every 4 ticks (2.4 s) Every 6 ticks (3.6 s) Draw None Adds to your max hit - Arrows fly over water and down off terraces and cliffs. Shooting down from higher ground reaches 1 hex further . - You can't shoot through trees or walls, or up onto a terrace higher than you. - Your hit chance comes from your Archery level and your gear's Archery bonus. Bows, vambraces and archer's armour all add to it. - Your max hit comes from your Archery level and your arrow's strength, plus a longbow's draw. - A bow only takes arrows up to its own tier. If the arrows at the front of your quiver are too good for the bow in hand, it swaps in the best kind it can take. Styles With a bow in hand, the attack styles on the Pack tab change to: Style Trains Effect Accurate (the default) Archery Hit more often Rapid Archery Shoot 1 tick faster Longrange Archery and Defence 2 hexes more range Each point of damage gives 4 XP (Longrange splits it, 2 Archery and 2 Defence) and 1.33 Hitpoints XP . Armoured and light foes Arrows do a quarter less against armoured foes and a quarter more against light ones. The monster card shows which a monster is. Golems, sentries and knights tend to be armoured; wolfmen, bandits and drakes are light. See monsters."},{"id":"your-quiver","text":"The quiver sits on your tool belt and holds arrows with no stack limit. Without a proper quiver, about 4 in 5 arrows come back. Quiver Crafting Arrow kinds Arrows that come back Canvas quiver 1 1 80% Hide quiver 30 2 85% Drakehide quiver 65 3 90% Arrows you've shot lie on the ground for about five minutes."},{"id":"bows","text":"Wood Archery to wield Shortbow (Crafting) Longbow (Crafting) String Logs 1 1 3 Flax Oak 5 5 8 Flax Willow 10 10 13 Flax Ash 20 20 23 Flax Emberleaf 30 30 33 Sinew Frostpine 40 40 Sinew Elderwood 50 50 53 Frostsilk Duskwood 60 60 63 Drake sinew Hexwood 70 70 73 Drake sinew How to make them is on the Crafting page. A frostpine longbow, frostsilk to string elderwood bows, a few special bows, trick arrows and more archer's armour."},{"id":"arrows","text":"Arrows Archery to shoot Strength Fits bows from Bronze 1 7 Logs Iron 5 12 Oak Steel 10 18 Willow Starsteel 40 33 Frostpine Deepsteel 50 38 Elderwood Sealsteel 60 44 Duskwood Hexsteel 70 49 Hexwood Max hit at a glance (Accurate, no Archery gear bonus counted): Archery Bronze, shortbow Iron, oak longbow Starsteel, shortbow Sealsteel, duskwood longbow 10 2 2 3 3 20 3 4 4 5 40 5 6 7 9 70 9 10 12 15 99 12 13 16 20"},{"id":"archer-s-armour","text":"Light leather that needs Defence and Archery at its level, and adds to your Archery hit chance as well as your Defence. Each set has a coif, body, chaps and vambraces . Vambraces give the most Archery bonus. Set Wear at Made from Leather 1 Leather Hardened leather 10 Leather and tallow Studded leather 20 Leather and iron studs See Archer's armour for each piece."},{"id":"where-to-buy","text":"- The Windle butts (Highmoor): feathers, arrows, flax bowstrings, a canvas quiver and a wooden shortbow. - Ossie Nock's bench in Thornwick (greenwood): feathers, arrow shafts, and unstrung oak, willow and ash bows. - The Old Mill (fairmeadow): leather and tallow."}]},{"slug":"etching","title":"Etching","summary":"Make your own magic tiles. Mine hexslate, smelt it into wardglass, and etch it at a Warden statue.","section":"Skills","status":"partial","tags":["etching","magic","tiles","hexslate","wardglass","chisel"],"headings":[{"id":"step-by-step","text":"Step by step"},{"id":"tiles-and-levels","text":"Tiles and levels"},{"id":"warden-statues","text":"Warden statues"},{"id":"chisels","text":"Chisels"}],"sections":[{"id":"step-by-step","text":"1. Mine hexslate. It's in every region, needs Mining 1, and never runs out. See mining. 2. Smelt it at a forge into blank wardglass . No level needed: 1 tick, 2 Etching XP. 3. Walk to a Warden statue. Long-press it and tap Etch . Each blank becomes tiles of that statue's element. If a monster hits you, etching stops. Sure Chisel: from Etching 30 , each blank has a chance of an extra tile: 10%, rising to 15% at Etching 60 and 20% at 90. Smelt 1 hexslate with 1 bone ash for 2 blanks (4 XP). Bone ash comes from burning bones at a forge. See bones."},{"id":"tiles-and-levels","text":"Each blank gives one tile, and more as your Etching level climbs. Tile Etching XP per tile Extra tile per blank at Waters 1 5 11, 22, 44, 66 Roads 5 7 40, 75 Winds 10 7 20, 40, 60, 80 Flame 20 8 30, 50, 70, 90 Roots 25 8 35, 55, 75, 95 Stone 35 9 45, 65, 85 Hollow 50 15 85 So at Etching 44, a blank at a Waters statue makes 4 Waters tiles."},{"id":"warden-statues","text":"Element Statue in the game Waters The Gullwatch shrine in Saltmere (a small wayside shrine that works like a statue), and the Warden of Waters in Mistmarsh. Monsters near one of the Mistmarsh statues only come out at night, so etch there by day. Roads Fairmeadow Winds Highmoor Roots Greenwood (needs Etching 25) Flame Cinderreach (needs Etching 20) Stone Frostpeak (needs Etching 35) Hollow The Hollow rift in Greenwood (needs Etching 50). It etches greyed slate from the Crown Valley, not wardglass."},{"id":"chisels","text":"Hang a chisel on your tool belt to etch faster and sometimes get a bonus tile. Chisels are smithed at a forge. Chisel Etching Ticks per blank Bonus tile chance None – 2 – Bronze 1 2 2% Iron 5 2 4% Steel 10 1 5% Sealsteel 60 1 15% Hexsteel 70 1 17% The etcher's set (apron, cap and gloves) adds more: the gloves give +5% bonus tile chance, and the full set gives +2.5% Etching XP. Bonus tile chance tops out at 20%. - Rarer tiles at higher Etching levels. - Higher-level Etching perks up to 99."}]},{"slug":"trade","title":"Trade and your stall","summary":"Rent a stall in Kingsferry's Grand Market, set your prices, and let shoppers buy while you're off doing other things.","section":"Skills","status":"shipped","tags":["trade","stall","market","coins","haggling","grand-market"],"headings":[{"id":"getting-a-stall","text":"Getting a stall"},{"id":"selling","text":"Selling"},{"id":"how-shoppers-react","text":"How shoppers react"},{"id":"the-trade-skill","text":"The Trade skill"}],"sections":[{"id":"getting-a-stall","text":"In Kingsferry, two town works from Old Hob: 1. Reopen three Grand Market stalls (25,000 coins, 30 planks, 30 oak logs). 2. Rent a Grand Market stall (10,000 coins). Your stall then stands on the square, east of the bank. Tap it to open it."},{"id":"selling","text":"- Tap Put out on a bag item to set out the whole stack. - Each stack starts at 1.3 times its fair price . Fair is what Kingsferry would normally pay, ignoring any flooding. - Drag a stack's price slider anywhere from half to 2.5 times fair. A note says how shoppers will take it. - Take back returns a stack to your bag. Shoppers keep coming wherever you are in the world , as long as the game is running. Each picks a stack at random. Sales go into the till ; tap Collect to move the coins to your bag."},{"id":"how-shoppers-react","text":"Your price What happens Fair or below They always buy Up to 1.25× fair Most buy Up to 1.5× fair About half buy. This is the sweet spot. Above 1.5×, up to your greedy cap They haggle, and sometimes pay something between 1.5× and your price Above your cap They walk off Your greedy cap starts at 1.8× fair and rises to 2.2× at Trade 99. Higher Trade also wins you more of each haggle."},{"id":"the-trade-skill","text":"Trade level What it gives 1 4 stacks on your stall, a shopper about every 7 seconds 10 A 5th stack 20 A 6th stack 30 A 7th stack 40 An 8th stack Higher Shoppers come more often (twice as often by 99), and you haggle better You earn 0.1 Trade XP for every coin from stall sales, notice-board orders and Market Warrants."}]},{"slug":"brewing","title":"Brewing","summary":"Forage wild herbs, brew draughts at a still, and drink them from 4-sip bottles to be better at things for a while.","section":"Skills","status":"partial","tags":["brewing","potions","draughts","herbs","foraging","bottles","sips","hud-pins","herbal","boosts"],"headings":[{"id":"the-short-version","text":"The short version"},{"id":"getting-started","text":"Getting started"},{"id":"herbs","text":"Herbs"},{"id":"brewing-a-draught","text":"Brewing a draught"},{"id":"brewing-perks","text":"Brewing perks"},{"id":"bottles-and-hud-pins","text":"Bottles and HUD pins"},{"id":"drinking","text":"Drinking"},{"id":"draughts-so-far","text":"Draughts so far"},{"id":"warden-s-ink","text":"Warden's ink"},{"id":"marsh-fever","text":"Marsh fever"},{"id":"brewing-99","text":"Brewing 99"},{"id":"harsh-places","text":"Harsh places"},{"id":"what-s-coming","text":"What's coming"}],"sections":[{"id":"the-short-version","text":"Brewing is a skill for getting ready. You forage herbs out in the world, brew them with one more ingredient at a brewing station, and drink the result to play at a higher level for a while. - Each brew makes a bottle of 4 sips in your pack. Tap it to drink a sip. - Bottles take a pack slot each, and stack in the bank. - Pin a brew to the HUD to drink it with one tap. - A boost never changes your real level, and never counts for wearing gear, quest requirements or Etching. - Brewing counts toward your character level, like every other skill."},{"id":"getting-started","text":"After Washed Ashore, Hattie on Saltmere has a quest for you, The Mender's Kit . It opens the Brewing skill, teaches you to brew at Hattie's salve still beside her cottage, and sends you off with 5 bottles of Grit Draught. Ask her about it. See quests. Everything about Brewing lives on the Brews tab of the Magic menu, next to Spells. The Brews tab lists Your brews , and its brewing book lists every herb, where it grows and the nearest cauldron, and says where made ingredients (like bone ash) are made. Tap ? for a help card. Now and then a one-time tip will suggest a brew that would help, for example against heat, cold or fever, or when you're low on health."},{"id":"herbs","text":"Herbs grow in small leafy patches. Tap one to forage. A patch empties after a few pickings and grows back. Herb Brewing Where Saltwort 1 Saltmere Meadowsweet 5 Fairmeadow Bog myrtle 10 Mistmarsh. It glows faintly at night. Heather 20 Highmoor Elderflower 30 Greenwood. It keeps its own hours. Firethorn 40 Cinderreach, by the lava Rimecap 50 Frostpeak Greyleaf 60 Greenwood, in the Hollow Each herb has its own spot. Finding them is part of the fun, so this page won't say exactly where. From Brewing 30 , herb patches show on your region maps as a green H ."},{"id":"brewing-a-draught","text":"1. Go to a brewing station and tap it. 2. Pick a herb and a second ingredient from your bag. 3. If it's a real recipe and your level is high enough, it brews a bottle of 4 sips into your pack (or your bank, if your pack is full), and the draught goes into your Herbal . 4. If it isn't, it turns into a murky brew . You lose the herb and the ingredient, so guesses aren't quite free. Mother Gloam will buy murky brews for a coin or two. The Herbal, on the station's sheet, lists every draught. Ones you haven't brewed show as ??? with their level and a hint about the second ingredient. Station Region Hattie's salve still Saltmere, beside Hattie's cottage The Wayside apothecary Fairmeadow Mother Gloam's cauldron Mistmarsh, by her hut The peat still Highmoor The root kettle Greenwood The vent cauldron Cinderreach The spring kettle Frostpeak Brewing perks Brewing Perk 20 Combine on a part-drunk bottle pours all your part bottles of that draught together 30 Herb patches show on your maps 40 A 25% chance of a second bottle per brew 45 Brews come in Emberfall amber bottles of 6 sips 65 Skill draughts last 25% longer 70 The chance of a second bottle rises to 50% 85 Brews come in wardglass bottles of 8 sips 95 Twin bottles: two skill draughts in one bottle, and one sip gives you both"},{"id":"bottles-and-hud-pins","text":"- Every brew is a bottle of 4 sips (6 from Brewing 45, 8 from 85). Each bottle takes one slot in your pack, and they stack in the bank. Carry a clay brew crock (see Pottery) and the draught goes in it instead, with 2 more sips. - Tap a bottle in your pack to drink a sip. Drinking takes your turn, like eating. - Pin to HUD: hold a bottle (or food, or another usable) and tap Pin to HUD . You can pin up to 4 , and a pinned brew drinks from whichever bottle of it you're carrying. - Menders will pour your part-drunk bottles together for free. - If you fall, your bottles go into your grave with the rest of your pack."},{"id":"drinking","text":"While a boost is running, a badge sits under the place name. Tap it to see how long is left. - Skill draughts last a number of actions, so they don't wear off while you walk. - Combat and travel draughts last a set time. - You can have one draught of each kind going at once. Drinking another of the same kind replaces it. - Shape draughts change what you are for a short while. Only one can run at a time, and you can tap its badge to end it early. - Stout adds gold hearts on top of your health. Hits take them first, healing can't refill them, and they go when it wears off."},{"id":"draughts-so-far","text":"There are twenty-one draughts so far, from Brewing 1 to 60, plus one more at 99. Their names and effects show in the Herbal once you've brewed them. The Herbal's hints point you to each second ingredient. Some come from other skills, and some you'll only find if you keep your eyes open, sometimes only at a certain time of day. Brewers also have a use for fish scales , which you get at the scaling boards by the water (see Scaling boards). Some creatures can be picked up, if you're there at the right time: bats at dusk, moonmoths round the Lost Lantern at night, and ember beetles under the Kiln's stones. Some monsters leave useful bits too: wolfmen drop claws , frost trolls drop teeth , and golems drop pebbles ."},{"id":"warden-s-ink","text":"From Brewing 20 you can brew Warden's ink at a cauldron, two pots at a time. It isn't a draught: mages use it to rebind their tomes. See Tome editions."},{"id":"marsh-fever","text":"Some Mistmarsh creatures, bog-drowned and mire golems, can give you marsh fever . A badge shows while you have it. It deals 1 damage every few seconds for about a minute, and catching it again restarts the clock. - Marsh Cure ends it at once and keeps it off for a while. - Gloam's tonic also ends it. Mother Gloam sells three a day. - A fall ends it too."},{"id":"brewing-99","text":"Reaching Brewing 99 is a big moment, with four rewards, and something happens at Reedwick's pool."},{"id":"harsh-places","text":"Four places wear you down by 1 health every so often while you're out in them: Place When Ward The Hollow, in Greenwood Always Thawwater (or Hollowbane) The Kiln, in Cinderreach Always Cinderward The nests and crater on Wyrmholm (2 health a time) Always Cinderward Frostpeak's open snow At night Frostward They never take your last point of health, and they can't reach you indoors."},{"id":"what-s-coming","text":"- More draughts. - Herb hints from station keepers, and a herb pouch."}]},{"slug":"muscle-memory","title":"Muscle Memory","summary":"Once you know a skill well enough, your character keeps working at it while the game is closed, slower than playing.","section":"Skills","status":"partial","tags":["muscle-memory","offline","away","gathering","afk","old-hands","quests","mentors"],"headings":[{"id":"switching-it-on","text":"Switching it on"},{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How it works"},{"id":"which-skills-and-when","text":"Which skills, and when"},{"id":"tiers","text":"Tiers"},{"id":"the-hands-chip","text":"The hands chip"},{"id":"while-you-were-away","text":"While you were away"},{"id":"turning-it-off","text":"Turning it off"}],"sections":[{"id":"switching-it-on","text":"Muscle Memory starts switched off. The first time one of your skills reaches its Muscle Memory level, the person who taught you that skill sends for you, and their quest switches it on. Finish it once and every skill has Muscle Memory from then on. Skill Mentor Woodcutting Pell Fishing Gaffer Moss Mining, Smithing Brannoc Cooking, Etching Old Tamsin Crafting Hattie Combe Until you've finished it, the dark hands chip points you to the quest: it shows what to do next without a tap, and the mentor gets a red ! on your region map. After that, a skill's mentor has a word for you when it unlocks Muscle Memory and when it reaches each new tier. The quest's steps are in your Journal; this wiki won't spoil them."},{"id":"how-it-works","text":"Start gathering or making something, then leave the app: go to the home screen, lock your phone, or switch apps. Your character keeps working, at a slower pace than if you were playing. When you come back, the game works out what you made using the same chances, burns and XP as normal play. - Always slower than playing. It's a bonus, not a replacement. - Away for less than 30 seconds? Nothing happens. - It stops when your storage is full, you run out of materials, or after 12 hours . - It never runs in a fight or with something hostile coming for you. The fight waits exactly as you left it, and the card says \"Your hands stopped: you were under attack.\" - It doesn't run while bundling either. - Cooking, Smithing and Crafting only finish the batch you chose."},{"id":"which-skills-and-when","text":"Skill Muscle Memory from Woodcutting, Fishing, Mining Level 10 Cooking, Smithing, Crafting Level 15 Etching Level 20 Combat, Magic and quests never carry on while you're away."},{"id":"tiers","text":"Your level in the skill you're training sets how well your hands keep going. Tier Level Pace What fills up The Knack 10 25% Your bag Habit 30 35% Your bag and your sacks Routine 50 45% Plus a field chest within 2 hexes Second Nature 70 55% Breaks of up to 2 minutes count at full pace Mastery 90 65% – Making skills start at level 15 (Etching at 20), so their first tier, The Knack, begins there. The later tiers open at the same levels. Make a sack and set down a field chest before you leave. More room means more hours of work. See hauling."},{"id":"the-hands-chip","text":"A small hands chip sits under the place name. - Lit , with a note like \"Bag full in ~16 min\" : leaving now would count. - Dark : it wouldn't. Tap it to see why. When it's dark, it tells you the reason, for example that wardglass smelting is Etching work and only counts from Etching 20."},{"id":"while-you-were-away","text":"When you come back, a While you were away card shows your hero, how long you were gone, how long your hands worked, why they stopped, the items, XP and levels you gained, and any draught sips you used."},{"id":"turning-it-off","text":"··· › Settings › Muscle Memory switches it on or off. - Rare finds at Mastery. - An optional phone notification when your bag is full."}]},{"slug":"combat","title":"Fighting","summary":"How melee works on the tick, attack styles, hit chance and max hit, combat level, and healing.","section":"Combat","status":"shipped","tags":["combat","melee","attack-styles","combat-level","healing","food","hitpoints"],"headings":[{"id":"the-basics","text":"The basics"},{"id":"attack-styles","text":"Attack styles"},{"id":"hit-chance-and-max-hit","text":"Hit chance and max hit"},{"id":"combat-level","text":"Combat level"},{"id":"healing","text":"Healing"},{"id":"danger-music","text":"Danger music"}],"sections":[{"id":"the-basics","text":"Tap a monster. You walk up beside it and start swinging on your own. Melee only reaches the next hex , and never across a cliff. For fighting at range, see magic. There are four melee skills: Skill What it does Attack How often you hit. Needed to wield better weapons. Strength How hard you hit (your max hit). Defence How often you get hit. Needed to wear better armour. Hitpoints Your health. Your max HP is your Hitpoints level. You start at 10. Attack speed comes from your weapon: daggers and swords swing every 4 ticks (2.4 s), maces and battleaxes every 5, warhammers and greatswords every 6. Bare fists swing every 4. Each swing first rolls to hit. A miss shows a small miss . A hit always deals at least 1, up to your max hit. The same goes for monsters hitting you. Monsters fight with what they carry: archers draw and shoot, and mages cast from a staff."},{"id":"attack-styles","text":"Pick a style on the Pack tab, under your gear. It chooses which skill your XP goes to and gives a small hidden boost. Style Trains Boost Accurate (the default) Attack Hit more often Aggressive Strength Hit harder Defensive Defence Get hit less Every point of damage you deal gives 4 XP in your style's skill and 1.33 Hitpoints XP . Misses give nothing. Tougher foes teach more. Fight something above your combat level and every hit gives extra XP, more the bigger the gap. Pick on things far below you and the XP drops off. This counts for melee, archery and magic alike."},{"id":"hit-chance-and-max-hit","text":"HexSeal uses Old School RuneScape's maths. - Your hit chance compares your Attack level and your gear's Attack bonus against the monster's Defence. - Your max hit comes from your Strength level and your gear's Strength bonus. - Monsters roll the same way against your Defence level and your gear's Defence bonus. Max hit at a glance (no style boost): Strength No weapon Bronze sword Iron sword Steel sword 1 1 1 1 1 20 3 3 3 4 40 5 5 6 7 80 9 10 10 12 99 11 12 13 15"},{"id":"combat-level","text":"Your combat level comes from Attack, Strength, Defence, Hitpoints, Magic and Archery, using the RuneScape formula. A new character is combat level 3 . Melee, Magic and Archery count as three routes; whichever is highest counts. In a fight, each enemy shows its name and level over its health bar, coloured against your own combat level: green is easier, cream about even, orange harder and red much harder. Bosses just say Boss ."},{"id":"healing","text":"- Eat. Tap cooked food in your bag. It's eaten on the next tick. You can't eat at full health. - Rest. Out of a fight, with no angry monsters within 6 hexes, you heal 1 HP a minute. - Fish broth heals 4 at once, then 1 a tick for 5 ticks, and has two bites. See cooking. - Spells. Mend, and more you can discover. See magic. - Beds and benches. Sleeping at an inn or in your cottage, or resting at the Tide chapel bench (a town work) heals you fully. Food Heals Sells for Food Heals Sells for Shrimp 3 5 Salmon 12 35 Sardine 5 8 Marsh eel 16 90 Herring 6 12 Frostfin 18 160 Perch 8 18 Bread 6 6 Trout 10 25 Pike 14 45 Cinder eel 22 360 Sealray 35 1,980 Hexcarp 30 1,200 Kilnfin 26 720 Raw fish sell for about 70% of the cooked price, and burnt fish for 1 coin. A food's item card shows how much it heals."},{"id":"danger-music","text":"When a monster that's fighting or chasing you is within 6 hexes, the music shifts to a danger theme. It eases back once you're clear."}]},{"slug":"gear","title":"Equipment and gear","summary":"Seven slots, metal tiers from bronze up, weapon types and their speeds, and what you need to wear things.","section":"Combat","status":"shipped","tags":["gear","equipment","weapons","armour","shields","metal-tiers"],"headings":[{"id":"your-gear-on-the-pack-tab","text":"Your gear on the Pack tab"},{"id":"what-you-need-to-wear-things","text":"What you need to wear things"},{"id":"metal-tiers","text":"Metal tiers"},{"id":"weapons","text":"Weapons"},{"id":"armour","text":"Armour"},{"id":"archer-s-armour","text":"Archer's armour"},{"id":"drake-gear","text":"Drake gear"}],"sections":[{"id":"your-gear-on-the-pack-tab","text":"Open Pack from the dock. Your gear sits above your bag: a paper doll with seven slots around a 3D model of your hero that you can spin: - Left: Head, Cape, Body, Legs - Right: Weapon, Shield, Hands Below are your total Attack, Strength and Defence bonuses (with the change from your last swap), your combat level, the attack style buttons and your tool belt. - Tap an item in your bag to wear it. - Tap a worn slot to take it off. You need room in your bag. Weapons and shields show in your hero's hands, and armour shows on your hero in its metal's colour."},{"id":"what-you-need-to-wear-things","text":"Gear Needs Weapons Attack level Bows and arrows Archery level Armour and shields Defence level Archer's armour Defence and Archery level Mage robes, capes and tomes Magic level Etcher's set Etching level If you're short, the game tells you, for example: \"You need Attack level 5 to wear that.\" Gowns fill both the Body and Legs slots."},{"id":"metal-tiers","text":"Metal Level to wear Level to smith (from) Bronze 1 1 Iron 5 10 Steel 10 20 Embersteel 20 30 Frostsilver 30 40 Starsteel 40 50 Deepsteel 50 60 Sealsteel 60 70 Hexsteel 70 80 All gear is smithed from bars at a forge. See smithing."},{"id":"weapons","text":"Weapon Speed Bronze (Att/Str) Iron Steel Dagger 2.4 s 5 / 3 9 / 5 12 / 8 Sword 2.4 s 8 / 7 14 / 12 20 / 18 Mace 3.0 s 6 / 9 10 / 16 15 / 22 Battleaxe 3.0 s 9 / 12 16 / 21 22 / 30 Warhammer 3.6 s 8 / 15 14 / 26 20 / 38 Greatsword 3.6 s 12 / 14 21 / 24 30 / 35 Each metal above steel adds a little more again; an embersteel sword is 26 / 23, a deepsteel one 44 / 38. Slow weapons hit harder per swing; fast ones hit more often. Staffs are crafted from logs and are mostly for magic."},{"id":"armour","text":"Defence bonus by piece: Metal Full helm Viking helm Platebody Platelegs Square shield Bronze 3 4 11 7 8 Iron 5 7 19 12 14 Steel 8 10 28 18 20 Embersteel 10 13 36 23 26 Frostsilver 12 16 44 28 32 Starsteel 14 19 52 33 38 Deepsteel 16 22 60 38 44 Sealsteel 19 25 69 44 50 Hexsteel 21 28 77 49 56 Wooden round shields are crafted from logs: wooden 5, oak 9, willow 12, ash 16. Metal armour throws off spellcasting. If you fight with magic, see Mage gear."},{"id":"archer-s-armour","text":"Light leather for archery. It needs Defence and Archery at its level, and adds to your Archery hit chance as well as your Defence. Bows take both hands, so there's no shield. Piece Leather (1) Hardened leather (10) Studded leather (20) Coif 2 Def, +2 Archery 4 Def, +3 Archery 6 Def, +5 Archery Body 6 Def, +4 Archery 11 Def, +6 Archery 16 Def, +9 Archery Chaps 4 Def, +3 Archery 7 Def, +5 Archery 10 Def, +7 Archery Vambraces 1 Def, +5 Archery 2 Def, +8 Archery 3 Def, +11 Archery Crafted at a workbench. See Crafting."},{"id":"drake-gear","text":"Made from what the drakes of Wyrmholm leave behind. Drakehide and Wyrmscale are crafted at a workbench; wyrmfang pieces are smithed at a forge. Piece Wear at Bonus Made with Drakehide coif Defence 60 14 Def Crafting 65: 2 drakehide Drakehide chaps Defence 60 28 Def Crafting 66: 3 drakehide Drakehide body Defence 60 44 Def Crafting 68: 5 drakehide Wyrmscale helm Defence 70 21 Def Crafting 72: 6 drake scales, 4 sealsteel rivets Wyrmscale shield Defence 70 56 Def Crafting 73: 14 drake scales, 8 rivets Wyrmscale legs Defence 70 49 Def Crafting 74: 12 drake scales, 8 rivets Wyrmscale body Defence 70 77 Def Crafting 76: 18 drake scales, 10 rivets Wyrmfang spear Attack 70 54 Att / 52 Str Smithing 75: 2 wyrmfangs, 2 sealsteel bars - Drakehide is light, so it doesn't throw off spellcasting the way metal does. - The drakehide body and Wyrmscale body keep off Wyrmholm's heat and halve drake breath. The Wyrmscale shield takes a quarter off breath. - The wyrmfang spear hits from 2 hexes away. It sells for 5,000 coins. - For the wyrmglass staff see Mage gear, and for the wyrmfang axe and pickaxe see tools. Heartsteel, the metal above hexsteel, is in the game's data but needs an ore you can't mine yet."}]},{"slug":"monsters","title":"Monsters","summary":"What's out there, how tough it is, whether it attacks on sight, and what it drops.","section":"Combat","status":"shipped","tags":["monsters","combat","drops","bone-guard","aggressive","night"],"headings":[{"id":"how-monsters-behave","text":"How monsters behave"},{"id":"monster-list","text":"Monster list"},{"id":"inside-the-windwarden-tower","text":"Inside the Windwarden tower"},{"id":"the-delvings","text":"The Delvings"},{"id":"drops","text":"Drops"},{"id":"using-elements","text":"Using elements"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-monsters-behave","text":"- Aggressive monsters attack when you come close. Passive ones only fight back. - A monster chases you until it's 7 hexes from home . Then it gives up, walks back and heals to full. It also gives up if you get well clear, or if it stops gaining on you, as long as you haven't hit it and it can't see you. - Defeated monsters come back after a while. - Loot falls on the ground and stays for 2 minutes. - People of level 12 and up carry more coins the tougher they are, and sometimes a cooked fish. The undead carry coins but no food, and beasts carry no purse at all. - Some monsters only come out at night , others only by day . At dawn or dusk they slip away, unless they're fighting you. - Every monster has a small chance, about 1 in 100, to drop a weapon. - Any monster of level 5 or higher can drop a worn riddle map , 1 time in 120. See riddle-maps."},{"id":"monster-list","text":"For archery, some monsters are armoured (arrows do a quarter less) and some light (a quarter more); the monster card shows the tag. Armoured: Bone Guard sentries, golems, the Hollow warden, sentries and sentinels, the Gate-breaker, the Starsteel champion, deep knights, seal colossi, greyed guards and the Bone Captain. Most bandits, wolfmen, wreckers, raiders and drakes are light. Monster Level HP Max hit Attacks you? Where Sheep 1 3 0 No, and never fights back Fairmeadow's farmstead pens Drowned oarsman 2 4 1 No Saltmere's shore (more at night) Cow 3 6 0 No, and never fights back Fairmeadow's farmstead pens, a few near Kingsferry Bone Guard scout 6 10 1 Within 2 hexes Saltmere, Fairmeadow (night), Mistmarsh Kingsway bandit 8 12 1 Within 2 hexes Fairmeadow and Highmoor (day) Fen toad 10 14 2 Within 1 hex Mistmarsh, near Reedwick Bone Guard sentry 12 18 2 Within 3 hexes Saltmere, Highmoor Bog-drowned 14 20 2 Within 2 hexes Mistmarsh (more on the West Gate road at night) Highwayman 16 22 2 Within 3 hexes Fairmeadow and Highmoor (night) Bone Guard lamplighter 18 24 2 Within 3 hexes Saltmere, Highmoor Moor wolfman 24 34 3 Within 4 hexes Highmoor, in Hollin Wood (night) Greenwood outlaw 24 32 3 Within 3 hexes Greenwood (day) Mire golem 30 48 3 Within 2 hexes Mistmarsh Orc raider 28 38 3 Within 3 hexes Cinderreach Rootbound scout 32 42 3 Within 3 hexes Greenwood Cinder lamplighter 38 48 4 Within 3 hexes Cinderreach Wolfman 40 54 4 Within 4 hexes Greenwood (night) Hollow warden 42 56 4 Within 3 hexes Greenwood, near the Hollow Delver shade 40 50 4 Within 3 hexes Frostpeak Frost sentry 46 62 5 Within 3 hexes Frostpeak Frost troll 48 70 5 Within 3 hexes Frostpeak Heat golem 50 72 5 Within 2 hexes Cinderreach, on the Kiln Rime lamplighter 55 66 5 Within 4 hexes Frostpeak Seal colossus 68 130 7 If you step on its road The Crown Valley, inside each gate Greyed guard 72 100 7 Within 3 hexes The Crown Valley Seal sentinel 80 112 8 Within 3 hexes The Crown Valley, by the Hexhold walls The Bone Captain 90 320 9 Within 1 hex, until he talks to you The bridge, Crown Valley Rust drake 62 88 6 Within 3 hexes Wyrmholm, near the shore Ember drake 70 104 7 Within 3 hexes Wyrmholm, the nests Obsidian drake 80 130 8 Within 4 hexes Wyrmholm, the obsidian ridge Fell ram 12 18 2 Within 1 hex Highmoor, the open moor Heath adder 16 20 2 Within 2 hexes Highmoor, in the heather Reed lurker 18 24 2 Within 2 hexes Mistmarsh, out in the reeds Crag kite 20 24 2 Within 3 hexes Highmoor, the crags and fells Corpse-light 22 22 2 Within 3 hexes Mistmarsh, deeper in Drowned watchman 26 38 3 Within 2 hexes Mistmarsh, deeper in Barrow wight 28 36 3 Within 3 hexes Highmoor, among the cairns (folk say only at night) Mudback 34 56 4 Within 2 hexes Mistmarsh, the deepest bog Tor troll 34 58 4 Within 2 hexes Highmoor, the tors Thornback boar 22 30 3 Within 2 hexes Greenwood, the clearings round Thornwick Ash hound 28 34 3 Within 3 hexes Cinderreach, round Emberfall's edges Greywood stag 30 40 3 Within 3 hexes Greenwood, the Greying Dune viper 32 38 3 Within 3 hexes Cinderreach, the Glass Sands Outlaw captain 36 48 4 Within 3 hexes Greenwood, the Alder fen (day) Slag crawler 36 50 4 Within 2 hexes Cinderreach, the Scorch Snow wolf 36 46 4 Within 4 hexes Frostpeak, the Rimewood Gloam owl 38 44 4 Within 3 hexes Greenwood, the Deepwood (night) Ember wraith 42 52 4 Within 3 hexes Cinderreach, the old workings and the lava (night) Rime ram 42 56 5 Within 2 hexes Frostpeak, the Shoulder's ledges Moss troll 46 72 5 Within 2 hexes Greenwood, the Highwood Raider warchief 46 74 5 Within 3 hexes Cinderreach, by the raiders' drum (day) Cragbear 50 76 5 Within 3 hexes Frostpeak, the Shoulder's crags and the Fells Barrow chief 56 70 5 Within 4 hexes Frostpeak, the Barrows (night) Rimeback giant 62 110 7 Within 3 hexes Frostpeak, the Shoulder (day) The marsh's creatures get tougher the further you go from Reedwick. Inside the Windwarden tower The ruined tower on Highmoor's north moor has its own residents, harder on each floor. See The Windwarden tower. Monster Level HP Max hit Attacks you? Moor reaver 18 24 2 Within 3 hexes Gust wisp 22 22 2 Within 4 hexes Gale hound 28 38 3 Within 4 hexes Hollow vaneguard 32 48 4 Within 3 hexes Vaneguard captain 36 58 4 Within 4 hexes The Delvings Below Kettlepass in Frostpeak. See The Delvings. Monster Level HP Max hit Attacks you? Hollow delver 48 66 5 Within 2 hexes Gnawling 64 84 7 Within 3 hexes On Saltmere, the Bone Guard only pick fights at night."},{"id":"drops","text":"Monster Always Often Sometimes Sheep Bones, raw meat, fleece – – Drowned oarsman Bones Coins, raw shrimp Waters tiles Cow Bones, raw meat, cowhide – – Bone Guard scout Guard bones Coins Raw trout, bronze dagger, tiles Kingsway bandit Bones Coins Shrimp, bronze dagger Bone Guard sentry Guard bones Coins, copper and tin ore Bronze full helm, iron bar, tiles Bog-drowned Bones Coins, Waters tiles Raw trout Highwayman Bones Coins Trout, iron sword Bone Guard lamplighter Guard bones Coins, Flame tiles, iron ore Trout, iron sword, other tiles Moor wolfman Big bones Coins Coal, Winds tiles Fell ram Bones Coins, fleece, raw meat Winds tiles Heath adder – Coins, heather, Winds tiles Leather Reed lurker Bones Coins, willow logs, Roots tiles Willow shortbow, iron dagger Crag kite Bones Coins, feathers, iron ore, Winds tiles – Corpse-light – Coins, Waters tiles, bog myrtle Winds tiles Drowned watchman Bones Coins, Waters tiles, cooked salmon Iron bar, iron full helm, iron mace, Roads tiles Barrow wight Big bones Coins, coal, Winds tiles Stone tiles Mudback Coins Iron ore, raw marsh eel, Stone and Roots tiles Golem pebble Tor troll Coins, big bones Iron ore, coal, Stone and Winds tiles – Thornback boar Bones Coins, raw meat, cowhide Roots tiles Greywood stag Big bones Coins, raw meat, cowhide Roots tiles Outlaw captain Coins, bones Ash logs, iron arrows, cooked salmon Leather body (rare) Gloam owl Bones Coins, feathers Roots tiles Moss troll Coins, big bones Ash logs, Roots tiles Elder logs Ash hound Bones Coins, raw meat Flame tiles Dune viper Bones Coins Firethorn, Flame tiles Slag crawler Big bones Coins, iron ore, coal Gold ore Ember wraith Bones Coins, coal, Flame tiles – Raider warchief Coins, big bones Iron ore, cooked salmon Gold ore, steel warhammer (rare) Snow wolf Bones Coins, raw meat – Rime ram Bones Coins, raw meat Frostsilk Cragbear Raw meat, big bones Coins, cowhide – Barrow chief Guard bones Coins, gold ore, cooked salmon Ice twin tiles, frostsilver sword (rare) Rimeback giant Coins, big bones Troll tooth, frostsilver bar, cooked salmon Frostsilver warhammer (rare) Gust wisp – Coins, feathers, Winds tiles – Gale hound Bones Coins, leather, Winds tiles – Hollow vaneguard – Coins, iron bar, Winds tiles, cooked salmon Steel bar, steel mace Vaneguard captain Coins Steel bar, Winds tiles, cooked salmon Steel warhammer Hollow delver Bones Coins, starstone, coal, cooked salmon Frostsilver pickaxe Gnawling – Coins, duskite, gold ore, big bones – Greenwood outlaw Bones Coins, cooked trout Ash logs, salmon, iron sword Mire golem – Coins, iron ore, Roots tiles Stone tiles, iron platebody Orc raider Bones Coins, cooked salmon Coal, gold ore Rootbound scout Guard bones Coins Iron ore, elder logs Cinder lamplighter Guard bones Coins Coal, gold ore Wolfman Big bones Coins Ash logs, elder logs Hollow warden Guard bones Coins Coal, iron platebody Heat golem – Coins, gold ore Iron bar, iron platebody Delver shade Bones Coins, iron ore Gold ore Frost sentry Guard bones Coins Iron bar, iron platebody Frost troll Bones Coins Frostfin, iron bar, coal Rime lamplighter Guard bones Coins Frostfin, gold ore Seal colossus – Coins, sealstone, hexslate Sealsteel bar Greyed guard Guard bones Coins Greyed slate, sealsteel bar, sealsteel gear Seal sentinel Guard bones Coins Hexstone, hexsteel bar, hexsteel gear The Bone Captain Coins – Hexsteel greatsword Rust drake Dragon bones Coins, drake scales Drakehide, Flame tiles, wyrmglass, wyrmfang (rare) Ember drake Dragon bones Coins, drake scales Drakehide, Flame tiles, wyrmglass, wyrmfang (rare) Obsidian drake Dragon bones Coins, drake scales Wyrmglass, Flame tiles, wyrmfang Every Bone Guard drops Guard bones . See bones for what to do with them."},{"id":"using-elements","text":"Magic hits some monsters harder. Water is strong against fire creatures, earth against bone, and fire against wood and bog creatures. See Hitting and damage."}]},{"slug":"death","title":"Death and the mending house","summary":"Fall in a fight and you wake at the nearest mending house. Your things wait in a grave that never expires.","section":"Combat","status":"shipped","tags":["death","gravestone","mending-house","hard-mode","fees","lost-and-found"],"headings":[{"id":"what-happens-when-you-fall","text":"What happens when you fall"},{"id":"the-fee","text":"The fee"},{"id":"your-grave","text":"Your grave"},{"id":"falling-again-before-you-get-back","text":"Falling again before you get back"},{"id":"when-nothing-is-lost","text":"When nothing is lost"},{"id":"hard-mode","text":"Hard mode"}],"sections":[{"id":"what-happens-when-you-fall","text":"1. \"Oh dear, you are dead!\" The screen fades, and you wake at the mending house of the region you fell in, at full health. If the last bed you slept in, at an inn or at home, is in that region, you wake in that bed instead, for free. See Beds. 2. The mender charges a fee . 3. Everything you were carrying waits for you in a grave near where you fell. The fall is saved straight away, so closing the app doesn't undo it."},{"id":"the-fee","text":"The fee comes from your bag's coins first, then your bank. If you can't cover it, the rest goes on your tab , and half of every coin you earn goes toward it until it's paid off. Region Mending house Fee Saltmere Hattie's spare cot Your first 3 falls are free, then 10 Fairmeadow The Wayside Mending House, Kingsferry 25 Mistmarsh Mother Gloam's hut 60 Highmoor The shepherds' lambing shed 60 Greenwood The Lost Lantern, Thornwick 120 Cinderreach Old Bellows' burn shed, Emberfall 250 Frostpeak The Kettle, Kettlepass 500 The Crown Valley Lantern-keeper Ide's tent, Lantern camp 1,200 Wyrmholm Old Bellows' burn shed, Emberfall 250 Take a room at the inn before a risky trip. Fall in that province and you wake in the bed you paid for, with no fee. Your cottage bed works the same way on Saltmere."},{"id":"your-grave","text":"- Your bag, your worn gear and what's in your sacks go into a grave on the nearest clear hex. If you fall indoors, it's by that building's door. - It never expires. A small headstone on the compass points the way. - Tap the grave to choose: Take everything , or take one thing at a time (a worn piece, or all of one item). Whatever you leave keeps waiting in the grave. Long-press it for Take all in one go. Gear goes back on first, and it's free. - You always keep your tool belt, the sacks themselves, and quest items."},{"id":"falling-again-before-you-get-back","text":"If you fall a second time before reaching your grave, the old grave crumbles and its contents go to that region's mender, in the lost-and-found . You can buy them back for 10 times the region's fee , or the value of the things if that's less. The lost-and-found keeps one lot . Fall a third time before buying it back and that older lot is gone for good."},{"id":"when-nothing-is-lost","text":"Falling on Saltmere before you've finished your first days there costs nothing and leaves no grave: \"Someone found you and kept your things safe.\""},{"id":"hard-mode","text":"Tick Hard mode on the title screen before starting a new game. In Hard mode, falling destroys your bag and worn gear , with no grave. Fees are the same. You still keep your tool belt, what's in your sacks, and quest items. A one-life mode."}]},{"slug":"bones","title":"Bones and the Guard's Rest","summary":"Lay the Bone Guard's bones to rest and each province calms down. Bones also make ash, broth and coins.","section":"Combat","status":"shipped","tags":["bones","bone-guard","guards-rest","blessing","bone-ash","broth"],"headings":[{"id":"kinds-of-bones","text":"Kinds of bones"},{"id":"laying-the-guard-to-rest","text":"Laying the Guard to rest"},{"id":"the-guard-s-blessing","text":"The Guard's Blessing"},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps"},{"id":"the-captain-s-rest","text":"The Captain's Rest"},{"id":"other-uses","text":"Other uses"}],"sections":[{"id":"kinds-of-bones","text":"Bones From Stack in bag? Bones Drowned oarsmen, bandits, highwaymen, bog-drowned Yes Big bones Moor wolfmen No Guard bones Every Bone Guard No Dragon bones Drakes on Wyrmholm (lay them at the Wyrm's Cairn) No"},{"id":"laying-the-guard-to-rest","text":"The Bone Guard are skeletons in old royal armour. Every province has a Guard's Rest : a roll stone with a lantern, where their bones can be laid to rest. Greenwood has two, Rootwatch and Greywatch , and both count toward Greenwood. Cinderreach's is the Kiln road rest , and Frostpeak's is the North Gate rest . - Tap the rest with Guard bones and choose to lay one, five, or all . You kneel and set each one down, and each takes a tick: \"Your watch is ended.\" - Bones count toward the province the rest is in , wherever the bones came from. - Tap the stone to see how far along the province is and how many more bones the next step needs. The Guard's Blessing Lay 5 or more at once and you get the Guard's Blessing : the Bone Guard hit you 5% less often for 10 minutes. A badge shows on screen. Laying more starts the 10 minutes again. Steps As you lay bones, each province moves through five steps: Restless, Uneasy, Settled, At peace and Honoured . Saltmere needs fewer bones than the other provinces. Tap a rest to see where you are. Step Saltmere Other provinces Restless 0 0 Uneasy 12 25 Settled 50 100 At peace 125 250 Honoured 250 500 At Uneasy , the Guard leave you alone if your combat level is 10 or more above theirs. What the later steps bring is for you to find out. The Guard's Rest is introduced by a hidden quest on Saltmere. Keep your ears open for rumours. See Hidden quests."},{"id":"the-captain-s-rest","text":"In the Crown Valley, the Captain's Rest is a memorial to the Crown Watch's officers, where their captains' bones are laid. Where those bones are is for you to find."},{"id":"other-uses","text":"- Bone ash. Burn bones at a forge (Smithing 1): bones make 1 ash, big bones 3, Guard bones 2, dragon bones 6. Ash with hexslate makes extra wardglass for etching. - Fish broth. Cook 1 raw fish with 2 bones or 1 big bones. See cooking. - Selling. Ada Pike in Fairmeadow buys bones for 4 coins each and big bones for 12. - Trading. Someone in Mistmarsh will trade magic tiles for Guard bones. Bone crafting (needles, hooks, beads, dice and charms), and more kinds of bones."}]},{"slug":"bag-and-bank","title":"Bag, bank and coins","summary":"Your 28-slot bag, the shared bank with 200 slots, and where coins come from and go.","section":"Economy","status":"shipped","tags":["bag","bank","coins","inventory","storage"],"headings":[{"id":"your-bag","text":"Your bag"},{"id":"the-bank","text":"The bank"},{"id":"depositing-and-withdrawing","text":"Depositing and withdrawing"},{"id":"brews","text":"Brews"},{"id":"coins","text":"Coins"}],"sections":[{"id":"your-bag","text":"- 28 slots , four across. - Most things take a slot each: one log, one fish, one ore. These stack in a single slot: coins, magic tiles, twine, bone ash and planks . - Tap an item to use it. Food is eaten if you're hurt, and it's the one you tapped that gets eaten. - Hold an item to see its card, with everything you can do with it. The card opens just beside the slot. - Hold and drag an item onto another slot to swap them. - Sort , beside the bag title, packs everything to the front with coins first and like items together. A full bag stops gathering, unless you've made a sack to catch the overflow. See hauling. Anything a quest hands you while your bag is full goes straight to your bank."},{"id":"the-bank","text":"Every bank opens the same storage , so something banked in Saltmere is waiting for you in Kingsferry. It has 200 slots and everything stacks. Bank Where Saltmere village bank The gold-roofed tower in the village Kingsferry bank The gold-roofed tower on the square, Fairmeadow Windle bank chest Windle, Highmoor Reedwick bank barge Moored in Reedwick, Mistmarsh Second bank barge By the marsh pools in Mistmarsh, once it's paid for (see town-works) Depositing and withdrawing - Tap an item to move one; in your bag, it's the slot you tapped that moves. Hold it to choose 1, Half, All or X (type an amount). The choice opens right beside the stack you held. - The bank, post boxes and other storage open as one window with your bag beside them. Close it with the single × . - Deposit all empties your bag, your belt sacks and any bundles or loaded chests (they unpack) in one tap. - The bank has its own Sort button. One tap groups everything by kind (coins, tools, gear, ores and bars, wood, food, herbs and brews, magic, crafting, drops, hauling, curios, keepsakes), lower tiers first. Tap it again and it sorts A to Z instead. - The Pack opened beside a shop or station takes half the screen, like the bank, so both stay in view. Brews Brewed draughts are bottles of 4 sips . In your bag each bottle takes a slot; in the bank they stack. See brewing."},{"id":"coins","text":"Coins are an item in your bag. Shops and the cottage take coins from your bag only; town works also take them from your bank. Ways to earn: - selling at a general store, or filling a notice-board order (shops-and-markets) - your own market stall (trade) - monster drops and quest rewards - riddle-map chests (riddle-maps) - selling bones to Ada Pike: 4 coins a bone, 12 a big bone (bones) Ways to spend: tiles and gear, the Grand Market, Mags the Pedlar, town works, cottage rooms, and Tamsin's cooking service. If you owe a mending house after a fall, half of every coin you earn goes toward what you owe. Coins taken out of your bank or grave don't count. See death."}]},{"slug":"shops-and-markets","title":"Shops and markets","summary":"General stores, keeper shops, the Grand Market, local prices that rise and fall, and daily notice-board orders.","section":"Economy","status":"shipped","tags":["shops","market","prices","notice-board","general-store","grand-market","selling"],"headings":[{"id":"general-stores","text":"General stores"},{"id":"what-things-are-worth","text":"What things are worth"},{"id":"local-prices","text":"Local prices"},{"id":"notice-boards","text":"Notice boards"},{"id":"keeper-shops","text":"Keeper shops"},{"id":"the-grand-market","text":"The Grand Market"}],"sections":[{"id":"general-stores","text":"Every town has one: Saltmere village, Kingsferry, Reedwick and Windle. The general store buys almost anything from your bag. Tap Sell 1 or All , and the store asks you to confirm each sale, with the price, before it goes through. Quest and story items, pages and seals can't be sold . Riddle maps are the exception: you can sell one, but the store warns you first, because a sold map is gone for good. They show dimmed in the store ( \"Can't sell: you'll need this\" ), and no shop, market or stall will take them. What you get depends on the town. Each town wants some things and has plenty of others."},{"id":"what-things-are-worth","text":"Everything has a shop value, shown on its card. Things up to level 30 are cheap; past that, value climbs steeply with level (about 4 times as much at level 60, and 7 times at level 90). Something you make is never worth less than what went into it."},{"id":"local-prices","text":"- Wants: the town pays more than the item's usual value. - Plenty: the town pays only 70%. - After a sale you hear the coins and see how many you sold and your new total. - An up arrow on a sell row means the town pays well for it; a down arrow means it doesn't. - Flooding: every item you sell drops that item's price in that town by 1%, down to half. Prices recover gradually: one sale is forgotten every 36 seconds, so a flooded price is back to normal in about half an hour. Town Pays well for Has plenty of Saltmere village Coal, ash logs, salmon, iron and bronze bars Shrimp, herring, logs Kingsferry Marsh eel, steel and iron bars, trout, gold bars Logs, oak and willow logs, raw shrimp Reedwick Iron, copper and tin ore, coal, oak logs, iron bars Willow logs, marsh eel Windle Willow logs, herring, salmon, trout, steel bars, marsh eel Oak logs, logs, coal Reedwick pays twice the usual value for iron ore. Carrying ore to the marsh is worth the trip."},{"id":"notice-boards","text":"Open a general store and tap Notice board . Each town keeps 3 orders on its board. Each one asks for a set amount of one item and always pays noticeably more than selling the same goods in that town (at least 1.3 times the town's own price). An order stays up for 2 in-game days (3 for big ones paying 700 coins or more), and each order shows its days left. When one is filled or runs out, a new one goes up at dawn, one slot at a time. Tap Deliver with the full amount in your bag. You get: - the coins - Trade XP (0.1 per coin) - XP in the skill that made the goods (5% of the making XP) - a 15% chance of 1 to 3 bonus magic tiles In Windle and Emberfall the board can also carry a drake bounty, the Joint Notice . See The Joint Notice. The board also tells you which town Mags the Pedlar is in today. Every market also posts one big warrant a day, with a delivery crate by the store. See market-warrants."},{"id":"keeper-shops","text":"Some people sell things directly. Talk to them, or tap their stall, and use Buy 1 or Buy 10 . Shop Where Sells Ida's stall Saltmere Flame and Waters tiles Silas's curios Kingsferry All six tiles, woollen and dyed wool robes, mage capes, the etcher's set, House capes, worn riddle maps Mother Gloam's chest Mistmarsh Reedsilk robes How keeper prices work: - They charge twice the item's value. - Each one you buy puts the price up 5% , up to double. - Stock comes back one at a time, and the price eases back down as it does."},{"id":"the-grand-market","text":"Kingsferry's Grand Market has three empty stalls. Once they're reopened (a town work), a fishmonger , a timber merchant and a smith's stall sell raw fish, logs, planks, ore and bars. Stall Sells Fishmonger Raw shrimp, herring, trout, salmon Timber merchant Logs, oak logs, willow logs, planks Smith's stall Copper, tin and iron ore, coal, bronze and iron bars Their prices are set so you can't buy there and resell at a profit. They're for when you'd rather pay than gather. Reopening the market also lets you rent your own stall . See trade."}]},{"slug":"hauling","title":"Sacks, chests and bundles","summary":"Carry more than 28 things. Belt sacks catch overflow, field chests hold your haul on site, bundles pack many items into one slot, and the King's Post and its rooks carry loads to the bank.","section":"Economy","status":"partial","tags":["hauling","sacks","field-chests","bundles","crafting","storage","post","runners","post-boxes","rooks"],"headings":[{"id":"gathering-sacks","text":"Gathering sacks"},{"id":"field-chests","text":"Field chests"},{"id":"bundles","text":"Bundles"},{"id":"the-king-s-post","text":"The King's Post"},{"id":"calling-a-runner","text":"Calling a runner"},{"id":"post-boxes","text":"Post boxes"},{"id":"nails-and-signs","text":"Nails and signs"},{"id":"post-rooks","text":"Post rooks"}],"sections":[{"id":"gathering-sacks","text":"Make sacks at a workbench with Crafting. A sack hangs on your tool belt and takes no bag slot; tap it in your bag to hang it. - Each sack holds one kind of thing: a log sling for logs, a fish creel for raw fish, an ore sack for ore. - When your bag is full, what you gather goes into the sack on its own. - Fill on the Pack tab moves matching items from your bag into the sack. - Deposit all at a bank empties your sacks too. - A bigger sack replaces a smaller one once the old one is empty. Size Holds Crafting (sling / creel / ore sack) Made from Small 14 5 / 8 / 10 4 twine, 1 bronze bar Regular 28 25 / 28 / 30 6 twine, 1 iron bar Large 42 45 / 48 / 50 8 twine, 1 steel bar Twine is made at a workbench: 1 log makes 3."},{"id":"field-chests","text":"A field chest is a big box you set down where you're working. 1. Craft one, then tap Set down on its card while standing by an open hex. Chests can't go in towns, on roads or on travel points. 2. Tap the chest to open it. Stow all empties your bag into it. Everything stacks inside. 3. Pick it up when you're done. An empty chest is just a chest; a full one becomes a loaded chest that holds the whole load in one bag slot . 4. Deposit the loaded chest at any bank to unpack it. A loaded chest halves your walking speed , except the light willow hamper. Chest Crafting Materials (plus 4 twine) Holds Driftwood crate 15 8 logs, 2 bronze bars 40 Oak chest 22 8 oak logs, 2 iron bars 60 Willow hamper (no slowdown) 30 10 willow logs, 2 steel bars 80 Ash strongchest 38 8 ash logs, 3 steel bars 110 Hexwood chest 80 8 hexwood logs, 4 sealsteel bars 400 You can have one chest out at a time, two from Crafting 50, three from Crafting 80."},{"id":"bundles","text":"From Crafting 10, tie raw logs, fish or ore with 1 twine at a workbench into a bundle that takes one bag slot . Bundling gives no XP. Crafting Items per bundle 10 5 30 10 50 15 70 20 90 25 Bundles unpack at the bank, or you can Unpack one from its card. Anything that won't fit stays packed, so nothing's lost."},{"id":"the-king-s-post","text":"The Roadwardens once ran a post along the Kingsway. Old Hob in Kingsferry still has the horn. Help him with The Last Toll and the Kingsferry post house opens again, and the post horn goes on your tool belt. Calling a runner Tap the post horn in your Pack and a Kingsway runner comes to you. Choose what he carries: - My bag: your resources (logs, fish, ore and the like). Tools, gear, belt kit and quest things stay with you. Bundles go too. - My sacks: whatever your gathering sacks hold. - To today's warrant: a runner or rook can deliver straight to a market's warrant crate. See market-warrants. - My ore to the forge: your ore and coal go to the village forges' shared ore bin instead of the bank. See The village forges. Rooks can carry ore there too. The load lands in your bank about a minute later . If the bank is full, it waits at the post house until you make room. Fee About a tenth of the load's shop value, at least 5 coins, paid from your bag Farther away Half again for each province between you and the nearest open post house How often Once every 5 minutes Runners keep to the roads. They won't come underground , indoors , out to Saltmere , or near the gates in the Crownwall. A runner also needs an open post house somewhere he can reach. Every province on the Kingsway had a post house once. Ask around in each town. Post boxes Roadwarden post boxes stand on Saltmere and along the mainland woods and roads. They are deposit only : whatever goes in the slot turns up in your bank, free. Not every box still works. Nails and signs - Iron nails: forge, Smithing 10, 1 iron bar makes 10 (12.5 XP). - Signboard: workbench, Crafting 8, 2 planks and 5 iron nails (40 XP)."},{"id":"post-rooks","text":"Ida on Saltmere keeps rooks, and she hears everything. Talk to her at her stall to start Ida's Rumour Mill; it ends with a rook whistle on your tool belt. 1. Tap the whistle in your Pack. 2. Pick one stack : one kind of thing, up to 28. 3. The rook takes your cheapest fish as its fee (raw or cooked) and flies the stack to your bank about half a minute later. - Rooks fly from anywhere outdoors , Saltmere and the gate stones included. Not indoors, and never underground. - Each rook rests 3 minutes after a flight. You start with one rook, get a second at Crafting 30 and a third at Crafting 60 . - No fish, no flight. If the stack you're sending is fish, the rook is paid with a different fish. - Ida's rooks roost on the Saltmere mill . Runner Rook Carries Your whole bag or your sacks One stack, up to 28 Fee Coins One fish Where Mainland roads, away from the gates Anywhere outdoors Rest 5 minutes 3 minutes a rook Bigger chests and sacks from higher-tier materials."}]},{"slug":"cottage","title":"The Driftwood Cottage","summary":"Your home on Saltmere. Fix it up room by room for a bed, a hearth and a larder.","section":"Economy","status":"shipped","tags":["cottage","home","housing","saltmere","larder","sleep"],"headings":[{"id":"getting-the-cottage","text":"Getting the cottage"},{"id":"rooms","text":"Rooms"}],"sections":[{"id":"getting-the-cottage","text":"Once you've finished your first days on Saltmere, Hattie lets you the Driftwood Cottage for free. It's a wreck, so you'll be paying a carpenter to fix it up. Tap the cottage to see inside, the rooms you can pay for, and buttons for Sleep , Cook and Larder once you have them. Rooms are paid for with coins from your bag."},{"id":"rooms","text":"Patch the roof comes first. After that, buy the rest in any order. Room Cost What it gives Patch the roof 500 A bed. Sleep at night to skip to morning and heal to full. Sleeping here once makes it where you wake after a fall on Saltmere, for free. You can't sleep while something is fighting or chasing you close behind. Hearth and range 2,500 Cook at home, just like a fire. Larder chest 5,000 Larder : 50 slots where everything stacks. It opens only at the cottage, separate from the bank. Trophy wall 15,000 A wall for the things you're proudest of. Once the roof is patched, Market Seals buy furnishings too: a framed warrant, a rug, a rocking chair, a floor lantern, a dresser and a banner by the door. See Spending Market Seals. More rooms (a workshop, a map room, a garden, a hall for your curios and a second floor), trophies to hang, and a larder you can reach from the bank."}]},{"slug":"town-works","title":"Town works","summary":"Pay to fix up the towns. Each repair changes the town for good and opens a lasting perk.","section":"Economy","status":"partial","tags":["town-works","coins","perks","patron","saltmere","kingsferry","reedwick","windle","thornwick","emberfall","kettlepass"],"headings":[{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How it works"},{"id":"works-you-can-fund-now","text":"Works you can fund now"},{"id":"saltmere","text":"Saltmere"},{"id":"kingsferry","text":"Kingsferry"},{"id":"reedwick","text":"Reedwick"},{"id":"windle","text":"Windle"},{"id":"thornwick","text":"Thornwick"},{"id":"emberfall","text":"Emberfall"},{"id":"kettlepass","text":"Kettlepass"},{"id":"late-works","text":"Late works"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-it-works","text":"Some townsfolk want something fixed. Talk to them and pick Fund . Each work costs coins plus materials, taken from your bag first, then your bank . When it's done, the town changes on the map and a perk opens for good. Some works only appear after another work, or after you've made progress in the town's story. Fund every work in a region to earn its Patron title."},{"id":"works-you-can-fund-now","text":"Saltmere Work Cost Perk Mend the Salted Eel's second hearth 1,500 coins, 5 bronze bars, 10 oak logs Tamsin cooks the raw fish in your bag for 1 coin each. No XP, but nothing burns. Only fish your Cooking level could cook. Relight the Gullwatch lamp 3,000 coins, 20 logs, 10 willow logs Two quay fishing spots bite 1.5 times as often after dark, and herring join the catch. Opens after progress in a local story. Re-roof the Tide chapel 4,000 coins, 25 oak logs, 30 hexslate Rest on the chapel bench to heal to full, once per in-game day. Raise the stove-in boat 6,000 coins, 30 oak logs, 10 bronze bars, 10 willow logs Gil rows you out once a day for sardines and herring, with a small chance of a Waters tile. Fund all four for Patron of Saltmere . Kingsferry Work Cost Perk Reopen three Grand Market stalls 25,000 coins, 30 planks, 30 oak logs The fishmonger, timber merchant and smith's stall open. See The Grand Market. Rent a Grand Market stall 10,000 coins Your own stall. See trade. Restore the guild hall 120,000 coins, 80 planks, 30 steel bars, 10 gold bars The Kingsferry notice board gets a fourth order slot, and the Grand Market starts posting Market Warrants. Opens after the Grand Market stalls. Reedwick Work Cost Perk Moor a second bank barge 30,000 coins, 40 oak logs, 15 iron bars A second bank right by the eel beds. Build Marta's eel smokery 350,000 coins, 300 willow logs, 100 iron bars, 150 marsh eel A smoke-hole roof on the stilt house, racks of eels under the eaves, and Marta's thanks. Windle Work Cost Perk Raise the shearing barn 330,000 coins, 400 oak logs, 80 iron bars, 300 hexslate Your cottage gets Windle's long shearing barn, with fleeces drying on the rail. Thornwick Work Cost Perk Re-roof the foresters' lodge 45,000 coins, 60 oak logs, 30 ash logs Thornwick pays 30% more for logs of every kind, and three foresters, Rowan, Ash and Bree, come home. Open the bowyer's loft 900,000 coins, 300 ash logs, 60 elder logs, 60 iron bars A bowyer's loft on your cottage, with bows and staves under the eaves. Emberfall Work Cost Perk Re-hang the Emberfall bell 350,000 coins, 200 iron bars, 20 gold bars, 150 oak logs The bell rings the hours. Ask Bellows, by the bell, how long until midnight, when every Market Warrant changes. Gild the Anvil Council 2,000,000 coins, 250 gold bars, 200 steel bars, 500 coal Gold round the Council hall door, a gold anvil on its roof, and the title Gilder of Emberfall . Kettlepass Work Cost Perk Open the fur trader's 900,000 coins, 300 oak logs, 100 steel bars, 100 frostfin A fur trader's awning on the townhouse, a fur roll by the door and a stove going."},{"id":"late-works","text":"The biggest works of all are for players deep into the game, and cost a fortune. More works in every town (a bridge, a barber, lodgings, a glider launch, a dye shed, a bathhouse and more), perks for the big works above, and Patron titles for every region."}]},{"slug":"pedlar","title":"Mags the Pedlar","summary":"A wandering pedlar who sets up in a different town each day, with four rare things for sale.","section":"Economy","status":"shipped","tags":["pedlar","mags","curios","ledger","shops","roads-tiles","riddle-maps"],"headings":[{"id":"finding-her","text":"Finding her"},{"id":"what-she-sells","text":"What she sells"}],"sections":[{"id":"finding-her","text":"Mags travels with a cart. She turns up once you've finished your first days on Saltmere. - Each in-game day (20 real minutes), she stands near the general store in one town . - She only visits towns you've been to , in order: Saltmere village, Kingsferry, Reedwick, Windle, Thornwick, Emberfall, Kettlepass. - Not sure where she is? Every notice board says which town she's in today."},{"id":"what-she-sells","text":"Talk to her and choose What have you got? - Four things a day , one of each, at a fixed price. - What she brings depends on the regions you've reached. - Miss something and it may come back another day. Her wares include curios , rare keepsakes from around the provinces that only she sells, plus bundles of Roads tiles for magic and worn riddle maps (riddle-maps). Curios you buy go into your Ledger . Curios cost thousands of coins. Fill a few notice-board orders before you go looking for her. More curios from provinces not built yet, plus dyes, hats, lanterns and cottage furniture."}]},{"slug":"riddle-maps","title":"Riddle maps","summary":"Treasure maps that send you from place to place, solving one riddle at a time, to a chest at the end.","section":"Economy","status":"partial","tags":["riddle-maps","treasure","puzzles","coins","digging"],"headings":[{"id":"getting-a-map","text":"Getting a map"},{"id":"reading-it","text":"Reading it"},{"id":"rules","text":"Rules"},{"id":"the-chest","text":"The chest"}],"sections":[{"id":"getting-a-map","text":"- Buy one from Silas Quill in Kingsferry or from Mags the Pedlar, 5,000 coins each. - Find one. Any monster of combat level 5 or higher has a 1 in 120 chance to drop a worn riddle map ."},{"id":"reading-it","text":"Tap the map and Read it. It gives you a chain of riddles, using only places you've already been . If none fit yet, hold on to it and travel more. Each step is a single riddle. Work out where it means and: - Dig : stand on the right hex and tap Dig on the map's card. The map is your spade, so you don't need a tool. - Search : tap the right landmark from a hex next to it. Some steps only work at dawn, dusk or night . Watch the time dial."},{"id":"rules","text":"- You work one map at a time . Its card shows the chain you're on. - Keep the map in your bag. You can't drop one by accident. You can sell it (the store warns you first), but then it and your progress are gone for good. - Worn maps have 3 steps ."},{"id":"the-chest","text":"The last step digs up a chest, and the map is used up. A worn chest holds 1,500 to 6,000 coins plus two extra rewards, such as raw sardines or Roads tiles. Anything that doesn't fit lands at your feet. Sealed maps (4 to 5 steps) and Royal maps (6 steps) with bigger chests. Their riddles cross provinces that aren't built yet. Dyes, hats and furniture as chest rewards."}]},{"slug":"market-warrants","title":"Market Warrants","summary":"One big order a day at every market. Fill it for about two and a half times shop value, Trade XP and a Market Seal.","section":"Economy","status":"shipped","tags":["warrants","market","orders","trade","market-seals","cottage","daily","coins"],"headings":[{"id":"how-warrants-work","text":"How warrants work"},{"id":"the-markets","text":"The markets"},{"id":"the-market-clerks-chest","text":"The Market Clerks' chest"},{"id":"spending-market-seals","text":"Spending Market Seals"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-warrants-work","text":"Each of the eight markets keeps one warrant up at a time: a big order for one thing, picked to suit your skill levels. The warrant sheet hangs on the general store , with a delivery crate beside it. (At the Grand Market it's the guild hall, once the hall is restored. See town-works.) 1. Tap the crate to see the order: what, how many, what it pays and how many days are left. 2. Deliver from your bag, a bit at a time if you like. A Kingsway runner or post rook can deliver straight to the crate too. 3. Once it's filled, the crate is nailed shut and the sheet stamped FILLED . The pay goes to your bank . A warrant pays about 2.5 times shop value (2.2 times for things up to level 30), plus Trade XP (0.1 per coin), XP in the skill that made the goods (10% of the making XP) and a Market Seal . It's sized to take about fifty minutes. A warrant stays up for 3 real days . Fill it and a new one goes up the next day. If it runs out part-done, you're still paid for what you delivered, with no penalty. Townsfolk post these orders, so you'll see familiar names on them. Brannoc and the other smiths post their smithing orders this way. Fine pieces from a village forge count. See The village forges."},{"id":"the-markets","text":"Market Buys Saltmere village Fish off the island, and island iron Kingsferry Farm, river and mill goods The Grand Market (Kingsferry guild hall) Fine goods from every province, for the Kingsway houses Reedwick Eels, willow and fen fish Windle Moor oak, iron and the bars made from them Thornwick Timber, planks and bows Emberfall Smithed goods, gold and ember fish Kettlepass Iron, starsteel and deep fish"},{"id":"the-market-clerks-chest","text":"Fill any 5 warrants in 7 days and the clerks send you a chest: 25,000 coins and 3 Market Seals . You can earn it at most once a week."},{"id":"spending-market-seals","text":"Tap a delivery crate and choose Spend Market Seals . Everything here is for your cottage, so patch the cottage roof first . Piece Seals Your first warrant, framed 2 The Eight Markets rug 4 A rocking chair 6 A floor lantern 8 The market dresser 10 The market banner (by your door) 12"}]},{"slug":"wardlands","title":"The Wardlands","summary":"The world of HexSeal as the islanders tell it. Just enough to get your bearings; the rest is yours to uncover.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["world","lore","wardlands","provinces"],"headings":[{"id":"what-the-islanders-will-tell-you","text":"What the islanders will tell you"},{"id":"the-provinces","text":"The provinces"},{"id":"about-this-wiki-and-spoilers","text":"About this wiki and spoilers"}],"sections":[{"id":"what-the-islanders-will-tell-you","text":"You wake on a grey beach on Saltmere Isle , a windy fishing island off the coast of the mainland, with no idea how you got there. Ask around and you'll hear about the Wardlands : six provinces in a ring, joined by an old royal road called the Kingsway . At the centre of the ring is a wall of mountains, the Crownwall , and behind it a keep with six walls, the Hexhold . Its gates have been shut for as long as anyone can remember. Lately, skeletons in old royal armour have started walking out of the ruins. Nobody on the island agrees on why. HexSeal's story is told in pieces, by different people, and not everyone tells it the same way. Talk to everyone, and read the signposts."},{"id":"the-provinces","text":"Province In the game Fairmeadow Yes Mistmarsh Yes Highmoor Yes Greenwood Yes Cinderreach Yes Frostpeak Yes Saltmere Isle, where you start, isn't one of the six. Inside the Crownwall is the Crown Valley."},{"id":"about-this-wiki-and-spoilers","text":"This wiki explains how the game works. It keeps the story for the game to tell. - Never here: story twists and secrets, quest solutions, riddle answers, spell recipes and hidden places. - Behind a tap: things you'd normally discover by playing, such as the spells waiting in your spellbook. They show as a dashed Spoiler box that says what it reveals, and search never shows what's inside. - Open: how things work, and anything the game shows you up front. Names of places and people are working names and may change."}]},{"slug":"saltmere","title":"Saltmere Isle","summary":"The windy island where you wake up. A fishing village, a quay, woods, a quarry, and an old fort on the headland.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["saltmere","region","starting-area","village"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"people","text":"People"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"leaving-the-island","text":"Leaving the island"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"A small island of beach, meadow and woodland. Good for levels 1 to 10. Places: the village, Eel Quay, Wreckers' Strand, Gullwood, Heron Cove, Tinker's Cut (a quarry), Gull Downs, Crownwake Bay and Gullwatch Point."},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Woodcutting Trees, oaks and willows Fishing The sea and a creek. Ask Gaffer Moss what bites. Mining Copper, tin, iron and hexslate Smithing The forge Cooking The hearth inside the Salted Eel, and the Quay campfire Crafting The workbench Etching The Gullwatch shrine , a small wayside Waters shrine set into the old fort wall, where most new players etch their first Waters tiles Brewing Saltwort, and Hattie's salve still by her cottage Services: a bank (the gold-roofed tower), a general store, Ida's stall (tiles), the Salted Eel inn (a bed up the stairs for 5 coins), a chapel, and a waystone. Your home: once you've finished your first days here, the Driftwood Cottage is yours."},{"id":"people","text":"Hattie (beachcomber), Wenna Gale (harbourmaster), Brannoc (smith), Pell (shipwright), Gaffer Moss (old fisher), Old Tamsin (keeps the Salted Eel), Ida Rook (market stall, rooks, and the island's gossip), Maudie (the bank clerk), and a lamplighter you'll only meet at night."},{"id":"danger","text":"- Drowned oarsmen shamble on the strand. They're weak and won't attack you. - The Bone Guard hold the old fort at Gullwatch Point. They keep to themselves by day. The signpost's advice: \"Go by day.\" The keep inside has a door that stays shut to strangers. See bones. Mending house: Hattie's spare cot. Your first three falls here are free."},{"id":"leaving-the-island","text":"The ferry from Eel Quay goes to Kingsferry in Fairmeadow, but it needs building first. Pell will tell you what she needs. See travel."}]},{"slug":"fairmeadow","title":"Fairmeadow","summary":"Farmland on the river Brin and the trading port of Kingsferry, home of the Grand Market.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["fairmeadow","kingsferry","region","grand-market","roads"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"people","text":"People"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Roads out"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"Rolling farmland around the port town of Kingsferry , with 18 farmsteads in the fields and gentle terraces rising from the harbour to the South Gate. Four bridges cross the river Brin. It's the biggest province on the mainland. Good for levels 5 to 25. Places: Kingsferry and its cobbled market square, the farms, the foothills, the Old Mill, Spire Hill (an old ruin on top you can see from far off), the tollhouse ruins and the South Gate."},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Woodcutting Adds ash trees (level 30), found only here Fishing The sea, a river mouth, and five spots on the Brin itself, fished from the bank. Ask Fen Barrow what bites. Mining Copper, tin, iron, coal and hexslate, most of it at the foothills quarry, up on a terrace with four ramps along its length Smithing, Crafting, Cooking Forge, workbench and campfire Etching A Warden statue for Roads tiles Brewing Meadowsweet, and the Wayside apothecary Services: a bank, a general store, Silas Quill's curio shop (tiles, robes, riddle maps), a tavern (bed for 5 coins), the docks, a lighthouse, the old post house (ask Old Hob) and a Roadwarden post box on the Spire Hill road. The Old Mill grinds wheat and sells baking things: flour, jugs, eggs, milk, apples and raw meat, plus leather and tallow for archer's armour. Flax grows in the fields, and the farmsteads have spinning wheels and hen runs (for feathers). Wheat grows in the fields and bread ovens stand in the farmsteads. See Baking. The Grand Market: three stalls and a place for your own, once they're fixed up. See The Grand Market and trade. Livestock: sheep and cows graze in fenced pens by the farmsteads, with a few strays near Kingsferry. They never fight back, and they give raw meat, fleece and cowhide. Waystones: the Old Mill and the South Gate."},{"id":"people","text":"Ferryman Odo, Old Hob (the tollkeeper), Silas Quill (curio dealer), Ada Pike (runs the Old Mill), Fen Barrow (a barge-master with a broken barge), Lorna Wick (lighthouse keeper), Aggie Brand (the smith), Nell, a mender who walks the Kingsway at dusk, and Prue Crock , a potter across the Brin from the watermill, who teaches pottery."},{"id":"danger","text":"Kingsway bandits by day, highwaymen by night, and Bone Guard scouts in the foothills after dark. See monsters. Kingsferry folk say the old Guard still keep the gate , up on the South Gate terrace. Go carefully. Mending house: the Wayside Mending House, behind the Kingsferry tavern. Fee 25. Guard's Rest: the Tollhouse rest , by the tollhouse ruins. See bones."},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Way Leads to Ferry to Saltmere (by day) Saltmere Isle Kingsway west Mistmarsh. \"Travellers 10+ advised.\" Kingsway east Highmoor. \"Travellers 15+ advised.\" The Crown Valley's South Gate (sealed) The Crown Valley, once it opens"}]},{"slug":"mistmarsh","title":"Mistmarsh","summary":"A wide, foggy marsh of reed beds, bog and black meres around the stilt village of Reedwick.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["mistmarsh","reedwick","region","marsh","waters","willow-carr","meres","bog-iron","eels","fen-toad"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"people","text":"People"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Roads out"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"A big, foggy marsh, about the size of Fairmeadow, with plenty of open water and bog. Reedwick sits on stilts at its heart, and its houses thin out into the reeds the further you go. Good for levels 10 to 35. Places: Reedwick, the Bell, Willow Carr, the Meres (a boardwalk causeway across the water), the foothills and the West Gate. Somewhere out in the marsh, the old folk say, an older village went under."},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Fishing Marsh pools, more eel pools, a pond, a river mouth and the coast. Ask Marta Eelwife what bites. Woodcutting Plenty of willows, oaks and wet-footed alders Mining Tin, hexslate, and bog iron where the mere water seeps out. It mines like any iron. Crafting, Cooking Workbench and campfire Etching A Warden statue for Waters tiles Brewing Bog myrtle, and Mother Gloam's cauldron There's no forge here. Services: a bank barge , a general store, the Leaky Eel, and Mother Gloam's chest of reedsilk robes and her fever tonic. Waystones: Reedwick and the West Gate."},{"id":"people","text":"Tobin Reed (reed-cutter and unofficial mayor), Marta Eelwife (sells eels), and Mother Gloam (the bog witch)."},{"id":"danger","text":"Fen toads sit near the village. Further out you'll meet bog-drowned, mire golems and Bone Guard scouts, and the deeper you go into the marsh, the tougher its creatures get. See monsters. After dark , bog-drowned gather on the road to the West Gate. Bog-drowned and mire golems can give you marsh fever . See Marsh fever for the cures. Mending house: Mother Gloam's hut. Fee 60. At night the general store and Marta's eel trade close. Windows, the bank barge and the eel pools glow, and the Drowned Bell rings at midnight."},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Way Leads to Kingsway east to Fairmeadow Fairmeadow Kingsway north Greenwood The Crown Valley's West Gate (sealed) The Crown Valley, once it opens"}]},{"slug":"highmoor","title":"Highmoor","summary":"Wide, windy moorland, fells, tarns and windmills around the hill town of Windle.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["highmoor","windle","region","moor","winds","fells","tarns","wind","windwarden-tower","tower"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"the-windwarden-tower","text":"The Windwarden tower"},{"id":"people","text":"People"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Roads out"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"Wide high moorland around Windle , a shepherds' hill town, with windmills on the ridges. Good for levels 10 to 35. It's windy up here: gusts sweep across the sky in bursts, and the windmill sails spin faster when a gust comes through. Grass, heather and trees sway with it. For a stiller moor, turn on Calmer effects in Settings. Places: Windle, the Fells, moorland tarns, the Sixstones (a ring of standing stones), Hollin Wood, the foothills and the East Gate. A ruined tower, the Windwarden tower , stands on the north moor. See The Windwarden tower."},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Woodcutting Lots of trees and oaks Mining Iron, a coal seam, tin and hexslate Fishing The sea and the river. Ask Tib Beck what bites. Smithing, Crafting, Cooking Forge, workbench and campfire Etching A Warden statue for Winds tiles Brewing Heather, and the peat still Services: a bank chest, a general store, the Windy Ewe inn, windmills, a glider tower, a spinning wheel and the archery butts (straw targets and a shop). Flax grows in the fields. Waystones: Windle and the East Gate."},{"id":"the-windwarden-tower","text":"A track off the Kingsway leads up to a ruined tower on the north moor. Inside are four floors , each harder than the last. Words are carved into the walls, and they're worth reading closely. Folk in Windle say something penned up on the roof still howls, and that whoever quiets it will find a glide home waiting. If you fall in the tower, you wake at Bryn's shed in Windle."},{"id":"people","text":"Wren (the glider-maker's daughter), Bryn Hollis (head of the shepherd clans), Morwen Hollis (the smith), Old Corra, and Captain Hale Yarrow , who runs the archery butts and starts First Feather . See archery."},{"id":"danger","text":"Moorland creatures roam the open moor: fell rams , heath adders , crag kites and tor trolls , and people say a barrow wight walks among the cairns at night. Bandits by day, a highwayman by night, the Bone Guard, and after dark a moor wolfman (level 24) prowling Hollin Wood that attacks from 4 hexes away. Heed the north signpost. See monsters. At night the general store closes. Mending house: the shepherds' lambing shed. Fee 60."},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Way Leads to Kingsway south to Fairmeadow Fairmeadow Kingsway north Cinderreach The Crown Valley's East Gate (sealed) The Crown Valley, once it opens"}]},{"slug":"travel","title":"Getting around","summary":"Walking between regions, the ferry, waystones, and teleport spells.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["travel","ferry","waystones","teleport","kingsway"],"headings":[{"id":"walking","text":"Walking"},{"id":"gliders","text":"Gliders"},{"id":"the-ferry","text":"The ferry"},{"id":"waystones","text":"Waystones"},{"id":"teleports","text":"Teleports"}],"sections":[{"id":"walking","text":"Each region is its own map. Walk to an exit at its edge to travel on; the game shows \"You set off for …\" and loads the next region. Long-press an exit that won't open to see why: - \"The road beyond is not yet passable.\" The region beyond isn't in the game yet. - \"Sealed. It won't budge.\" One of the Crownwall's gates, not open to you yet. See crown-valley. From To How (as the exit is labelled) Saltmere Fairmeadow Ferry from Eel Quay to Kingsferry Fairmeadow Mistmarsh Kingsway west Fairmeadow Highmoor Kingsway east Mistmarsh Greenwood Kingsway north Highmoor Cinderreach Kingsway north Greenwood Frostpeak The Cold Road east to Frostpeak Cinderreach Frostpeak The Cold Road west to Frostpeak, or the mine cart (by day) Any province The Crown Valley Its Crownwall gate, marked \"(sealed)\" until it opens"},{"id":"the-ferry","text":"The ferry between Saltmere and Kingsferry sails by day only . On Saltmere it has to be built first: Pell needs 25 oak logs and 10 bronze bars . You can bring them over several trips, and she tells you how far along you are."},{"id":"waystones","text":"Most towns have a waystone . Touch one and \"it hums, and you feel it remember you.\" A waystone you've touched is a place your teleport spells can take you."},{"id":"teleports","text":"Spell Magic Takes you to Home Tide 1 Saltmere, free, once every 30 minutes Kingsferry Teleport 12 The Old Mill waystone, Fairmeadow Reedwick Teleport 20 Reedwick, Mistmarsh Windle Teleport 22 Windle, Highmoor Thornwick Teleport 32 Thornwick, Greenwood Emberfall Teleport 38 Emberfall, Cinderreach Kettlepass Teleport 45 Kettlepass, Frostpeak Waymark, Return to Waymark 55 Anywhere you set your mark Crownwall Gate 68 Inside any Crownwall gate you've walked through Hexhold Teleport 90 The Hexhold's door, in the Crown Valley See magic for the tiles each one needs. Walking straight from one waystone to another, and gliders in Highmoor."}]},{"slug":"greenwood","title":"Greenwood","summary":"A big old forest around the foresters' town of Thornwick: the Deepwood, Wendmere, the Highwood ridge, the Alder fen and the grey wood by the gate.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["greenwood","thornwick","region","forest","roots","elderwood","deepwood","wendmere","highwood","charwood","greying","alder fen"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"around-the-wood","text":"Around the wood"},{"id":"people","text":"People"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Roads out"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"An old, deep forest around Thornwick , a foresters' town on the river Wend. Good for levels 20 to 50, and the further you walk from Thornwick, the tougher it gets. Places: Thornwick, the Elder Tree, the Deepwood, Wendmere, the Highwood, the Charwood, the Greying, the Alder fen, the Hollow and the North-West Gate. Getting here: take the Kingsway north out of Mistmarsh. You come in at the south edge and follow the road up through the Alder fen to Thornwick."},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Woodcutting Elderwood trees (level 60) for elder logs, with more out in the Deepwood. Ash groves (30), willows round the fen and Wendmere, and oaks Fishing The river and a lily pond, still water at Wendmere and the fen pools, and shrimp and herring off the west coast. Ask Old Wade at the weir. Mining Iron and hexslate up on the Highwood, and hexslate near town Crafting, Cooking Workbench and campfire Etching The Warden of Roots statue, for Roots tiles (Etching 25) Brewing Elderflower, greyleaf in the Hollow, and the root kettle There's no forge here. Services: a bank in a green tower, a general store, the Lost Lantern inn, Dunstan's lumbermill, and a Roadwarden post box (deposit only: whatever you drop in turns up in your bank). At night the general store and the mill shut. Everyone's at the Lost Lantern. Waystone: Thornwick, and another near the North-West Gate."},{"id":"around-the-wood","text":"Area What's there The Deepwood The oldest, thickest wood, north of town. More elderwood, and a foresters' camp at the end of the Deepwood track. Gloam owls hunt here after dark. Wendmere A long black lake under the north hills, with still-water fishing. Thornwick folk say the Wend runs under the hill from here and comes up again at the lily pond. The Highwood A wooded ridge in the north-east with iron and hexslate in the rocks, and moss trolls asleep among the trees. The Highwood track climbs to an old lookout on Owl Knoll, with a view over the whole forest. The Charwood The charcoal burners' clearing on the Cold Road, where turf clamps smoke for days. The Greying The wood between Thornwick and the gate, going greyer the closer you get. Grey stags walk there. The Alder fen Pools and willows in the south, with the eel-catchers' huts. Outlaws camp near the coast. Hunters have built stands up in the trees here and there. Climb one for a look round."},{"id":"people","text":"Hesper Vane (head forester), Granny Burl (reads the bark of the Elder Tree), Kip Lantern (keeps the lantern-owls), Dunstan Oakes (runs the mill) Abel Thorne (one of Dunstan's woodcutters), and Ossie Nock , a bowyer whose bench sells bows and fletching supplies. See archery. Hesper gives this region's map quest, The Forester's Count . See maps."},{"id":"danger","text":"This is a step up from the south. Outlaws work the fen and the Kingsway by day, boars root in the clearings, the Bone Guard walk the deep wood, and at night something else comes out. See monsters. Mending house: the Lost Lantern. Fee 120. Kip's lantern-owls find whoever falls in the wood. Guard's Rests: two, Rootwatch and Greywatch . See bones."},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Way Leads to Kingsway south, through the Alder fen, to Mistmarsh Mistmarsh The Cold Road east, past the Charwood, to Frostpeak Frostpeak. \"Travellers 35+ advised.\" The Greying road south-east The Hollow and the North-West Gate The Crown Valley's North-West Gate (sealed) The Crown Valley, once it opens. It's past the Hollow. The signpost by the Hollow says \"The trees there are dead. Turn back.\" It means it. Its cold wears you down; a Thawwater keeps it out. See Harsh places."}]},{"slug":"cinderreach","title":"Cinderreach","summary":"Red badlands under the smoking Kiln, around the smithing town of Emberfall.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["cinderreach","emberfall","region","badlands","flame","kiln","gold"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"around-the-reach","text":"Around the Reach"},{"id":"people","text":"People"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Roads out"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"Hot, red badlands under the Kiln , a smoking volcano whose plume you can see from the whole east. The town is Emberfall , home of the hottest furnace in the Wardlands. Good for levels 25 to 55. Places: Emberfall, the Kiln, the Scorch, the Glass Sands, the Steamwells, the old workings, the foothills and the North-East Gate. The further you walk from Emberfall, the tougher it gets. Getting here: take the Kingsway north out of Highmoor. You come in at the south edge and follow the road north to Emberfall on the coast."},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Mining Gold veins on the Kiln's slopes and up on the Scorch (level 45), lots of coal and iron on the Scorch, hexslate, and fireclay in the foothills Smithing The great furnace in Emberfall Fishing Shrimp and herring down the east coast, and a cooling pond. Ask Cass what bites. Crafting, Cooking Workbench and campfire Etching The Warden of Flame statue, for Flame tiles (Etching 20) Brewing Firethorn bushes across the badlands, ember beetles, and the vent cauldron Services: a bank in a red tower, a general store (shut at night), the Quench tavern, a glassworks, the Anvil Council hall, and a Roadwarden post box (deposit only: whatever you drop in turns up in your bank). Waystone: Emberfall, and another near the North-East Gate."},{"id":"around-the-reach","text":"Area What's there The Scorch A raised red mesa in the north-west, rich in coal, iron and gold. Slag crawlers tunnel through it. The Scorch track climbs to an old Delver lookout on its knoll, with a view over the whole Reach. The Glass Sands Red dunes west of town. Dune vipers lie just under the sand. The Steamwells Two hot springs and steam vents out west by the Ashfall, with a bathhouse. Emberfall smiths say if the vents ever stop together, run. The old workings Slag heaps and fallen-in huts from Delver mines shut long ago. After dark, embers drift between the heaps with nothing burning under them. The Firebreak An old warm crack running west from the Kiln toward the Scorch, with small lava pools. The foothills The road south-west to the North-East Gate, past the fireclay pits."},{"id":"people","text":"Forgemistress Ysolde (head of the Anvil Council), Tamsy Glass (glassblower), Cass (salamander handler, with a scorched little skiff) and Old Bellows (a retired smith). Tamsy gives this region's map quest, The Glass Map . See maps."},{"id":"danger","text":"Raiders work the roads, and their warchief keeps the drum on the Cold Road by day. Ash hounds run round the town's edges, the Bone Guard keep a watch here too, and golems walk the Kiln itself. See monsters. Mending house: Old Bellows' burn shed, behind the great furnace. Fee 250. Guard's Rest: the Kiln road rest , baked red. See bones."},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Way Leads to Kingsway south to Highmoor Highmoor The Cold Road west, past the Glass Sands and the raiders' drum, to Frostpeak Frostpeak. \"Travellers 35+ advised.\" The mine cart Kettlepass, Frostpeak (by day) The gate road south-west The foothills and the North-East Gate The Kiln road north The Kiln. \"Mind the cracks.\" The Crown Valley's North-East Gate (sealed) The Crown Valley, once it opens Cass's skiff (by day) Wyrmholm, once the skiff is seaworthy Gold doesn't come easy. A vein gives one ore and then needs time to come back. The Kiln's heat wears you down while you're on it; a Cinderward keeps it off. See Harsh places."}]},{"slug":"maps","title":"Maps","summary":"Each region's map has to be earned. The world map stays under cloud until you do.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["maps","world-map","compass","quests","navigation"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-the-map","text":"Opening the map"},{"id":"the-region-map","text":"The region map"},{"id":"the-wardlands-map","text":"The Wardlands map"},{"id":"earning-maps","text":"Earning maps"}],"sections":[{"id":"opening-the-map","text":"Tap the compass or the place name at the top, or open ··· › Map . The map has two tabs."},{"id":"the-region-map","text":"If you have this region's map , you see a parchment chart you can drag and pinch. Tap a mark for its card. Mark Means B Bank $ Shop F , W Fire or forge, workbench \\ Waystone Exit or travel + Your grave Red ! Someone with a quest step for you Gold arrow You Open it from inside a building and it shows the town outside. If you don't , you see fog and a hint about who keeps the chart, such as \"Reedwick folk say Tobin Reed keeps the only chart.\""},{"id":"the-wardlands-map","text":"The whole realm. Provinces you have no map for stay under cloud , but your own position always shows as a pulsing gold ring. The footer counts your maps, out of 7."},{"id":"earning-maps","text":"Each region's map comes from a short map quest given by someone who lives there. See quests. Maps aren't bag items; you keep them for good. Until you have it, the region's map page tells you what to do next: finish an earlier quest first, who to ask, or that the map quest is already in your Journal. When you earn one, that province's cloud rolls back and its mark on the compass lights up. Map Ask Saltmere Wenna Gale, at the docks Fairmeadow Silas Quill, in Kingsferry Mistmarsh Tobin Reed, in Reedwick Highmoor Wren, in Windle Greenwood Hesper Vane, in Thornwick Cinderreach Tamsy Glass, in Emberfall Frostpeak Sorrel, at the Kettle in Kettlepass The Crown Valley Old Hob, in Kingsferry, once you own all six province maps and have heard about it"}]},{"slug":"frostpeak","title":"Frostpeak","summary":"Snowbound mountains around the Delvers' town of Kettlepass and its hot spring.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["frostpeak","kettlepass","region","snow","mountains","stone","frostfin"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"around-the-mountains","text":"Around the mountains"},{"id":"people","text":"People"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"the-delvings","text":"The Delvings"},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Roads out"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"High, snowbound mountains under Bellstone Peak , which rings like a bell when the wind is right. The town is Kettlepass , a Delvers' town that lives because of its hot spring. The toughest province so far: good for levels 35 to 65. Places: Kettlepass, the Switchbacks and the Delvings, the Fells, the tarn, the Rimewood, the Barrows, the Shoulder and Hornhold, and the North Gate. The mountains climb in steps here, up to five levels high, and the higher and further out you go, the tougher it gets. Getting here: the Cold Road , east from Greenwood or west from Cinderreach. Or ride the mine cart from Emberfall, by day only: \"The carters won't drive the Cold Road in the dark.\""},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Fishing Ice holes in the tarn, and more on the Black Tarn in the Rimewood. Ask Ulla Auger what bites. Mining Gold veins (45), coal seams, plenty of iron, hexslate, and snowclay up by the tarn Smithing A smithy in Kettlepass Crafting, Cooking Workbench and campfire Etching The Warden of Stone statue, for Stone tiles (Etching 35) Brewing Rimecap patches out in the snow, and the spring kettle Services: a bank, a general store (shut at night), the Kettle (bathhouse and tavern in one, with a bunk for 8 coins), the Delvers' hall, and a Roadwarden post box (deposit only). Waystone: Kettlepass, and another near the North Gate."},{"id":"around-the-mountains","text":"Area What's there The Switchbacks The road from Kettlepass up three cliff lines to the Delvings door, high on its shelf under Bellstone Peak. Mind the edges. The Fells High bare ground in the north, with ice spires that sing in the wind. Herders say the old clans used the spires' notes to find their way in a whiteout. The tarn A frozen lake on a shelf to the north-east, with the ice-fishers. The tarn track climbs to it from the Cold Road. The Rimewood Thick snow pines in the south-west, with snow wolves, the hunters' lodge, and the Black Tarn , a small lake that never freezes. The Barrows Old clan barrows on the high ground in the south-west. \"Leave them be after dark.\" The Shoulder A great massif in the south-east. A long track climbs it to Hornhold , a broken watchtower on top, with a view of all Frostpeak. Rams on the ledges, bears in the crags, and a giant who sits up there by day."},{"id":"people","text":"Reeve Halde (the town's reeve), Gunna Delve (foreman of the Delvers), Sorrel (keeps the Kettle) and Sergeant Brask (guards the road south). Sorrel gives this region's map quest, The Singing Map . See maps."},{"id":"danger","text":"The Bone Guard walk here in force, and they aren't the only thing in the snow: wolves in the Rimewood, cragbears, and what walks out of the Barrows at night. See monsters. The night cold: out in the open snow at night, the cold wears you down. Get indoors or drink a Frostward . See Harsh places. Mending house: a warm bath at the Kettle. Fee 500. Guard's Rest: the North Gate rest , furred with rime. See bones."},{"id":"the-delvings","text":"The old Delver mine under Bellstone Peak. Gunna Delve keeps its gate, and she's the one to talk to."},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Way Leads to The Cold Road west to Greenwood Greenwood The Cold Road east to Cinderreach Cinderreach The mine cart Emberfall, Cinderreach (by day) The Switchbacks north The Delvings door The gate road south Past the Rimewood and the Barrows to the North Gate The Crown Valley's North Gate (sealed) The Crown Valley, once it opens"}]},{"slug":"quests","title":"Quests","summary":"How quests start and how to keep track, plus every quest in the game so far. No solutions here.","section":"World","status":"partial","tags":["quests","map-quests","journal","rumours","pin","tasks","riddles"],"headings":[{"id":"how-quests-work","text":"How quests work"},{"id":"quests-in-the-game","text":"Quests in the game"},{"id":"hidden-quests","text":"Hidden quests"},{"id":"tasks","text":"Tasks"},{"id":"the-journal","text":"The Journal"},{"id":"quests","text":"Quests"},{"id":"rumours","text":"Rumours"},{"id":"maps","text":"Maps"},{"id":"spoilers","text":"Spoilers"}],"sections":[{"id":"how-quests-work","text":"- Quests start by talking to people . Someone with work for you will offer it once you're ready, and ask if you'll take it on. Say no and they won't keep asking: the offer waits at the top of their talk menu for whenever you're ready, and you still hear the whole story. - A story card opens when a quest starts (brown ribbon), gets a new step (blue) or is complete (gold, with your rewards). It shows who's speaking, with their portrait, and the world waits while you read. Tap Next , then Done . - Quest talk follows what you say. Some pages give you two replies to pick from. - Steps can mean talking to people, bringing things, beating monsters, or inspecting places and objects. A normal tap does it when a quest wants you to look at something; long-pressing works too. - Lost? Talk to the quest giver again. Once you've taken a quest, they tell you where things stand. - When a quest is done, its giver has one last word for you, said once. - Anyone a quest is waiting on stays out at their usual spot after dark and will still talk to you. Shops still close at night. - If you have the region's map, a red ! marks anyone with a step for you. HexSeal's quests are puzzles. They send you from place to place and expect you to look closely. This page won't spoil them."},{"id":"quests-in-the-game","text":"Quest Region Ask Reward Washed Ashore Saltmere Starts on its own 25 coins. See first-steps. The Harbour Chart Saltmere Wenna Gale, after Washed Ashore Saltmere map, Woodcutting XP, coins Three Honest Maps Fairmeadow Silas Quill, Kingsferry Fairmeadow map The Three Tatters Mistmarsh Tobin Reed, Reedwick Mistmarsh map, Fishing XP, coins A Bird's-Eye View Highmoor Wren, Windle Highmoor map, Woodcutting XP, coins The Forester's Count Greenwood Hesper Vane, Thornwick Greenwood map, Woodcutting XP, coins The Glass Map Cinderreach Tamsy Glass, Emberfall Cinderreach map, Smithing XP, coins The Singing Map Frostpeak Sorrel, Kettlepass Frostpeak map, Mining XP, coins A quest line in Frostpeak Frostpeak Gunna Delve, at the Delvings gate See The Delvings The Last Toll Fairmeadow Old Hob, Kingsferry, after Washed Ashore The post horn and the King's Post. See The King's Post. Ida's Rumour Mill Saltmere Ida, at her stall, after Washed Ashore The rook whistle, coins, Fishing and Trade XP. See Post rooks. Six Notches Fairmeadow Old Hob, Kingsferry The start of a long quest line. See Something for the centre. Rung Backwards Mistmarsh Tobin Reed, Reedwick The start of a long quest line. See Something for the centre. Counted With the Wind Highmoor Old Corra, at the Sixstones The start of a long quest line. See Something for the centre. The Grey Wood Greenwood Hesper Vane, Thornwick The start of a long quest line. See Something for the centre. Six-Sided Flaws Cinderreach Tamsy Glass, Emberfall The start of a long quest line. See Something for the centre. The Bone Captain The Crown Valley The Bone Captain, once he'll talk See The Bone Captain First Loaf Fairmeadow Mabry Loaf, at the Kingsferry Hearthhouse Her bake book, whose pages fill in as your Cooking grows. See Baking. First Firing Fairmeadow Prue Crock, across the Brin from the watermill Crafting XP, and pottery opens up. See Pottery. First Feather Highmoor Captain Hale Yarrow, at the Windle butts Archery and Crafting XP, a bow, arrows and a quiver. See archery. The Mender's Kit Saltmere Hattie Combe, after Washed Ashore Opens Brewing, and 5 bottles of Grit Draught"},{"id":"hidden-quests","text":"A handful of quests, including side quests in Highmoor and Greenwood, stay ??? in your Journal until someone lets slip a rumour about them. They're worth listening for: hidden quests pay better than they'd otherwise pay, and each one gives a one-of-a-kind cosmetic you can't get any other way."},{"id":"tasks","text":"Tasks are big hand-ins you can deliver over several trips. Task Who What Opens Build the ferry Pell, Saltmere 25 oak logs, 10 bronze bars The ferry to Kingsferry"},{"id":"the-journal","text":"Open Journal from the dock. A gold dot on it, and a short Journal updated message, mean something new has been written. The Talk to chip, and the pinned quest line above the dock, show the person's portrait. Quests Your quests, grouped by region with the one you're in first. Each shows how far along you are, your latest diary line, who to see and where, and live counts of anything you're collecting. If a step needs a particular control, a Tip: line under the diary entry tells you how, for example to press and hold a hex to look closer. Finished quests fold away under Done . If you're carrying a riddle map, its riddle is listed here too (never the answer). Button What it does Show on map Opens the region map with a gold ring on the person to see Ask again Repeats their last clue. It never gives you the next one. Saying goodbye takes you back to the Journal. Story so far Your diary for the quest from the start Pin Keeps this quest's next step in a strip above the dock, bottom right. Tap the strip to open the Journal. Rumours Every story quest is listed here from the start, under the region it begins in, with its name and where it starts, so you always know where to go next. People also let rumours slip the first time you talk to them, and the innkeeper at the Salted Eel will tell you one you haven't heard if you ask \"Any news?\" . A few quests show only as ??? until you hear their rumour. Each region's header counts the quests you know and the ones still ???. See Hidden quests. If you'd rather see every quest start, hidden ones included, turn on Journal: show all quest starts in Settings. Maps The region maps you own, and a hint about where to find the ones you don't. Notice-board orders and town works as errands, long-press to pin, rereading finished quests, and a ribbon for finishing a region."},{"id":"spoilers","text":"None here, on purpose. If you're stuck, try tapping or long-pressing everything near where the quest points you, and check the time of day."}]},{"slug":"crown-valley","title":"The Crown Valley","summary":"The grey valley inside the Crownwall, ringed by six gates, with the Hexhold at its heart.","section":"World","status":"partial","tags":["crown-valley","crownwall","hexhold","region","lantern-camp","bone-captain","duels","hexwood","hexstone","hexcarp"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"people","text":"People"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"the-hexhold-door","text":"The Hexhold door"},{"id":"the-bone-captain","text":"The Bone Captain"},{"id":"the-captain-s-rest","text":"The Captain's Rest"},{"id":"duels","text":"Duels"},{"id":"the-crown-valley-map","text":"The Crown Valley map"},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Roads out"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"Inside the ring of mountains lies the Crown Valley : grey grass, a black moat, and the Hexhold itself. The hardest place in the game so far, for levels 65 to 90. A road runs right round the moat, and the roads from each gate ramp down to meet it. There's only one way across the water, though: the bridge. Places: the Lantern camp, the six gates, the moat, the bridge, the Hexhold door, the duel ring and the Captain's Rest. Getting here: through one of the six Crownwall gates , one on the far side of each province. Each stays shut until you've earned your way through, and how is part of that province's story. Once you've walked through a gate, the Crownwall Gate teleport (Magic 68) can take you back inside it. See magic."},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Woodcutting Duskwood (70) and hexwood (80) trees Mining Sealstone (70), greyed slate (75) and hexstone (80) Fishing The moat. Pike bite at any hour; hexcarp (70) come up after dark. Cooking The Lanternfolk's campfire Services: at the Lantern camp , just inside the South Gate: a bank chest, a campfire and a waystone. There's no shop, forge or workbench in the valley. Ide has bigger plans for the camp, in time (see Late works). The valley has its own music."},{"id":"people","text":"Lantern-keeper Ide of the Lanternfolk, who carry the fallen out of places like this. And the Bone Captain , on the bridge."},{"id":"danger","text":"Everything here hits hard. See monsters. - Seal colossi stand just inside the gates. They only move if you step on the road they watch. - Greyed guards and seal sentinels walk the valley and the foot of the Hexhold walls. - The Bone Captain holds the bridge to the Hexhold door, and he isn't friendly. Not at first. Mending house: Ide's tent at the Lantern camp. Fee 1,200."},{"id":"the-hexhold-door","text":"The door at the end of the bridge has a hollow for each seal. Every seal you hold lights one."},{"id":"the-bone-captain","text":"Hold enough of the land's trust and the Captain stops fighting and starts talking. That starts a quest, The Bone Captain . The Captain's Rest A roll stone by the bridge, carved with the names of the Crown Watch's officers. It's where the captains' bones are laid to rest. Finding them is up to you. See bones. Duels The Captain spars in the duel ring : a set of bouts, each with its own rules. You need a total level of 750 to take him on, and losing costs you nothing . What the rules are, and what you win, is for you to find out."},{"id":"the-crown-valley-map","text":"Once you own all six province maps, word gets round that Old Hob in Kingsferry has one more map in him. See maps."},{"id":"roads-out","text":"Gate Leads to The South Gate to Kingsferry Fairmeadow The West Gate to Mistmarsh Mistmarsh The East Gate to Highmoor Highmoor The North-West Gate to the Greenwood Greenwood The North-East Gate to Cinderreach Cinderreach The North Gate to Frostpeak Frostpeak"}]},{"slug":"wyrmholm","title":"Wyrmholm","summary":"A smoking island off Cinderreach where drakes nest. Late-game, reached only by Cass's skiff.","section":"World","status":"shipped","tags":["wyrmholm","island","drakes","dragons","cinderreach","heat","kilnfin","wyrmglass","late-game"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance"},{"id":"getting-there","text":"Getting there"},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"What's here"},{"id":"danger","text":"Danger"},{"id":"drake-breath","text":"Drake breath"},{"id":"the-joint-notice","text":"The Joint Notice"},{"id":"the-wyrm-s-cairn","text":"The Wyrm's Cairn"},{"id":"the-end-of-the-line","text":"The end of the line"},{"id":"lore","text":"Lore"}],"sections":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"A black-sand island of ash, glass and steam off the Cinderreach coast. Drakes nest in its middle, and the ground is warm wherever you stand. It's for seasoned fighters: combat level 60 at the very least, and more is wise. Places: the black-sand shore, the ash ground, the obsidian ridge, the crater lake, the lava pools, the drake nests and the Wyrm's Cairn ."},{"id":"getting-there","text":"Cass keeps a little skiff in the shallows off Emberfall. When it's seaworthy, Cass will row you out to \"the smoking rock\" , by day only . \"Not at night. Things fly at night.\" The way there opens through two hidden Cinderreach quests, late in the game. Listen for rumours of smoke in Windle. See Hidden quests."},{"id":"what-s-here","text":"Skill Where to train Woodcutting Emberleaf trees (level 40) and duskwood (70) Mining Wyrmglass rocks (level 75) Fishing Lava pools : cinder eel (50), and now and then a kilnfin (82) Crafting Search the drake nests for shed drake scales (Crafting 60) Cooking Cass's driftwood fire on the shore There's no bank, shop or waystone on the island."},{"id":"danger","text":"The heat. Out by the nests and the crater, heat comes up in waves and takes 2 health every 12 ticks . A Cinderward draught keeps it off. See brewing. Drakes nest in the middle of the island: rust drakes near the shore, ember drakes deeper in, and darker ones up on the ridge. They don't like visitors. See monsters. Drake breath Every so often a drake takes a deep breath, and the hexes in front of it toward you glow orange . A couple of ticks later they burn. Step off the glow and you take nothing. Drake Breath Burns for Rust drake A short line, 2 hexes 8 to 14 Ember drake A cone, 3 hexes deep 12 to 20 Obsidian drake A cone, 3 hexes deep, more often 16 to 26 If it does catch you, a Cinderward halves the burn, a drakehide or Wyrmscale body halves it again, and a Wyrmscale shield takes another quarter off. Drake-made bodies also keep off the island's heat. See Drake gear. The Joint Notice Once Cass's skiff is running, the notice boards in Windle and Emberfall post a drake bounty each dawn: kill 6 to 12 drakes of any kind, then hand it in at either board. It pays coins per drake (more for the bigger ones), a few drake scales, and now and then a riddle map or a curio. Mending house: fall here and you wake in Emberfall, at Old Bellows' burn shed. Fee 250."},{"id":"the-wyrm-s-cairn","text":"A cairn of stacked black glass, taller than a man, with enormous bones laid at its foot. Every drake drops dragon bones , and laying them at the cairn seems to please the brood. See bones."},{"id":"the-end-of-the-line","text":"The drake quests end with a third, from Old Bellows. It's the hardest fight in Cinderreach. Bring good food, a Cinderward, and a Water spell or two."},{"id":"lore","text":"Emberfall's smiths say the smoke used to come only from the Kiln. Old Bellows says his master sang to the Kiln every night, and won't say why. Cass says the island has always been there, and that's all Cass will say. Lately, Emberfall folk say the Kiln breathes easier at night."}]},{"slug":"release-notes","title":"Pre-release notes","summary":"What each test build added, newest first. Kept high level and spoiler-free.","section":"Updates","status":"shipped","tags":["updates","builds","release-notes"],"headings":[{"id":"about-these-notes","text":"About these notes"},{"id":"november-24-2026","text":"November 24, 2026"},{"id":"build-991-testflight-991","text":"Build 991 (TestFlight 991)"},{"id":"november-23-2026","text":"November 23, 2026"},{"id":"build-990-testflight-990","text":"Build 990 (TestFlight 990)"},{"id":"november-22-2026","text":"November 22, 2026"},{"id":"build-989-testflight-989","text":"Build 989 (TestFlight 989)"},{"id":"november-20-2026","text":"November 20, 2026"},{"id":"build-988-testflight-988","text":"Build 988 (TestFlight 988)"},{"id":"november-19-2026","text":"November 19, 2026"},{"id":"builds-943-to-987-testflight-987","text":"Builds 943 to 987 (TestFlight 987)"},{"id":"october-21-2026","text":"October 21, 2026"},{"id":"build-942","text":"Build 942"},{"id":"october-20-2026","text":"October 20, 2026"},{"id":"build-929","text":"Build 929"},{"id":"build-921","text":"Build 921"},{"id":"build-903","text":"Build 903"},{"id":"build-890","text":"Build 890"},{"id":"build-867","text":"Build 867"},{"id":"build-858","text":"Build 858"},{"id":"build-855","text":"Build 855"},{"id":"build-838","text":"Build 838"},{"id":"build-818","text":"Build 818"},{"id":"build-813","text":"Build 813"},{"id":"build-796","text":"Build 796"},{"id":"build-782","text":"Build 782"},{"id":"build-767","text":"Build 767"},{"id":"build-763","text":"Build 763"},{"id":"build-739","text":"Build 739"},{"id":"october-18-2026","text":"October 18, 2026"},{"id":"build-722","text":"Build 722"},{"id":"build-709","text":"Build 709"},{"id":"build-691","text":"Build 691"},{"id":"build-686","text":"Build 686"},{"id":"build-674","text":"Build 674"},{"id":"build-647","text":"Build 647"},{"id":"build-624-testflight-0-1-0-624","text":"Build 624 (TestFlight 0.1.0 (624))"},{"id":"october-17-2026","text":"October 17, 2026"},{"id":"build-610-testflight-0-1-0-610","text":"Build 610 (TestFlight 0.1.0 (610))"},{"id":"build-597-also-the-first-testflight-build-0-1-0-602","text":"Build 597 (also the first TestFlight build, 0.1.0 (602))"},{"id":"build-590","text":"Build 590"},{"id":"build-548","text":"Build 548"},{"id":"build-508","text":"Build 508"},{"id":"build-499","text":"Build 499"},{"id":"build-485","text":"Build 485"},{"id":"build-476","text":"Build 476"},{"id":"build-471","text":"Build 471"},{"id":"build-463","text":"Build 463"},{"id":"build-458","text":"Build 458"},{"id":"build-450","text":"Build 450"},{"id":"build-447","text":"Build 447"},{"id":"build-442","text":"Build 442"},{"id":"build-436","text":"Build 436"},{"id":"build-432","text":"Build 432"},{"id":"build-429","text":"Build 429"},{"id":"build-426","text":"Build 426"},{"id":"build-422","text":"Build 422"},{"id":"build-419","text":"Build 419"},{"id":"build-415","text":"Build 415"},{"id":"october-16-2026","text":"October 16, 2026"},{"id":"build-409","text":"Build 409"},{"id":"build-407","text":"Build 407"},{"id":"build-404","text":"Build 404"},{"id":"build-392","text":"Build 392"},{"id":"build-384","text":"Build 384"},{"id":"build-380","text":"Build 380"},{"id":"build-376","text":"Build 376"},{"id":"build-370","text":"Build 370"},{"id":"build-361","text":"Build 361"},{"id":"build-356","text":"Build 356"},{"id":"build-351","text":"Build 351"},{"id":"build-346","text":"Build 346"},{"id":"build-341","text":"Build 341"},{"id":"build-336","text":"Build 336"},{"id":"build-333","text":"Build 333"},{"id":"build-330","text":"Build 330"},{"id":"build-327","text":"Build 327"},{"id":"build-323","text":"Build 323"},{"id":"build-320","text":"Build 320"},{"id":"build-318","text":"Build 318"},{"id":"build-314","text":"Build 314"},{"id":"build-309","text":"Build 309"},{"id":"october-15-2026","text":"October 15, 2026"},{"id":"build-295","text":"Build 295"},{"id":"build-290","text":"Build 290"},{"id":"build-285","text":"Build 285"},{"id":"build-281","text":"Build 281"},{"id":"build-277","text":"Build 277"},{"id":"build-272","text":"Build 272"},{"id":"build-266","text":"Build 266"},{"id":"build-262","text":"Build 262"},{"id":"build-257","text":"Build 257"},{"id":"build-252","text":"Build 252"},{"id":"build-248","text":"Build 248"},{"id":"build-243","text":"Build 243"},{"id":"build-237","text":"Build 237"},{"id":"build-233","text":"Build 233"},{"id":"build-230","text":"Build 230"},{"id":"build-226","text":"Build 226"},{"id":"build-219","text":"Build 219"},{"id":"build-216","text":"Build 216"},{"id":"build-208","text":"Build 208"},{"id":"build-200","text":"Build 200"},{"id":"build-196","text":"Build 196"},{"id":"build-190","text":"Build 190"},{"id":"build-185","text":"Build 185"},{"id":"build-179","text":"Build 179"},{"id":"build-175","text":"Build 175"},{"id":"build-170","text":"Build 170"},{"id":"build-161","text":"Build 161"},{"id":"october-14-2026","text":"October 14, 2026"},{"id":"build-155","text":"Build 155"},{"id":"build-150","text":"Build 150"},{"id":"build-146","text":"Build 146"},{"id":"build-142","text":"Build 142"},{"id":"build-136","text":"Build 136"},{"id":"build-130","text":"Build 130"},{"id":"build-126","text":"Build 126"},{"id":"build-120","text":"Build 120"},{"id":"build-116","text":"Build 116"},{"id":"build-112","text":"Build 112"},{"id":"build-108","text":"Build 108"},{"id":"builds-100-and-104","text":"Builds 100 and 104"},{"id":"build-96","text":"Build 96"},{"id":"build-92","text":"Build 92"},{"id":"october-13-2026","text":"October 13, 2026"},{"id":"build-87","text":"Build 87"},{"id":"build-83","text":"Build 83"},{"id":"builds-70-to-79","text":"Builds 70 to 79"},{"id":"build-68","text":"Build 68"},{"id":"build-57","text":"Build 57"},{"id":"build-51","text":"Build 51"},{"id":"build-43","text":"Build 43"},{"id":"builds-30-to-38","text":"Builds 30 to 38"},{"id":"october-12-2026","text":"October 12, 2026"},{"id":"build-29","text":"Build 29"},{"id":"builds-24-to-28","text":"Builds 24 to 28"},{"id":"build-18","text":"Build 18"},{"id":"builds-9-to-17","text":"Builds 9 to 17"},{"id":"build-4","text":"Build 4"},{"id":"build-1","text":"Build 1"}],"sections":[{"id":"about-these-notes","text":"HexSeal isn't released yet. These are notes for the test builds on the way there. The build number is the one shown at the bottom of Settings . When the game launches, these notes will be archived and the release notes will start fresh. Notes cover what changed for players, never story beats or secrets."},{"id":"november-24-2026","text":"Build 991 (TestFlight 991) - Five new bakes between Cooking 10 and 45, each with a page in Mabry's bake book. Pie dishes help with tarts and pasties too. See Baking. - Tap a bake in the Cooking skill guide for its recipe card, with how many you've baked. - Long-press a pile on the ground to choose which thing to take. A tap still takes everything. - Battery saver in Settings. The game also eases off its frame rate when nothing's moving, when your phone is hot, or in Low Power Mode. - Text size and Icon size settings, from 80% to 150%. - Teleports in the spellbook now say where they go. - Fences join up properly. - The pool beside the Leaky Eel in Mistmarsh no longer flickers."},{"id":"november-23-2026","text":"Build 990 (TestFlight 990) - Magic is complete up to 99. Two new combat ranks, Waves (from Magic 50) and Storms (from 82), draw on the Hollow. New spells to discover include a strong heal and a way across open water. See magic. - New teleports to Thornwick, Emberfall and Kettlepass, and a late one to the Hexhold. - Gust now swings windmill sails round, and has another use or two out in the world. - High Magic perks at 70, 75 and 85, and a quick bar for your first three pinned spells, bottom left above the menu. - Reaching Magic 99 is now worth it. See High Magic, behind a spoiler tap. - Workbenches are now a long two-hex carpenter's bench, and several have moved: Kingsferry, Reedwick, Windle, Thornwick and Emberfall. See Where to find the stations. - In Settings, only a setting's switch changes it, so a stray tap on the row does nothing. - The game now plays in portrait either way up. - The zoom slider is taller. On an open iPhone Duo, your pinned skills run down the far left edge."},{"id":"november-22-2026","text":"Build 989 (TestFlight 989) - Emberite and rimesilver can be mined. Emberite (Mining 30) is in Cinderreach and rimesilver (Mining 40) in Frostpeak, so embersteel and frostsilver gear can be made. There's more coal in Highmoor and Greenwood, more iron in Greenwood, and more of the deeper ores where they belong. See Where to mine. - Scenery stones no longer mine like ore. - Tougher foes teach more. Fighting above your level gives extra combat XP, and picking on much weaker foes gives less. - People of level 12 and up carry more coins and sometimes a cooked fish. - Doors and beds only turn you away while something is actually on you. Monsters that can't catch you give up and go home. - Tap your grave to take everything at once, or pick things out one at a time. Long-press still has Take all . See Your grave. - The ruin on Spire Hill is solid now, so you walk round its walls instead of through them. - Mags the Pedlar has her own hooded look, and a face on her story card. Every speaker now shows a face. - Old Corra greets you by your title again. - In Mistmarsh, a quest's reed-cutting spot is easier to find: the reeds stand tall where they grow, and a plain tap cuts them."},{"id":"november-20-2026","text":"Build 988 (TestFlight 988) - Greenwood, Cinderreach and Frostpeak are full size , as big as Fairmeadow, Mistmarsh and Highmoor. Each has new areas to explore, new signposts, places to look out from, and five new kinds of monster. See greenwood, cinderreach, frostpeak and monsters. - Greenwood gains the Deepwood, Wendmere, the Highwood ridge, the Charwood, the Greying and the Alder fen, with still-water and sea fishing and more elderwood. - Cinderreach gains the Scorch mesa, the Glass Sands, the Steamwells and the old workings, with much more ore and firethorn. - Frostpeak climbs in steps up to five levels high: the Switchbacks to the Delvings, the Fells, the Rimewood, the Barrows, and the Shoulder with Hornhold on top. More ore, rimecap and ice holes. - Saves made before this build carry over: wherever you were in these three regions, you pick up in the same spot on the bigger map. - The treasure charts for Greenwood, Cinderreach and Frostpeak are redrawn for the new maps. - A zoom slider above the menu zooms with one thumb. It replaces the tap-and-hold zoom, which kept going off by accident. Settings can hide it. On an open iPhone Duo held sideways, the slider and your pins sit at the far edge above the menu. - At night, tap an inn from outside to sleep through the night without going in."},{"id":"november-19-2026","text":"Builds 943 to 987 (TestFlight 987) Build numbers now count TestFlight releases, so they jump here. - Foldables. HexSeal now plays well on the iPhone Duo, with split, wide and side-by-side layouts. See Playing on a foldable. - Tapping the world while a menu is open closes the menu, and nothing else happens. - The bank's Sort groups by kind, and a second tap sorts A to Z. - The \"how many?\" menu and item cards open beside the slot you held. - Tapping food eats the one you tapped, and banking moves the slot you tapped. - After a sale you see how many sold and your new coin total. - A Carter's crate in the Saltmere, Kingsferry and Windle forges sends your smithing to the bank. - The Pack beside a shop or station shares the screen instead of pushing it away. - Settings sliders work with a sideways swipe, and an up or down swipe scrolls. - Tapping a villager's spot walks you beside them instead of on top of them. - A tricky step in a Saltmere quest has clearer signs now. - A region with no map yet tells you how to earn it. - Story cards always show a face or a picture, and fill in every name. - A new start-up screen with the HexSeal logo."},{"id":"october-21-2026","text":"Build 942 - The character preview in your Pack mirrors you more closely: it shows your rod and line while you fish, and takes on your disguise while a disguise draught lasts."},{"id":"october-20-2026","text":"Build 929 - The Magic tab is redesigned: taller, with your tile satchel at the top. - The rosette folds away while you browse and names the spell as you lay tiles. - Element filters can be combined, and you can pin favourite spells to the top. - The spellbook lists the spells you've found, plus the ones you've heard of. Build 921 - Sheep and cows graze in pens by Fairmeadow's farmsteads. They never fight back, and give raw meat, fleece and cowhide. - Cowhide tans into leather at a workbench. - Smoother walking on 120 Hz screens. - Spells and arrows follow a moving target. Build 903 - Pottery arrives as part of Crafting. Dig clay at riverbanks in four regions, shape it at a potter's wheel and fire it in a kiln. Firing can crack a piece, less often as your Crafting improves. - Prue Crock, a potter by the Brin in Fairmeadow, teaches the craft. - A pie dish helps your pies bake, and a brew crock holds more sips than a bottle. Build 890 - A jug of water now holds 4 pours. - Fill jugs at rivers, becks, ponds and tarns, but not the sea. - New wells beside bread ovens and mills, and water butts by the hearth in two taverns. - A new spell to discover, for carrying water. Build 867 - Highmoor's moving cloud shadows are gone for now; the gusts and windmills stay. - The Baking and Hauling \"Good to know\" tips show their text and a picture again. - Exits are named for the edge they sit on, such as \"Kingsway south to Fairmeadow\" from Highmoor, and the Crown Valley gates read \"(sealed)\" until they open. Build 858 - The Journal shows people's proper names again where some had gone missing. - First Feather is listed in the Journal again. Build 855 - New errand, First Loaf : Mabry Loaf at the Kingsferry Hearthhouse teaches you to bake your first loaf, and gives you her bake book. - The other Hearthhouse cooks point you to her if you ask about baking. - Every item has a proper bag picture, and quest items look right when dropped. Build 838 - Raw meat can be cooked on any fire. - Farmstead and farmhouse ovens now only bake; tavern hearths still work as fires. - Ovens list every bake, greying out the ones you can't make yet. - Windmills all face into the wind, and cloud shadows have soft edges. - Fleece has a proper look. Build 818 - Windwarden tower polish: the wind on the roof looks wilder. Build 813 - The Windwarden tower. The ruined tower on Highmoor's north moor is open, up a track off the Kingsway. Four floors, each harder than the last, and the carvings on the walls are worth reading. Folk in Windle say something on the roof still howls. - Some creatures' flying, sleeping and stunned poses now show properly. Build 796 - Highmoor's moorland creatures and cairns have their proper looks, and the Highmoor chart is tidier. - Some Mistmarsh creatures now carry weapons, and the fallen no longer sink into the ground. Build 782 - Highmoor is now full size, around the hill town of Windle, with the Fells, moorland tarns and more windmills on the ridges. - A ruined tower stands on the north moor, its door blocked by fallen stone for now. - Grass, heather and trees sway in the wind. Calmer effects keeps them still. - New moorland creatures roam. - New setting: Show frame rate . Build 767 - Highmoor has wind: gusts in the sky, drifting cloud shadows, and windmill sails that turn everywhere, faster in a Highmoor gust. - New setting: Calmer effects turns off the gust lines. Build 763 - The Gullwatch shrine in Saltmere is now a small wayside shrine in the old fort wall. Build 739 - The game is now called HexSeal, on the home screen, the title and in the Files app. Your saves stay where they were. - Mistmarsh is much bigger, with more open water and bog. Reedwick's houses spread out into the marsh, and there are new places to find, including Willow Carr and the Meres. - New marsh creatures, tougher the deeper you go. - Bog iron, more eel pools and coast fishing in Mistmarsh. - Saltmere's Warden statue is now the Gullwatch shrine."},{"id":"october-18-2026","text":"Build 722 - Staffs now take both hands and stay the strongest weapons for casting. - Ten new one-handed wands leave room for a tome or a shield. - Element tomes can be rebound into three stronger editions, using a new brew, Warden's ink. Build 709 - Every town now has a Hearthhouse, a cook house with a range that burns less, a quicker bread oven, a work table with water, and a resident cook. - One pantry chest is shared by all the Hearthhouses, and the range and oven cook straight from it. - Fixed: mixing dough from the pantry with a nearly full bag could lose the dough. Build 691 - Notice-board orders always pay more than selling the same goods in that town. - Market Warrants and orders now also give XP in the skill that made the goods. - More magic experts can give you a tome. Build 686 - A tile satchel for everyone from the start, holding 250 of each tile. Tiles go straight into it, and shops and your stall can sell from it. - Bigger satchels can be crafted at Crafting 20, 45 and 75. - A new Etching perk, Sure Chisel , gives a chance of an extra tile per blank from Etching 30. Build 674 - Your character in the Pack now shows what you're doing: mining, fighting, cooking and more. - The Cooking skill guide lists bakes, with All, Fish and Baking tabs. - The bank, post boxes and other storage now open as one window with your bag, closed with a single X. - Forge ore bins also hold bones and bone ash. - New tips point to easier ways to haul your goods. Build 647 - A monster's hit now interrupts etching. - Ash-fluxed wardglass at the forge now really does give twice the blanks. - Something you do later in the story makes etching safer. We'll leave you to find out what. Build 624 (TestFlight 0.1.0 (624)) - When a quest asks you to look at something, a normal tap does it now, not just the press-and-hold card. - The curio seller in Fairmeadow's map quest now describes each map on offer. - Monsters near one Warden statue in Mistmarsh only come out at night, so etching there is easier by day. - Includes TestFlight build 613."},{"id":"october-17-2026","text":"Build 610 (TestFlight 0.1.0 (610)) - Bridges now line up with the roads and walkways they carry, and the paths run right up to them. Build 597 (also the first TestFlight build, 0.1.0 (602)) - Riddle maps can be sold again. The store warns you first, since a sold map is gone for good. - The Journal's Rumours tab now lists each quest under the region it starts in. Build 590 - Archery, a new combat skill: shoot shortbows and longbows from range, with arrows in a quiver on your tool belt. Arrows that don't break come back when you walk over them. It counts toward combat level. See archery. - Bow-making in Crafting: spin flax into bowstrings, carve and string bows, and fletch your own arrows. Archer's leather armour too. - Captain Hale Yarrow at the Windle butts starts a new quest, First Feather . Ossie Nock in Thornwick sells bows and fletching supplies. - Shops now ask before every sale, and quest and story items can't be sold. Build 548 - Baking: pick wheat in Fairmeadow, grind it at a mill, and bake bread, pies and cakes in ovens, all part of Cooking. Pies and cakes are eaten a bite at a time. See Baking. - Clearer quest talk: quest conversations read better, replies fit what was said, and some pages give you two to pick from. Turning down a quest no longer gets you asked again on every visit. - New hidden side quests in Highmoor and Greenwood, found through rumours. Build 508 - A road round the moat: the Crown Valley now has a road right round the moat, with the gate roads ramping down to it. Build 499 - Bigger village buildings: cottages, wells, crates and cart stations are larger in every region. - The Cinderreach and Frostpeak forges are now full smithies. Build 485 - Every story quest in your Journal: the Rumours tab now lists each story quest and where it starts, right from the beginning. - A few quests stay hidden as ??? until you hear their rumour. They pay better and each gives a one-of-a-kind cosmetic. - Settings Journal: show all quest starts reveals everything, hidden quests included. Build 476 - Longer-lasting orders: Market Warrants now stay up for 3 real days, and a new one goes up the day after you fill one. Part-done warrants still pay for what you delivered. - Notice-board orders last 2 in-game days (3 for big ones), refill one slot at a time, and show their days left. - Cooked fish now have clearer names, like \"Cooked trout\". Build 471 - Easier quarry climb: the Fairmeadow quarry terrace now has four ramps up along its length instead of one. Build 463 - Brewing rewards for your bottles: combine part-drunk bottles at Brewing 20, bigger bottles at 45 and 85, and twin bottles holding two draughts at 95. - Menders will pour your part bottles together for free. Build 458 - Scaling boards by the water in every fishing region. Scale your raw fish for fish scales (you keep the fish) and a little Cooking XP. Build 450 - Brews are now bottles. Each brew makes a 4-sip bottle in your pack; tap it to drink. Bottles stack in the bank. The flask belt and the bank's Cellar tab are gone, and any sips you had are waiting in your bank as bottles. - HUD pins: pin up to 4 bottles, foods or other usables to the screen. Hold the item and tap Pin to HUD. - The Brewing perks at 40 and 70 now give a chance of a second bottle. - Cooking fish no longer gives fish scales. Build 447 - A Brews tab in the Magic menu, beside Spells, with your flasks, your cellar and a brewing book of herbs, where they grow and where to brew. Belt flasks show their names, and helpful tips suggest a brew when you need one. - Enemy name tags: in a fight, enemies show their name and level, coloured by how tough they are for you. - Fairmeadow's foothills quarry has more copper and tin, and now iron and coal too. Build 442 - New music: a warm village waltz for Thornwick, a low, uneasy theme for Wyrmholm's shore, and danger music for the island's deepest places. Build 436 - The drake quests reach their end with a third quest from Old Bellows, and a fight Cinderreach will talk about for years. Two new bounties come with it, and someone small and warm wants to follow you home. Build 432 - Drakes breathe fire now. Watch for the orange glow and step out of it. - Drake gear: drakehide and Wyrmscale armour, a wyrmglass staff, and a wyrmfang spear, axe and pickaxe, each with a trick of its own. - The Joint Notice , a daily drake bounty on the Windle and Emberfall notice boards. - Kilnfin and sealsteel rivets have their own looks. Build 429 - The outdoor forges on Saltmere, in Kingsferry and in Windle are now proper smithies, with a roof, a smoking chimney and a hearth that glows at night. Stand in front of the hearth to work. Build 426 - Every general store, and Kingsferry's curio shop and stables, are now bigger buildings. - A few things moved a step: the warrant crates on Saltmere and in Highmoor, the Reedwick store's door, and a rimecap patch in Frostpeak. Build 422 - Lamp posts light the town streets at night, and lit windows and lamps throw warm light on the ground around you. - Cottages, farmsteads, shops, stables, wells, crates and hay bales are now sized to match each other. Build 419 - Fairmeadow is reworked again: four bridges over the Brin, farmsteads in the fields, gentle terraces from the harbour up to the gate, and a cobbled market square in Kingsferry. - You can now fish the Brin itself, from the bank. Greenwood's river spots work the same way. Build 415 - Kingsferry's cottages, watermill, warehouses, stables and boatyard now stand at their proper size. - One of the late town works now leaves something growing by your cottage."},{"id":"october-16-2026","text":"Build 409 - After dark, a single tap on an inn's stairs or bed takes the room and puts you to sleep. Build 407 - Something is stirring in Cinderreach. Two new late-game quests start from Windle and Emberfall, for fighters of combat level 60 and up. - Wyrmholm , a smoking island off Cinderreach, with drakes, emberleaf trees, wyrmglass rocks and a rare lava fish. Bring a Cinderward. - A new spell, Kindle , and sealsteel rivets at the forge. Build 404 - Rooms at every inn. Inns let rooms from dusk to dawn, at the bed or from the innkeeper, and four inns have new keepers. The last bed you slept in is where you wake if you fall in that province, for free. - One ore bin for every forge. Ore left at one village forge is waiting at the others. Your old bins are merged, and nothing is lost. - New late-game town works for players who've come a long way. Build 392 - A much bigger Fairmeadow. The province is redrawn with hills and terraces, a larger Kingsferry and Spire Hill. Its map chart is redrawn to match. - If your save was in Fairmeadow, you'll wake somewhere safe nearby, and any field chests you'd left there are waiting in your bank. - Fishing spots sit right on the shore now, and the Fairmeadow post box is back, on the Spire Hill road. - The keep at Gullwatch Point has a door now. - The title screen's fly-bys show the new Fairmeadow. Build 384 - Brewing polish. Some draughts now meet the world in new ways, and there's a new puzzle for brewers who like a disguise. - Masters of Brewing get a livelier mantle, a new way to be greeted, and a moment of their own. Build 380 - Mistmarsh and Highmoor change at night. Different things walk the roads after dark, shops close, windows glow, and Reedwick has a bell to listen for at midnight. - The While you were away card now lists the draught sips you used. - The day clock keeps running while you're on the bank's Cellar tab. Build 376 - Every quest conversation has been rewritten. Quest givers now ask before a quest starts (say no and they'll ask again), tell you where things stand once you've started, and have a last word when you're done. Your own replies have more character. - Anyone a quest is waiting on stays out after dark so you can still find them. Shops still close at night. Build 370 - Two more town works: restore the guild hall in Kingsferry for a fourth notice-board order each day, and re-roof the foresters' lodge in Thornwick, where logs then sell for 30% more. - Fine pieces from the quench trough earn a little extra Trade XP. Build 361 - Higher-level things are worth more. Items past level 30 now sell for much more the higher their level, and anything you make is worth at least what went into it. - Early warrants pay a little less; later ones are unchanged. - Six big new town works : an eel smokery in Reedwick, a shearing barn in Windle, a bowyer's loft in Thornwick, the Emberfall bell and a gilded Anvil Council in Emberfall, and a fur trader in Kettlepass. Build 356 - Market Warrants. Every market now posts one big order a day, matched to your skills, at a delivery crate by the general store. It pays well, gives Trade XP and a Market Seal, and renews at midnight. - Fill any five in a week for the Market Clerks' chest. - Spend Market Seals on new things for your cottage. - Runners and rooks can deliver straight to a warrant's crate. Build 351 - The village forges. The forges on Saltmere, in Kingsferry and in Windle now have an ore bin your runners and rooks can fill, a strongbox that opens your bank, bellows that speed up every job, and a quench trough that sometimes turns out a Fine piece. - Two new smiths: Aggie Brand in Kingsferry and Morwen Hollis in Windle. Brannoc has tips. - The forge's list now has a tab for each metal. Build 346 - The Crown Valley. The Crownwall's gates can open at last, onto the grey valley around the Hexhold. It's the toughest place yet, for levels 65 to 90. - The Lantern camp inside the South Gate has a bank chest, a campfire, a waystone and a mender. - New trees, rocks and fish: duskwood , hexwood , sealstone , greyed slate , hexstone and hexcarp , with sealsteel and hexsteel gear to smith. The valley has its own music. - New monsters, and someone on the bridge who won't let you pass. - The Captain's Rest , a set of duels for players with total level 750, and a new map quest from Old Hob. - Crownwall Gate (Magic 68) teleports you inside any gate you've walked through. - Fixed where you wake up after being knocked out in Fairmeadow. Build 341 - Two more long quest lines , from Hesper Vane in Thornwick and Tamsy Glass in Emberfall. Like the last three, each is a puzzle across its province, and each ends with something that changes your Magic. - Fixed: a quest item handed to you with a full bag now goes to your bank instead of being lost. Build 336 - A friendlier Saltmere. The island's mentors remember you and how far you got, your answers have more character, and Hattie tells you which jobs are left. - Brannoc keeps his forge open at any hour while you're learning. - Your first drowned oarsman always drops a Waters tile, and Tamsin's magic lesson is clearer. - Something new above the Salted Eel's hearth. Build 333 - Three new quest lines , from Old Hob in Kingsferry, Tobin Reed in Reedwick and Old Corra at the Sixstones. Each is a puzzle that crosses its province, and the end of each one changes your Magic. Build 330 - Post rooks. Ida on Saltmere has a new quest, Ida's Rumour Mill. Finish it and a rook will fly one stack to your bank for a fish, from almost anywhere outdoors. - A spell you may know can now send a whole field chest home. - New sounds for the King's Post and the rooks. - Fixed a rare freeze when starting a conversation. Build 327 - The King's Post. A new quest in Kingsferry, The Last Toll, puts a post horn on your belt. Blow it and a runner carries your resources to the bank for a small fee. - Ruined post houses wait in the other provinces, and Roadwarden post boxes take deposits straight to your bank. - New things to make: iron nails at the forge and signboards at the workbench. - Small fixes to the story card. Build 323 - Every conversation now uses the story card , with the speaker's portrait, name and role. Long speeches come a few lines at a time, and passing remarks, rumours and mentor tips wait for your tap. - Anything said while another screen is open waits its turn. Build 320 - The Delvings now sound like a mine: dripping caves, torches, chains, the lift and the carts, and more. Build 318 - The Delvings, all the way down. The rest of the mine is open, to the very bottom. Bring plenty of food. - A new big fish bites off Saltmere at night, for very experienced anglers. Build 314 - The Delvings. Gunna Delve has work for you at the mine gate in Frostpeak. It's the start of a new quest line, with the first part of the mine to explore. Bring a light. Build 309 - Pin your skills. Keep up to 3 skills on the left of the screen and watch them fill as you train. - Monsters now carry their weapons. Archers shoot and mages cast from staffs, and every monster has a small chance to drop a weapon. - You put your weapon away to cast, each kind of spell has its own move, and your character eats with their hands free. - Misses now show a small \"miss\"."},{"id":"october-15-2026","text":"Build 295 - Fishing by water. Fish now live by the kind of water: sea, rivers, ponds, marsh pools, ice and more. Some bite only by day, some only at night. Every region has a local fishing expert to ask, with three new faces in Highmoor, Greenwood and Frostpeak. - A spot tells you when your level is too low or it's the wrong hour. - Pike can be caught now. - Fixed: some indoor furniture could be fished or gathered from. Build 290 - You now kneel at a Guard's Rest to lay bones down. - In combat, a hit that lands always does at least 1 damage, and misses show a \"miss\" puff instead of a 0. Build 285 - Fish broth now takes only small fish, from shrimp up to trout. - Fixed: inspecting a spot near the mine cart could send you on a cart ride the next time you walked. Build 281 - Better fish are worth more. Each step up the fishing ladder now heals more and sells for more, and item cards show how much food heals. See Healing. Build 277 - More sound. Brewing, every spell, teleports, doors, coins, the Journal and more now have their own sounds, and a chime plays the first time you reach a new place. Turn them up or down under Sound effects in Settings. Build 272 - Story cards. Quest starts, new steps and finished quests now open a big card with the speaker's portrait. The world waits while you read. - Portraits in the Journal and on the pinned quest line. - Brewing is easier to follow: you're told how many sips went to your cellar, empty flasks fill themselves, and Hattie explains the cellar after your first brew. - Your weapon is put away while you work, and brewing, crafting and digging have new animations. - Refreshed rooms, and the northern villagers have their own looks. Build 266 - Shape draughts. Three new draughts change what you are for a short while. Tap the badge to end one early. - Brewing 99 now has its reward, and it's a big one. - The Old Mill in Fairmeadow sells flour. Build 262 - Brewing comes north. Five new herbs (Brewing 20 to 60), a brewing station in every northern province, and eleven new draughts, including a few wards. - The Hollow, the Kiln and Frostpeak's night snow now wear you down while you're out in them. The right draught keeps them off. - Brewing perks at 30, 40, 65 and 70. - Magic tiles dropped by monsters now lie flat in the pile, and a message says when they go to your tile satchel. Build 257 - Frostpeak is open , the last of the six provinces. Take the Cold Road from Greenwood or Cinderreach, or ride the mine cart from Emberfall by day, to the snowbound Delvers' town of Kettlepass and its hot spring. - Frostfin to catch through the ice (Fishing 40), a Stone statue for Etching, its own map quest and Guard's Rest, and the toughest monsters yet, for levels 35 to 65. - Cinderreach's ground is now a sandy gold. Build 252 - Cinderreach is open. Take the Kingsway north from Highmoor to the red badlands under the Kiln and the smithing town of Emberfall, with its great furnace, its own map quest, a Guard's Rest and a Flame statue for Etching. - Gold to mine and smelt (Mining and Smithing 45), and tougher monsters for levels 25 to 55. Build 248 - Greenwood is open. Take the Kingsway north from Mistmarsh to an old forest and the foresters' town of Thornwick, with its own map quest, two Guard's Rests and a Roots statue for Etching. - Elderwood trees (Woodcutting 60), a salmon run, and tougher monsters for levels 20 to 45. - Opening the region map from inside a building now shows the town around you. Build 243 - Step inside. Ten buildings now open into rooms, including the Salted Eel, several forges and banks, the Wayside, the Leaky Eel, the Windy Ewe and Mother Gloam's hut. Tap one and choose Go into… . - Some familiar faces now live and work indoors, and the Salted Eel's hearth is where Saltmere cooks. Build 237 - Controls hints are now separate Tip: lines instead of being spoken by people, and quest tips stay in the Journal under that step. - A Bird's-Eye View in Highmoor has a new step. Build 233 - Twenty of the best-known villagers now each have their own look, and most carry something that suits their trade. Build 230 - Taps are more forgiving: tap anywhere on a person's, monster's or item pile's hex to talk, attack or pick up. - New look for the compass, health heart and day dial, plus wooden signposts you can tap to read. - × buttons to close Magic, the Journal and the long-press card. Level-up banners now sit above everything else. Build 226 - Brewing in Mistmarsh. Mother Gloam's cauldron, a new herb, two new draughts, and a new quest from Tobin in Reedwick. See brewing. - Marsh fever. Some marsh creatures can make you ill. There's a cure, and Gloam sells a tonic. - Cooking fish sometimes leaves fish scales. - When someone offers you a quest, you can now accept or turn it down in your own words. Lots of quest conversations have been rewritten. Build 219 - Every kind of fish now has its own look, raw, cooked and burnt. Build 216 - Brewing. A new skill: forage herbs, brew draughts, and drink them from flasks on your belt for a boost. Hattie on Saltmere has a quest to get you started. See brewing. - The bank has a Cellar tab for your draughts, and Fairmeadow has its own apothecary. - Etching at a Warden statue now shows your chisel at work. Build 208 - People now say goodbye in their own words, and some of your replies read better. - Saying Not now to a save import moves the file to a Declined folder instead of asking again. See settings-and-saving. Build 200 - Exported saves are now sealed, so a file edited outside the game is refused. Saves exported before this build need exporting again. Build 196 - Export now opens the share sheet, so you can AirDrop or message a save straight away. - Opening a .hexhold file in HexSeal asks to import it, even mid-game. It never offers to replace the save you're playing. Build 190 - Export and import saves. Copy a save to a .hexhold file and open it on another iPhone or iPad. See settings-and-saving. - The Muscle Memory quest now ends with a proper send-off from your mentor. - Villagers step aside instead of standing on your hero. Build 185 - Save slots. Keep up to five saves, and play, rename or delete them from Saves on the title screen. Your existing save moved into slot 1. See settings-and-saving. - The While you were away card shows your hero with their helmet on. Build 179 - The Journal is easier to read on phones, and its quest list opens at the top. Build 175 - The Journal. A new dock tab for your quests, the rumours you've overheard, and your maps. Pin a quest to keep its next step on screen. See quests. Build 170 - The Muscle Memory quest is easier to find. Until it's done, the hands chip shows what to do next, and the mentor is marked on your region map. - Double-tap-and-hold to zoom no longer sends your hero walking to where you tapped. Build 161 - A Muscle Memory quest. When a skill first reaches its Muscle Memory level, that skill's mentor sends for you, and finishing the quest switches Muscle Memory on for every skill. See muscle-memory. - Muscle Memory no longer runs when you leave mid-fight or with something hostile after you. - One-handed zoom. Tap, then tap again and hold, and slide down or up. See controls. - The While you were away card shows your own hero, and a dark hands chip explains why."},{"id":"october-14-2026","text":"Build 155 - Muscle Memory. Once a skill is high enough, your character keeps working at it while the app is closed, at a slower pace. A While you were away card shows what you made, and a hands chip shows when leaving would count. See muscle-memory. Build 150 - Magic help. A ? on the Magic tab explains the rosette, the satchel and casting. - When a Magic level opens a spell you haven't found, the level-up banner says so, and the Skills tab marks spells as can now be discovered or learned . - Character level-ups get their own banner and jingle. - New art for the Trade skill, your market stall and planks. Build 146 - The Grand Market in Kingsferry: fund three keeper stalls, then rent your own stall . - The Trade skill. Set prices, let shoppers buy while you're elsewhere, collect from the till. See trade. - Planks , sawn from oak logs at a workbench. Build 142 - Mags the Pedlar , in a different town each day, with rare curios. - Riddle maps : buy or find a worn map and follow its riddles to a chest. See riddle-maps. Build 136 - The Gullwatch fort is rebuilt , out on its own islet. - Bigger Warden statues in every region. Build 130 - Saltmere's Bone Guard now keep to themselves by day. - Laying the Guard to rest has more to discover. See bones. Build 126 - Town works : fund repairs around Saltmere, Kingsferry and Reedwick for lasting perks, and earn the title Patron of Saltmere . See town-works. Build 120 - Tutorial polish on Saltmere: more help from the islanders and clearer pointers. Build 116 - Local prices : each town wants different things, and prices drop if you flood a market. - Notice boards with daily orders. - The Driftwood Cottage , your home on Saltmere. See cottage. Build 112 - A new tutorial, Washed Ashore. Five islanders fill your tool belt, in any order, and you can skip any lesson or all of them. See first-steps. Build 108 - Bones and the Guard's Rest , the Guard's Blessing, bone ash and fish broth. See bones. Builds 100 and 104 - More magic: utility, healing, teleport and gathering spells, tile shops, tiles from monsters and lucky finds. - Mage gear : staffs, robes, capes, tomes, chisels and the etcher's set. - Magic folk around the provinces who share hints. Build 96 - A new stone hearth at every forge. Build 92 - A new head and face kit for the character creator, including an eyepatch."},{"id":"october-13-2026","text":"Build 87 - Magic and Etching. The tile satchel, the rosette, the spellbook and combat spells; make your own tiles at Warden statues. See magic and etching. Build 83 - Death and the mending house : fees, a grave that never expires, the lost-and-found, and an optional Hard mode. See death. Builds 70 to 79 - Maps : the compass, the map sheet, and a map quest in each region. See maps. - The Pack tab with a 3D paper doll, skill badges, and better hair under helmets. Build 68 - The new menu : the Pack, Skills and Magic dock, a new title screen and fonts, and the admire camera. Build 57 - Sacks, field chests and bundles for carrying more. See hauling. Build 51 - Mistmarsh and Highmoor , reached along the Kingsway. Build 43 - Day and night . See day-and-night. Builds 30 to 38 - Saltmere rebuilt and filled with islanders, gold-roofed banks, sound effects, bones, and the full ladder of metals, woods and fish."},{"id":"october-12-2026","text":"Build 29 - Character creator and title screen. Builds 24 to 28 - Fairmeadow and Kingsferry , the ferry, and the world map. - The tool belt . Build 18 - Combat : Attack, Strength, Defence and Hitpoints, monsters, food and drops. Builds 9 to 17 - Cooking, Crafting, Mining and Smithing , equipment, and the Skills screen. - Item icons, music, coins and selling, Settings, and the long-press What is this? card. Build 4 - Woodcutting and fishing , the 28-slot bag, the bank, and autosave. Build 1 - The first walkable hex island. Tap to walk, drag and pinch the camera."}]}];
